hadiths[600]{hadithnumber,text}:
"20401","Abu Umayyah al-Lakhmi. 908 - Muhammad ibn Hatim al-Marwazi narrated to us, Hibban ibn Musa narrated to us, and Suwayd ibn Nasr, saying: Abd Allah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Uqbah narrated to us, Bakr ibn Sawadah narrated to me, from Abu Umayyah al-Lakhmi: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Among the signs of the Hour are three, one of them: that knowledge is sought from the young (the lesser, unqualified)."
"20402","Those called Abu Umaymah. Abu Umaymah. 909 - Bakr ibn Sahl narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Salih narrated to me, that Issam ibn Yahya narrated to him, from Abu Qilabah, from Abd Allah ibn Ziyad, from Abu Umaymah who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to take lunch while traveling, and I was near him sitting. He said: Come to lunch. I said: I am fasting. He said: Allah has lifted from the traveler half of the prayer and fasting."
"20403","Those called Abu Sulalah. Abu Sulalah al-Sulami, also said Abu Sallam. 910 - al-Hasan ibn al-Abbas al-Razi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Humayd narrated to us, Hakkam ibn Salm narrated to us, from Anbasah, from Asim ibn Ubayd Allah, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah, from Abu Sulalah al-Sulami, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There will be rulers over you who control your provisions. They will narrate to you and lie to you, and they will act and act badly. They will not be pleased with you until you deem their evil good and confirm their lie. So give them the right as long as they are pleased with it; but when they transgress, whoever is killed upon that is a martyr."
"20404","Those called Abu al-Haytham. Abu al-Haytham, not traced to a lineage. 911 - Ward ibn Ahmad ibn Labid al-Bayruti narrated to us, Safwan ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, from Ibn Lahiah, from Bakr ibn Sawadah who said: Abu al-Haytham narrated to me, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw me performing ablution and said: The sole of the foot, O Abu al-Haytham."
"20405","Those called Abu Ghadiyah. Abu Ghadiyah al-Juhani. 912 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz and Abu Muslim al-Kashi narrated to us, saying: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Rabiah ibn Kulthum narrated to us, my father narrated to us, saying: I was at Wasit al-Qasab with Abd al-Ala ibn Abd Allah ibn Amir, and the doorkeeper said: This is Abu Ghadiyah al-Juhani. Abd al-Ala said: Admit him. He entered, wearing cut garments (?), a tall man, lean among men, as if he is not of this Ummah. When he sat down he said: I pledged to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). I said: With your right hand? He said: Yes. He addressed us on the day of al-Aqabah, saying: O people, your blood and your wealth are inviolable to you like the inviolability of this day of yours, in this month of yours, in this land of yours. Have I conveyed? They said: Yes. He said: O Allah, bear witness. He said: Do not return after me as disbelievers, striking the necks of one another. He said: And we used to count Ammar ibn Yasir among our best. He said: When the day of Siffin came, he advanced walking at the front of the squadron on foot, until when he was between the two ranks, a man stabbed him in the knee with a spear, so he stumbled and the helmet fell off him, and I struck him, and behold it was the head of Ammar. He said: A freedman of ours says: What hand sufficed (to kill) him? He said: I have not seen a man more clearly astray in my view than him, that he heard from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what he heard, then killed Ammar."
"20406","913 - Ahmad ibn Dawud al-Makki narrated to us, Yahya ibn Umar al-Laythi narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Kulthum ibn Jabr narrated to us, saying: I heard my father say: We were with Anbasah ibn Said, and one day I rode to al-Hajjaj, and a man called Abu Ghadiyah al-Juhani came to him. Abd al-Ala ibn Abd Allah said to him: Stand to him and bring him down, saying: Now return. So we went out to him and said to him: Now return. He dismounted and Abd al-Ala entered and asked for drink, and water was brought in a glass cup, but he refused to drink from glass. Then it was brought in a cup of crystal (?) and he drank. He said: I pledged to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while I was returning wealth to my family. Rashid ibn Abyad, who was with me at Abd al-Ala's, said: With this right hand of yours? Abd al-Ala rebuked him and said: Divide for him (?). He said: And I witnessed his sermon on the day of al-Aqabah, saying: Your blood and your wealth are inviolable to you like the inviolability of this day of yours, in this month of yours, in this land of yours. Do not return after me as disbelievers, striking the necks of one another. Until when the day came when Uthman was besieged, I heard a man saying: Do not kill this one. I looked at him, and behold it was Ammar. Had it not been for those Companions behind him, I would have struck his belly. I said: O Allah, if You will, let him meet me. When the day of Siffin came, behold a wicked man leading a squadron on foot. I looked at the armor, and it slipped from his knee, so I stabbed him, and behold it was Ammar."
"20407","Abu Ghadiyah al-Muzani. 914 - Abu Zurah al-Dimashqi, Abu Abd al-Malik al-Qurashi and Jafar ibn Muhammad al-Firyabi narrated to us, saying: Muhammad ibn Aidh narrated to us, al-Haytham ibn Humayd narrated to us, saying: Hafs ibn Ghaylan Abu Mabad narrated to us, from Hayyan ibn Hujr, from Abu Ghadiyah al-Muzani: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There will be severe trials after me; the best of people in them are the Muslims of the desert dwellers who do not stain themselves with the blood or wealth of people at all."
"20408","Those called Abu al-Jad. Abu al-Jad al-Damri, he is Abu al-Jad ibn Junadah ibn al-Mirdad ibn Abd ibn Kab ibn Damrah ibn Bakr ibn Abd Manah ibn Kinanah ibn Khuzaymah ibn Mudrikah ibn Ilyas ibn Mudar ibn Nizar ibn Mad ibn Adnan. 915 - Idris ibn Jafar al-Attar narrated to us, Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Amr informed us, saying: I heard Ubaydah ibn Sufyan say: I heard Abu al-Jad al-Damri say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever abandons three consecutive Fridays out of negligence, Allah seals his heart."
"20409","916 - Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr, from Ubaydah ibn Sufyan, from Abu al-Jad al-Damri, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Whoever abandons Friday thrice out of negligence, Allah seals his heart."
"20410","917 - Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zaidah narrated to us; (ha) and Uthman ibn Umar al-Dabbi narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Raja narrated to us, Zaidah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Amr al-Laythi narrated to us, Ubaydah ibn Sufyan al-Hadrami narrated to us, from Abu al-Jad al-Damri, and he had companionship with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever abandons Friday three times out of negligence, Allah seals his heart."
"20411","918 - Jafar ibn Sulayman al-Nawfali al-Madani narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Fulayh narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr, from Ubaydah ibn Sufyan, from Abu al-Jad al-Damri who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever abandons Friday three times out of negligence, Allah seals his heart."
"20412","919 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Said ibn Amr al-Ashathi narrated to us, Abthar ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr, from Abdah ibn Sufyan, from Abu al-Jad al-Damri who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Saddles are not tied except to the Sacred Mosque, and this mosque of mine, and al-Aqsa Mosque."
"20413","Those called Abu Nujayh. Abu Nujayh, not traced to a lineage, and he is not Amr ibn Abasah. 920 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ibn Jurayj who said: Abu al-Mughallis informed me: that Abu Nujayh informed him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever is well-off enough to marry and then does not marry is not of me."
"20414","Those called Abu Sallam. Abu Sallam, servant of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 921 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Bishr narrated to us, Misar narrated to us, Abu Aqil narrated to us, from Sabiq, from Abu Sallam, servant of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: There is no servant, or human, or Muslim who says when evening comes and when morning comes: I am pleased with Allah as Lord, and with Islam as religion, and with Muhammad as Prophet, except that it is due upon Allah to please him on the Day of Resurrection."
"20415","Those called Abu Khayrah. Abu Khayrah al-Sabbahi. 922 - Ahmad ibn al-Husayn ibn Mabahram narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Muhammad ibn Marzuq narrated to us, saying: The daughter of the daughter of Abd al-Rahman ibn Hisham ibn Hassan narrated to me, saying: My uncle Abd Allah ibn Hisham ibn Hassan ibn Abd Allah narrated to me, from Yazid ibn Abi Khayrah, saying: My father Khayrah narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Khayrah who said: I had camels upon which I carried loads. I came to Madinah and witnessed Khaybar with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), or he said: Hunayn. We used to carry water for him upon our camels. I had trade in Madinah, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) supplicated for blessing and supplicated for my children."
"20416","923 - Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Amr ibn Muhammad ibn Ararah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Humran ibn Abd al-Aziz al-Qaysi narrated to us, Dawud ibn al-Musawir narrated to us, Muqatil ibn Hammam narrated to us, from Abu Khayrah al-Sabbahi who said: I was among the delegation who came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and we were forty men. He forbade us from (using) dubba, hantam, naqir and muzaffat (vessels for nabidh). He said: Then he ordered arak (tooth-sticks) for us, saying: Clean your teeth with this. We said: O Messenger of Allah, we have herbs and we suffice ourselves with them. He raised his hands and said: O Allah, forgive Abd al-Qays, as they embraced Islam obediently, not unwillingly."
"20417","924 - Ibrahim ibn Nailah al-Asbahani narrated to us, saying: Shabab al-Usfuri narrated to us, Awn ibn Kahmas narrated to us, Dawud ibn al-Musawir narrated to us, from Muqatil ibn Hammam, from Abu Khayrah al-Sabbahi who said: I was among the delegation who came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from Abd al-Qays, and he provided us arak to clean our teeth with. We said: O Messenger of Allah, we have palm stalks, but we accept your honor and your gift. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Allah, forgive Abd al-Qays, as they embraced Islam obediently, not coerced, when my people sat back and embraced Islam only humiliated and wronged (?)."
"20418","Those called Abu Arwa. Abu Arwa al-Dawsi, also said his name is Rabiah, also said Ubayd ibn al-Harith. 925 - Abu Khalifah al-Fadl ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Abu Waqid al-Laythi, from Abu Arwa who said: I used to pray Asr with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then come to Dhu al-Hulayfah walking, reaching it while the sun had not set."
"20419","926 - Muhammad ibn Ali al-Saigh narrated to us, Bishr ibn Ubays ibn Marhum al-Attar narrated to us, al-Nadr ibn Arabi narrated to us, from Asim ibn Umar, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Arwa al-Dawsi who said: I was sitting with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when Abu Bakr and Umar appeared. He said: Praise be to Allah who has supported me with them."
"20420","Those called Abu al-Khattab. Abu al-Khattab. 927 - Muhammad ibn Abdus ibn Kamil al-Sarraj narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn al-Shair narrated to us, Abu Ahmad al-Zubayri narrated to us, Israil narrated to us, from Thuwayr ibn Abi Fakhitah, from a man among the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called Abu al-Khattab, that he asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about witr. He said: I love to pray witr in the middle of the night; Allah Almighty descends from the highest heaven to the lowest heaven and says: Is there any asker? Is there any seeker of forgiveness? Is there any supplicant? Until when dawn rises, He ascends."
"20421","Those called Abu Shaqrah. Abu Shaqrah. 928 - Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn Mukram narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn al-Mustamirr al-Uruqi narrated to us, Amr ibn Asim narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd al-Minqari narrated to us, Makhlad ibn Uqbah ibn Shurahbil narrated to us, from Abu Shaqrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you see those (women) who cast upon their heads like the humps of cows, inform them that no prayer will be accepted from them."
"20422","Those called Abu Uthman. Abu Uthman al-Ansari. 929 - Allan ibn Abd al-Samad al-Tayalisi Maghammah narrated to us, saying: Umar ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hasan narrated to us, my father narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Uthman al-Ansari who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) knocked on my door, while I had been with my wife, and I disliked going out to him until I bathe, so I was late for him. I caught up with him and informed him. He said to me: Had you emitted? I said: No. He said: Then upon you was but ablution."
"20423","Those called Abu al-Uryan. Abu al-Uryan al-Sulami. 930 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Abu Khaldah narrated to us, saying: I asked Ibn Sirin, saying: I pray and do not know whether I prayed two rakahs or four. He said: Abu al-Uryan narrated to me: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed one day and entered the house. Among the people was a man with long arms, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to call him Dhu al-Yadayn. Dhu al-Yadayn said: O Messenger of Allah, has the prayer been shortened or did you forget? He said: It was neither shortened nor did I forget. He said: Rather you forgot the prayer. He said: So he advanced and prayed two rakahs with them, then greeted, then said takbir and prostrated like his prostration or longer, then said takbir and raised his head, then said takbir and prostrated like his prostration or longer, then said takbir and raised his head. And Muhammad did not remember whether he greeted after that or not."
"20424","Those called Abu Zayd. Abu Zayd al-Jarmi. 931 - Muhammad ibn al-Abbas al-Muaddib narrated to us, Ubayd ibn Ishaq al-Attar narrated to us, Miskin ibn Dinar narrated to us, from Mujahid who said: I heard Abu Zayd al-Jarmi say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The disobedient to parents shall not enter Paradise, nor the reminder of favors, nor the addict to wine."
"20425","Those called Abu Shahm. Abu Shahm, not traced to a lineage. 932 - Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us, al-Ala ibn Abd al-Jabbar al-Attar narrated to us; (ha) and Ismail ibn al-Hasan al-Khaffaf al-Misri narrated to us, Zuhayr ibn Abbad narrated to us; (ha) and Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami and Mahmud ibn Muhammad al-Wasiti narrated to us, saying: Muhammad ibn Aban al-Wasiti narrated to us, saying: Yazid ibn Ata narrated to us, from Bayan ibn Bishr, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, from Abu Shahm, and he was an idle man, who said: I saw a slave girl walking on one of the roads of Madinah, leaning, and I reached with my hand to her side. The next day I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while people were pledging to him. I stretched my hand, saying: Pledge me, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Are you the fellow of the little girl yesterday? Indeed you are the fellow of the little girl yesterday. I said: O Messenger of Allah, pledge me, for by Allah I shall never return. He said: Then yes, in that case."
"20426","933 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam and Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, saying: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, saying: Aswad ibn Amir narrated to us, Huraym ibn Sufyan narrated to us, from Bayan, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, from Abu Shahm who said: I was in Madinah and a woman passed by me, so I seized her side, while I heard the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pledging people. I came to him and he did not pledge me, saying: The fellow of the little girl yesterday. I said: By Allah I shall never return, O Messenger of Allah. So he pledged me."
"20427","Those called Abu Sallam. Abu Sallam al-Sulami. 934 - Abd Allah ibn Najiyah narrated to us, Yusuf ibn Musa al-Qattan narrated to us, Hakkam ibn Salm narrated to us, saying: Anbasah ibn Said al-Asadi narrated to us, from Asim ibn Ubayd Allah, from Abd Allah ibn Ubayd Allah, from Abu Sallam al-Sulami who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There will be rulers over you who own your necks. They will narrate to you and lie to you, and they will act and act badly. They will not be pleased with you until you deem their evil good and confirm their lie. So give them the right as long as they are pleased with it."
"20428","Those called Abu Layla. Abu Layla al-Ashari. 935 - Abu Amir Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Nahwi al-Suri narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Ramadi narrated to us, Abu Amr al-Ansi narrated to us, from Sulayman ibn Habib al-Muharibi, from Amr ibn Ludayn al-Ashari, from Abu Layla al-Ashari, Companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Hold to obedience to your rulers and do not oppose them, for obedience to them is obedience to Allah, and disobedience to them is disobedience to Allah. Allah only sent me to call to His path with wisdom and good exhortation. Whoever succeeds me in that is my ally, and whoever is put in charge of any of your affairs and acts otherwise, upon him is the curse of Allah and the angels and all people. Rulers will rule you who, if asked for mercy, show no mercy; if asked for rights, do not give; if commanded to good, deny it. You will keep aloof from them and your multitude will disperse, until they impose nothing upon you except that you bear it willingly or unwillingly. The least right for you is that you take no stipend from them and attend no gathering for them."
"20429","936 - al-Hasan ibn Ali al-Mamari narrated to us, Ayyub ibn Muhammad al-Wazzan al-Raqqi narrated to us, Harun ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi Qays narrated to us, from Sulayman ibn Habib al-Muharibi, from Amir ibn Ludayn, from Abu Layla al-Ashari who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Hold to obedience to your rulers, do not oppose them, for obedience to them is obedience to Allah, and disobedience to them is disobedience to Allah. Allah sent me to call to His path with wisdom and good exhortation. Whoever succeeds me in that is of me and I am of him."
"20430","Those called Abu Jabir. Abu Jabir al-Sadafi. 937 - Abu Amir al-Nahwi narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Dimashqi narrated to us, Husayn ibn Ali al-Kindi, freedman of Jarir, narrated to us, from al-Awzai, from Qays ibn Jabir al-Sadafi, from his father, from his grandfather: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There will be caliphs after me, and after the caliphs commanders, and after the commanders kings, and after the kings tyrants. Then a man from my household will emerge, filling the earth with justice as it was filled with injustice. Then the Qahtani will be made ruler. By Him who sent me with the truth, he is no less than him."
"20431","Those called Abu al-Ala. Abu al-Ala al-Ansari. 938 - Ahmad ibn Amr al-Khallal al-Makki narrated to us, Yaqub ibn Humayd narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Umar al-Waqidi narrated to us, Ayyub ibn al-Ala al-Ansari narrated to us, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: I saw upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Uhud two coats of mail."
"20432","Those called Abu Junaydah. Abu Junaydah al-Fihri. 939 - Ahmad ibn Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najdah al-Hawti narrated to us, Ali ibn Ayyash al-Himsi narrated to us, Abu Ghassan Muhammad ibn Mutarrif narrated to us, from Ishaq ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Farwah, from Abu Junaydah al-Fihri, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever gives drink to a thirsty one and quenches him, a door of Paradise is opened for him and it is said to him: Enter through it. And whoever feeds a hungry one and satiates him, and gives drink to a thirsty one and quenches him, all the doors of Paradise are opened for him and it is said to him: Enter through whichever you wish."
"20433","Abu Abi Misma al-Hajabi. 940 - Muhammad ibn Salih ibn al-Walid al-Narsi narrated to us, Abu Hafs Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, Umar ibn Ali al-Muqaddami narrated to us, al-Ala ibn al-Akhdar al-Ijli al-Ram narrated to us, a shaykh of the Hijabah called Misma narrated to me, and he saw me praying behind the middle pillar of the House (Kabah), saying: My father narrated to me, from my grandfather, that he saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pray behind it two rakahs."
"20434","One named Abu Raitah. Abu Raitah ibn Karamah al-Madhhiji. 941 - Abu Amir al-Nahwi narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Ahmad al-Yahsabi narrated to us, Ali ibn Abi Ali narrated to us, from Amir al-Shabi, from Abu Raitah ibn Karamah al-Madhhiji who said: We were with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said to traveling people: Let no stray of these camels accompany you, meaning strays, and let none of you accompany a stray, nor turn away a beggar, if you desire profit and safety. And let none of the people accompany you, if you believe in Allah and the Last Day: neither a sorcerer nor a sorceress, nor a soothsayer nor a soothsayeress, nor an astrologer nor an astrologeress, nor a poet nor a poetess. Every punishment with which Allah wills to punish any of His servants, He only sends it down to the lowest heaven. So I forbid you from disobedience to Allah in the evening (?)."
"20435","Those called Abu al-Hajjaj. Abu al-Hajjaj al-Thumali. 942 - Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Irq al-Himsi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Musaffa narrated to us, Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid narrated to us, from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Maryam, from al-Haytham ibn Malik, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Aidh al-Azdi, from Abu al-Hajjaj al-Thumali who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The grave says to the dead when he is placed in it: Woe to you, son of Adam, what deceived you of me? Did you not know that I am the house of trial, the house of darkness, the house of solitude, and the house of worms? What deceived you of me when you used to pass by me strutting? If he was righteous, the answerer of the grave answers on his behalf, saying: Do you see, if he was of those who command good and forbid evil? The grave says: Then I return to him green, and his body returns as light, and his soul ascends to the Lord of the worlds. A man said: O Abu al-Hajjaj, what is al-faddad? He said: One who steps forward with one foot and back with the other, like your walk, O son of my brother, sometimes; and on that day he dresses and adorns himself."
"20436","Those called Abu Ubayd. Abu Ubayd, freedman of Rifah ibn Rafi. 943 - Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz ibn Miqlas narrated to us, my father narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Ayyash ibn Abbas informed us, from Abd Allah ibn al-Aswad, from Abu Miqal, from Abu Ubayd, freedman of Rifah ibn Rafi: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Cursed is he who asks by the Face of Allah, and cursed is he who is asked by the Face of Allah and refuses his asker."
"20437","Those called Abu Nuhaylah. Abu Nuhaylah. 944 - Ibrahim ibn Hashim al-Baghawi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr al-Muqaddami narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abu Wail, from Abu Nuhaylah, of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he was struck by an arrow and it was said to him: Pull it out. He said: O Allah, lessen the pain but do not lessen the reward. It was said to him: Supplicate. He said: O Allah, make me of those brought near, and make my mother of the houris."
"20438","Those called Abu Atiyah. Abu Atiyah. 945 - Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Irq al-Himsi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Musaffa narrated to us, Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid narrated to us, Bahir ibn Sad narrated to us, from Khalid ibn Madan who said: Abu Atiyah said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sat and it was related that a man had died. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Has any of you seen him upon any deed of good? A man said: Yes, I kept guard with him one night in the path of Allah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood with those with him. When he was entered into the grave, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) threw soil with his hand, then said: Your companions think you are of the people of the Fire, while I bear witness that you are of the people of Paradise. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Umar ibn al-Khattab: Do not ask about people's deeds, but ask about the fitrah."
"20439","The brother of Khuzaymah ibn Thabit, who has companionship. 946 - Abu Zurah Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr al-Dimashqi narrated to us, Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, Shuayb informed us, from al-Zuhri: Umarah ibn Khuzaymah narrated to me, that his uncle narrated to him, and he is of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bought a horse from a Bedouin and went ahead of him to give him the price of his horse. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) hastened in walking while the Bedouin lagged. Men began intercepting the Bedouin and bargaining with him for the horse, not knowing that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had bought it, until some of them raised the Bedouin in bidding above the price of the horse which the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had bought. The Bedouin called to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: If you are buying this horse, buy it, otherwise I shall sell it. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said when he heard the Bedouin's call: Have I not already bought it? He said: No, by Allah, we did not sell to you. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes indeed, I have bought it. People began gathering around the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the Bedouin while the two disputed. The Bedouin kept saying: Bring a witness to witness that I sold to you. Whoever came of the Muslims said to the Bedouin: Woe to you, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would say nothing but truth. Until Khuzaymah came and listened to the dispute of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the dispute of the Bedouin. The Bedouin kept saying: Bring a witness that I sold to you. Khuzaymah said: I bear witness that you have sold it. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned to Khuzaymah and said: On what basis do you testify? He said: By my belief in you, O Messenger of Allah. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made the testimony of Khuzaymah equal to the testimony of two men."
"20440","Abu Abi al-Khurayf. 947 - al-Husayn ibn al-Sumayda al-Antaki narrated to us, Musa ibn Ayyub al-Nasibi narrated to us, saying: Abd al-Aziz ibn al-Zubayr narrated to us, from Umar ibn Qays, from Sasah ibn al-Suwway, from Ibn Abi al-Khurayf, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: I came with my brother to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was in the Mosque of al-Khayf praying, though we had prayed the obligatory prayer at home and did not pray with them. He said: What prevented you from praying with us? We said: We had prayed the obligatory prayer at home. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When a man prays the obligatory prayer at home, then reaches a congregation, let him pray with them, and his prayer at home will be voluntary."
"20441","Those called Abu Hilal. Abu Hilal al-Taymi. 948 - Ahmad ibn al-Mualla al-Dimashqi and Ward ibn Ahmad ibn Labid narrated to us, saying: Safwan ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, one who heard Alqamah ibn Hilal, of Banu Taym Allah, narrated to me, narrating from his father, from his grandfather, that he came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a man of his people while he was in Madinah after his migration to it. We found him striking the necks of captives at a small water, and killing continued over it until blood covered the water. Safwan said: Safaha, meaning it covered the water."
"20442","Those called Abu Wahb. Abu Wahb al-Jushami. 949 - Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Hisham ibn Sad narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Muhajir narrated to us, Aqil ibn Shabib narrated to me, from Abu Wahb al-Jushami, and he had companionship, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Name yourselves with the names of the Prophets. The most beloved names to Allah are Abd Allah and Abd al-Rahman, the truest of them are Harith and Hammam, and the ugliest of them are Harb and Murrah. Tie horses, wipe their forelocks and rumps, or he said: their cruppers, and collar them but do not collar them with bowstrings. Upon you is every dark bay with white forehead and legs, or bright chestnut with white forehead and legs, or black with white forehead and legs."
"20443","Those called Abu Sud. Abu Sud al-Tamimi. 950 - Muhammad ibn Hatim al-Marwazi narrated to us, Suwayd ibn Nasr and Hibban ibn Musa narrated to us, saying: Abd Allah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Mamar, saying: A shaykh of Banu Tamim narrated to me, from Abu Sud who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The oath by which a man severs his brother's wealth will cut off kinship."
"20444","Those called Abu Amr. Abu Amr al-Ansari, a Badri. 951 - Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Ubadah ibn Ziyad al-Asadi narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn Ubayd Allah al-Arzami narrated to us, Jafar ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from his father, from Muhammad ibn Talhah ibn Yazid ibn Rukanah, from Muhammad ibn al-Hanafiyyah who said: I saw Abu Amr al-Ansari, and he was a Badri, Aqabi, Uhudi, fasting, writhing from thirst, saying to a slave boy of his: Woe to you, shield me. The boy shielded him until he drew a bow weakly, shooting three arrows, then said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever shoots an arrow in the path of Allah, whether it falls short or reaches, it will be light for him on the Day of Resurrection. It was said he was killed at sunset."
"20445","Abu Amr, not traced to a lineage. 952 - Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz ibn Miqlas narrated to us, my father narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, from Umar ibn Suhban, from Zamil ibn Amr, from his father, from his grandfather: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out on the day of Fitr to the Eid, with Ubayy ibn Kab on his right and Umar, or he said: Ibn Umar, on his left. When he finished, he passed by the door of Abu Kathir, or Abu Kabir, with butchers in its courtyard, and the people freshly out of Jahiliyyah. He said to them: How do you sell? They said: Such-and-such. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Sell as you wish, but do not mix carrion with slaughtered meat for people. O people, be mindful: do not hoard, do not engage in najsh (false bidding), do not meet goods (before the market), let no townsman sell for a Bedouin, let no man sell upon his brother's sale, let no man propose upon his brother's proposal until he permits him, and let no woman ask for another woman's divorce to overturn her vessel and marry (her husband), for her provision is upon Allah Almighty."
"20446","Those known by the kunya Abu al-Ward. Abu al-Ward. 953 - Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Jubarah ibn Mughallis narrated, Ibn al-Mubarak narrated, from Humayd, from Ibn Abi al-Ward, from his father, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw me and saw that I was a ruddy man, so he said: You are Abu al-Ward."
"20447","Those known by the kunya Abu Tibah. Abu Tibah the cupper. 954 - Ahmad ibn Masud al-Maqdisi narrated to us, al-Haytham ibn Jamil narrated, Sharik narrated, from Layth, from Abd al-Warith, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: Abu Tibah passed by us in the month of Ramadan, so we said: Where have you come from? He said: I cupped the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"20448","Those known by the kunya Abu Fusaylah. Abu Fusaylah. 955 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Ziyad ibn al-Rabi Abu Khidash narrated, Abbad ibn Kathir al-Shami, from the people of Palestine, narrated, from a woman among them called Fusaylah, she said: I heard my father say: I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: O Messenger of Allah, is it partisanship for a man to love his people? He said: No, but partisanship is for a man to help his people in wrongdoing."
"20449","Those known by the kunya Abu Thalabah. Abu Thalabah al-Ashjai. 956 - Muhammad ibn al-Abbas al-Muaddib and Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, both saying: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated, Mindal ibn Ali narrated, from Ibn Jurayj, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Umar ibn Nabhan, from Abu Thalabah, he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, two sons of mine have died in Islam, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever has two sons die in Islam, Allah will admit him to Paradise by virtue of His mercy upon them."
"20450","957 - Muhammad ibn Muhammad ibn Uqbah al-Shaybani narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Ali al-Hulwani narrated, Hammad ibn Masadah narrated, from Ibn Jurayj, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Umar ibn Nabhan, from Abu Thalabah al-Ashjai, he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, two sons of mine have died in Islam, so he said: Whoever has two sons die in Islam, Allah will admit him to Paradise by virtue of His mercy upon them. Then afterwards he met Abu Hurayrah and said to him: Are you the one to whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) spoke about the two sons? He said: Yes. He said: If he had said it to me, it would be dearer to me than all that Hims and Palestine enclose."
"20451","Those known by the kunya Abu al-Samh. Abu al-Samh, servant of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 958 - Muhammad ibn Salih ibn al-Walid al-Narsi narrated to us, Abu Hafs Amr ibn Ali narrated, and al-Hasan ibn Harun ibn Sulayman al-Asbahani narrated to us, Mujahid ibn Musa narrated, both saying: Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated, Yahya ibn al-Walid narrated, Muhill ibn Khalifah narrated to me, Abu al-Samh narrated to me, he said: I used to serve the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and when he wanted to bathe he would say: Turn your back to me, so I would turn my back to him. Then Hasan or Husayn was brought and urinated on his chest, so I came to wash it, and he said: The urine of a girl is washed, and the urine of a boy is sprinkled with water."
"20452","Those known by the kunya Abu al-Mujabbir. Abu al-Mujabbir. 959 - Abu Husayn Muhammad ibn al-Husayn the judge narrated to us, Yahya ibn Abd al-Hamid al-Himmani narrated, Mubarak ibn Said, brother of Sufyan ibn Said al-Thawri, narrated, he said: Khulayd al-Thawri narrated, from Abu al-Mujabbir, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever supports two daughters, or two sisters, or two maternal aunts, or two paternal aunts, or two grandmothers, he will be with me in Paradise like these two, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) joined his index finger and the one next to it. If they are three, then he is relieved, and if they are four or five, then O servants of Allah, help him, lend to him, lend to him, deal with him, deal with him."
"20453","Those known by the kunya Abu Mabad. Abu Mabad al-Juhani, and it has been said that he is Abdullah ibn Ukaym. 960 - Abd al-Rahman ibn Salim Abu Yahya al-Razi narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn al-Zibriqan, that is al-Kufi, narrated, al-Muttalib ibn Ziyad narrated, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Isa, he said: We entered upon Abu Mabad al-Juhani visiting him when sick, and we said: Will you not hang something as a charm? He said: Death is nearer than that. I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever hangs something as a charm will be entrusted to it."
"20454","Those known by the kunya Abu Aqil. Abu Aqil al-Budayli. 961 - Bakr ibn Ahmad ibn Muqbil al-Basri narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Sabbah ibn al-Muammal narrated, Abdullah ibn Harb narrated, Hassan ibn Asad, from the clan of Kannaz ibn Hisn al-Badri, narrated to me, he said: Abu Harim Hashim ibn Ayyash al-Qurashi narrated, from Abu Aqil al-Budayli, he said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and believed in him and affirmed him, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave me a drink of sawiq to drink; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) drank the first of it and I drank the last of it, and I have continued to feel its moisture upon my heart when thirsty and its coolness when exposed to the midday sun."
"20455","Those known by the kunya Abu Thabit. Abu Thabit al-Ansari, grandfather of Adi ibn Thabit. 962 - Fudayl ibn Muhammad al-Malti narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, and al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Asfati narrated to us, Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated, and Mahmud ibn Muhammad al-Wasiti narrated to us, Zakariyya ibn Yahya, Zahmawayh, narrated, all saying: Sharik narrated, from Abu al-Yaqzan, from Adi ibn Thabit, from his father, from his grandfather, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: A woman with prolonged non-menstrual bleeding leaves prayer during the days of her menstrual periods, then she bathes and prays, then ablution for every prayer."
"20456","963 - Fudayl ibn Muhammad al-Malti narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, and Mahmud ibn Muhammad al-Wasiti narrated to us, Zakariyya ibn Yahya narrated, both saying: Sharik narrated, from Abu al-Yaqzan, from Adi ibn Thabit, from his father, from his grandfather, raising the hadith, he said: Sneezing, postnatal bleeding, nosebleed, menstruation, vomiting, and yawning in prayer are from Satan."
"20457","Those known by the kunya Abu Umar. Abu Umar al-Ansari. 964 - Ibrahim ibn Mattuyah al-Asbahani narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Said al-Jawhari narrated, Abd al-Samad ibn al-Numan narrated, Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-Abbas narrated, Adi ibn Thabit narrated to me, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Every good deed is charity."
"20458","965 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, Bashir ibn Salman narrated, from an Ansari elder, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever prays four rakahs before Zuhr, it is equal to freeing a slave from the descendants of Ismail."
"20459","966 - Muhammad ibn Ishaq ibn Rahawayh narrated to us, my father narrated, al-Fadl ibn Musa narrated, Bashir ibn Salman narrated, from Umar al-Ansari, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"20460","Those known by the kunya Abu Masud. Abu Masud al-Ghifari. 967 - Muhammad ibn Yaqub ibn Sawrah al-Baghdadi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Bakkar narrated, al-Hayyaj ibn Bistam narrated, Abbad narrated, from Nafi, from Abu Masud al-Ghifari, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say one day when the month of Ramadan had begun: If the servants knew what is in the month of Ramadan, the servants would wish that the month of Ramadan were a whole year. Then a man from Khuzaah said: O Messenger of Allah, tell us. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Paradise is adorned for the month of Ramadan from the beginning of the year to the beginning of the next year, until when it is the first night, a wind blows from beneath the Throne and rustles the leaves of the trees of Paradise, and the wide-eyed houris look at that and say: O Lord, appoint for us in this month husbands from Your servants whose eyes find joy in them and whose eyes find joy in us. And there is no servant who fasts the month of Ramadan except that Allah marries him to a wife each day from the wide-eyed houris secluded in pavilions; upon each woman among them are seventy robes, no robe of which is of the color of another, and he is given seventy kinds of perfume, no kind of which resembles another. And each woman among them is upon a couch of ruby inlaid with pearls, upon seventy beddings whose linings are of brocade, and above the seventy beddings are seventy thrones. And for each woman among them are seventy servants attending her and seventy servants for her needs, and nobles, with each servant a platter of gold in which is a kind of food; he finds in the last of it the same delight as he finds in the first of it. And her husband is given the like of that upon a couch of red ruby bearing two bracelets of gold inlaid with red ruby. This is for each day he fasted of the month of Ramadan, besides what he did of good deeds."
"20461","Those known by the kunya Abu Jubayrah. Abu Jubayrah ibn al-Dahhak al-Ansari, then al-Sulami, and it has been said al-Dahhak ibn Abi Jubayrah, but the correct view is Abu Jubayrah. 968 - Muadh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Musaddad narrated, Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal narrated, Dawud ibn Abi Hind narrated, from Amir al-Shabi, he said: Abu Jubayrah ibn al-Dahhak said: Concerning us this verse was revealed, meaning Banu Salimah: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to Madinah, and there was no man among us except that he had two or three names, so when a man was called by a name they would say: O Messenger of Allah, he dislikes this, so there was revealed: {And do not insult one another by nicknames} - the verse."
"20462","969 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated, Abdullah ibn Idris narrated, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from al-Shabi, from Abu Jubayrah ibn al-Dahhak al-Ansari, he said: Concerning us, the company of the Ansar, this verse was revealed: {And do not insult one another by nicknames}. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came, and a man had two or three names, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would sometimes call them by some of those names, so it would be said: O Messenger of Allah, he becomes angry at this, so there was revealed: {And do not insult one another by nicknames}."
"20463","970 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Hudbah ibn Khalid narrated, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from al-Shabi, from Abu Jubayrah ibn al-Dahhak, he said: The Ansar used to give charity and give whatever Allah willed, then a year of drought struck them so they withheld, so Allah revealed: {And do not throw yourselves with your own hands into destruction}."
"20464","971 - Ahmad ibn Amr al-Khallal al-Makki narrated to us, Yaqub ibn Humayd narrated, Marwan ibn Muawiyah narrated, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from Shubayl ibn Awf, from Abu Jubayrah ibn al-Dahhak al-Ansari, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I have been sent, I and the Hour, like this, and he joined the middle finger and the index finger, and I preceded it just as this one preceded this one."
"20465","972 - Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn al-Bara narrated to us, Ali ibn al-Madini narrated, Mutamir ibn Sulayman narrated, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from Shubayl ibn Awf, he said: Abu Jubayrah informed me, from some elders of the Ansar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"20466","Those known by the kunya Abu Israil. Abu Israil. 973 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Minhal, brother of al-Hajjaj, narrated, Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated, from Layth, from Tawus, from Abu Israil, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw him standing in the sun and said: What is the matter with him? They said: He vowed to stand in the sun, to fast, and not to speak. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Sit, seek shade, speak, and fast."
"20467","Those known by the kunya Abu Usayb. Abu Usayb, freedman of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 974 - Idris ibn Jafar al-Attar narrated to us, Yazid ibn Harun narrated, Muslim ibn Ubayd Abu Basirah narrated, he said: I heard Abu Usayb, freedman of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Jibril (peace be upon him) came to me with fever and plague, so I kept fever in Madinah and sent plague to Sham, as martyrdom for my ummah and punishment upon the disbelievers."
"20468","Those known by the kunya Abu Khallad. Abu Khallad. 975 - Ahmad ibn al-Mualla al-Dimashqi narrated to us, Hisham ibn Ammar narrated, al-Hakam ibn Hisham al-Thaqafi narrated, Yahya ibn Said ibn Aban narrated, from Abu Farwah, from Abu Khallad, and he was a Companion, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you see a believing man granted detachment from the world and sparing speech, draw near to him, for he has been given wisdom."
"20469","Those known by the kunya Abu al-Dayyah or Abu Siyah. Abu Dayyah al-Ansari, martyred on the day of Khaybar with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 976 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani narrated to us, my father narrated, Ibn Lahiah narrated, from Abu al-Aswad, from Urwah, concerning the naming of those martyred on the day of Khaybar from the Ansar, then from Banu Amr ibn Awf, with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Abu al-Dayyah or Abu Siyah."
"20470","Those known by the kunya Abu Aziz. Abu Aziz ibn Umayr ibn Hashim ibn Abd Manaf ibn Abd al-Dar ibn Qusayy, brother of Musab ibn Umayr; he has a hadith concerning the battle of Badr about the captives. 977 - al-Husayn ibn Ali al-Attar al-Missisi narrated to us, Shabab al-Usfuri narrated, Bakr ibn Sulayman narrated, Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated, Nubayh ibn Wahb narrated to me, from Abu Aziz ibn Umayr, brother of Musab ibn Umayr, he said: I was among the captives on the day of Badr, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Treat the captives well. And I was among a group of the Ansar, and when they would bring their morning and evening meals, they would eat dates and feed me bread, by the instruction of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to them."
"20471","Those known by the kunya Abu Qays. Abu Qays al-Ansari, who died in the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 978 - Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi narrated, Qays ibn al-Rabi narrated, from Ashath ibn Sawwar, from Adi ibn Thabit, from a man of the Ansar, he said: Abu Qays died, and he was among the righteous of the Ansar, so his son Qays proposed to his wife, and she said: I consider you a son, and you are among the righteous of your people, but I shall come to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and seek his counsel. So she came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: Abu Qays has died, and he spoke well to her. She said: And his son Qays has proposed to me, and he is among the righteous of his people, though I used to consider him a son. So he said to her: Return to your house. Then this verse was revealed: {And do not marry those women whom your fathers married}."
"20472","Those known by the kunya Abu Muawiyah. Abu Muawiyah ibn Abd al-Lat al-Azdi. 979 - Musa ibn Jumhur al-Tinnisi narrated to us, Ali ibn Harb al-Mawsili narrated, Ali ibn al-Husayn narrated, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Khalid ibn Uthman, from his father Khalid ibn Uthman, from his father Uthman ibn Muhammad, from his father Muhammad ibn Uthman, from his father Uthman ibn Abi Muawiyah, from his father Abu Muawiyah ibn Abd al-Lat, of Namir of Azd, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Trustworthiness is in Azd, and modesty is in Quraysh."
"20473","Those known by the kunya Abu Hasan. Abu Hasan al-Ansari, then al-Mazini, a veteran of Badr and Aqabah. 980 - Muadh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Ali ibn al-Madini narrated, and Jafar ibn Ahmad ibn Sinan al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: al-Qasim ibn Dinar narrated, both saying: Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated, he said: Husayn ibn Abdullah al-Hashimi narrated to me, he said: Amr ibn Yahya narrated to me, from his father, from his grandfather Abu Hasan, and he was a veteran of Badr and Aqabah, he said: We were sitting with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when a man stood up and forgot his sandals, so a man took them and placed them under himself. The man returned and said: My sandals. The people said: We have not seen them. Then a man said: Here they are. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What about the fright of the believer? He said: O Messenger of Allah, I only did it in jest. So he said: What about the fright of the believer? Two or three times."
"20474","981 - Jafar ibn Sulayman al-Nawfali al-Madani narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir al-Hizami narrated, Muhammad ibn Fulayh narrated, Amr ibn Yahya ibn Umarah ibn Abi al-Hasan al-Mazini narrated to me, from his father, from Umarah ibn Abi al-Hasan, he said: We took in the markets two young collared doves when Abu al-Hasan, Companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), entered, and he took a palm stalk for it and struck him with it."
"20475","Those known by the kunya Abu Kulayb. Abu Kulayb. 982 - Muhammad ibn Muhammad al-Judhuyi the judge narrated to us, al-Husayn ibn Mahdi narrated, Abd al-Razzaq narrated, Ibn Jurayj informed: I was informed, from Uthaym ibn Kulayb, from his father, from his grandfather, that he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Cast off from yourself the hair of disbelief and get circumcised."
"20476","Those known by the kunya Abu Khusayfah. Abu Khusayfah. 983 - Muhammad ibn Nasr al-Saigh narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Musayyibi narrated, Yahya ibn Yazid ibn Abd al-Malik narrated, from his father, from Yazid ibn Khusayfah, from his father, from his grandfather, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Seek good from those with handsome faces."
"20477","984 - Muhammad ibn Nasr al-Saigh narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Musayyibi narrated, Yahya ibn Yazid narrated, from his father, from Yazid ibn Khusayfah, from his father, from his grandfather, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say: When one of you leaves his house, let him say: In the name of Allah, there is no power nor strength except with Allah, whatever Allah wills, I place my trust in Allah, Allah is sufficient for me and He is the best Disposer of affairs."
"20478","What has reached us of the musnad of the women who narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); I have arranged their names according to the letters of the alphabet, beginning with the daughters of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his wives, so that none precede them. Fatimah was the youngest daughter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the most beloved to him, so I began with her out of the love of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for her. Mention of the age of Fatimah, may Allah be pleased with her, her death, and some of her reports, virtues, and kunya. 985 - al-Husayn ibn Fahm narrated to us, Musab ibn Abdullah al-Zubayri narrated, he said: The kunya of Fatimah is Umm Abiha."
"20479","986 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: More than one said to me: Fatimah was the youngest of them and the most beloved to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), while al-Zubayr ibn Bakkar claimed that Ruqayyah was younger than Fatimah."
"20480","987 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, al-Zubayr ibn Bakkar narrated, he said: The children of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) were: al-Qasim, and he was his eldest child, then Zaynab, then Abdullah, and he was called al-Tayyib, and also called al-Tahir, born after prophethood and died small, then Umm Kulthum, then Fatimah, then Ruqayyah, thus in order, first to last. Al-Qasim died in Makkah, then Abdullah died."
"20481","988 - Muhammad ibn Ali al-Madini Fustuqah narrated to us, he said: Fatimah, daughter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), was known by the kunya Umm Abiha. It is said she was the youngest child of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from Khadijah, and it is said rather that she was the twin of Abdullah ibn the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"20482","989 - Abu Zurah Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr narrated to us, Abu al-Yaman al-Hakam ibn Nafi narrated, Shuayb ibn Abi Hamzah informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Aishah, she said: Fatimah died six months after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Ali ibn Abi Talib buried her at night."
"20483","990 - al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Asfati narrated to us, Ismail ibn Abi Uways narrated, my father narrated to me, from Sulayman ibn Bilal, from Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Atiq, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Aishah, that Fatimah lived six months after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"20484","991 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that Ali buried Fatimah at night."
"20485","992 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated, Yahya ibn Said narrated, from Sufyan, from Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, that Ali buried Fatimah at night."
"20486","993 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: Fatimah remained six months after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then she died."
"20487","994 - al-Hasan ibn Ali al-Mamari narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Humayd al-Razi narrated, Salamah ibn al-Fadl narrated, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Yahya ibn Abbad ibn Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, from his father, he said: Aishah said: Fatimah lived six months after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"20488","995 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Abu Jafar, he said: Fatimah remained three months after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and she was not seen laughing after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), except that they disputed about the edge of her eyetooth."
"20489","996 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Mamar, from Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, that when death came to Fatimah, she ordered Ali to prepare washing water for her, so she bathed and purified herself, and called for her shrouding clothes, so coarse rough clothes were brought and she wore them, and she touched perfume, and she ordered Ali not to uncover her when she passed away, and to wrap her as she was in her clothes. So I said to him: Do you know anyone who did that? He said: Yes, Kathir ibn Abbas, and he wrote on the edges of his shrouds: Kathir ibn Abbas testifies that there is no god but Allah."
"20490","997 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, he said: I heard Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah say: Fatimah, daughter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), died when she was twenty-seven years old."
"20491","998 - Ahmad ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahim al-Barqi narrated to us, Abd al-Malik ibn Hisham narrated, Ziyad ibn Abdullah al-Bakkai narrated, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, he said: Fatimah died when she was twenty-eight years old. Her birth was when Quraysh were building the Kabah, and Quraysh built the Kabah seven years and six months before the mission of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) remained in Makkah ten years after his mission, then emigrated and remained ten years, then Fatimah lived six months after him, and she died in the eleventh year of the Hijrah."
"20492","Among the virtues of Fatimah, may Allah be pleased with her. 999 - Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, Abd al-Hamid ibn Bahr al-Zahrani narrated, Khalid ibn Abdullah narrated, from Bayan, from al-Shabi, from Abu Juhayfah, from Ali, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: When it is the Day of Resurrection, it will be said: O people of the gathering, lower your gazes until Fatimah bint Muhammad passes. So she will pass wearing two green or two red garments."
"20493","1000 - Abdullah ibn Said ibn Yahya al-Raqqi narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Abi Shaybah al-Rahawi narrated, Abu Qatadah al-Harrani narrated, Sufyan al-Thawri narrated, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, she said: I used to see the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kiss Fatimah, so I said: O Messenger of Allah, I see you do something I never saw you do before. So he said to me: O Humayra, on the night I was taken up to heaven I was admitted into Paradise, and I stood by a tree of the trees of Paradise, never having seen in Paradise a tree more beautiful than it, nor whiter in leaf, nor more pleasant in fruit. So I took a fruit from its fruit and ate it, and it became a sperm-drop in my loins. Then when I descended to earth I was with Khadijah, so she conceived Fatimah. So whenever I long for the fragrance of Paradise, I smell the scent of Fatimah. O Humayra, Fatimah is not like the women of the children of Adam, nor does she suffer illness as they do."
"20494","1001 - Bishr ibn Musa and Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us, both saying: Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Salim al-Qazzaz narrated, he said: Husayn ibn Zayd ibn Ali and Ali ibn Umar ibn Ali narrated, from Jafar ibn Muhammad, from his father, from Ali ibn al-Husayn, from al-Husayn ibn Ali, from Ali, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Fatimah: Allah becomes angry for your anger and is pleased for your pleasure."
"20495","1002 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz and Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, both saying: Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated, Ali ibn Zayd informed, from Anas ibn Malik, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would pass by the house of Fatimah for six months when he went out to the dawn prayer, saying: Prayer! {Allah only intends to remove impurity from you, O people of the House, and to purify you thoroughly}."
"20496","1003 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, Abd al-Razzaq informed, Mamar conveyed, from Qatadah, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Sufficient for you among the women of the worlds are Maryam bint Imran, Khadijah bint Khuwaylid, Asiyah bint Muzahim, wife of Pharaoh, and Fatimah bint Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"20497","1004 - al-Qasim ibn Zakariyya al-Mutarriz narrated to us, Yusuf ibn Musa al-Qattan narrated, Tamim ibn al-Jad narrated, all saying: Abu Jafar al-Razi narrated, from Thabit al-Bunani, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The best women of the worlds are Maryam bint Imran, Asiyah bint Muzahim, Khadijah bint Khuwaylid, and Fatimah bint Muhammad, peace be upon her."
"20498","1005 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Asim ibn Ali narrated, Qays ibn al-Rabi narrated, from Maysarah ibn Habib, from Adi ibn Thabit, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, from Hudhayfah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This is an angel who sought permission from his Lord to greet me and visit me; he never descended to earth before it. And he gave me glad tidings that Hasan and Husayn are the masters of the youth of Paradise, and that their mother is the mistress of the women of Paradise."
"20499","1006 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, Muhammad ibn Marwan al-Dhuhli narrated, Abu Hazim narrated to me, Abu Hurayrah narrated to me, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: An angel from heaven who had never visited me sought permission from Allah to visit me, and he gave me glad tidings, or informed me, that Fatimah is the mistress of the women of my ummah."
"20500","1007 - Zakariyya ibn Yahya al-Saji narrated to us, Khalid ibn Yusuf al-Samti narrated, Abu Awanah narrated, from Umar ibn Abi Salamah, from his father, he said: Usamah ibn Zayd informed me, he said: I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Which of your household is dearest to you? He said: The dearest of my household to me is Fatimah."
"20501","1008 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, Abd al-Salam ibn Harb narrated, from Abu al-Jahhaf, from Jumay ibn Umayr, he said: I entered with my paternal aunt upon Aishah, and I said: O Mother of the Believers, which people were dearest to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? She said: Fatimah."
"20502","1009 - al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Yahya al-Himmani narrated, Sharik narrated, from al-Amash, from Jumay ibn Umayr, he said: I entered, I and my maternal aunt, upon Aishah, and my aunt said to her: O Mother of the Believers, who was dearest of people to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? She said: His daughter Fatimah."
"20503","1010 - Muttalib ibn Shuayb al-Azdi narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Salih narrated, al-Layth narrated to me, Abdullah ibn Ubaydullah ibn Abi Mulaykah al-Qurashi narrated to me: That al-Miswar ibn Makhramah informed him that he heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say upon the pulpit: My daughter is but a part of me; what disturbs her disturbs me, and what harms her harms me."
"20504","1011 - Musa ibn Harun narrated to us, Qutaybah ibn Said narrated, Ibn Lahiah narrated, Ibn Abi Mulaykah narrated, from al-Miswar ibn Makhramah: That the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ascended the pulpit, praised Allah and extolled Him, then said: My daughter, meaning Fatimah, is but a part of me; what disturbs her disturbs me, and what harms her harms me."
"20505","1012 - Ahmad ibn Muhammad al-Khuzai al-Asbahani narrated to us, Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated, Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, from al-Miswar ibn Makhramah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Fatimah is a part of me; whoever angers her angers me."
"20506","1013 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Mani narrated to me, and Muhammad ibn Salih ibn al-Walid al-Narsi narrated to us, Muammal ibn Hisham narrated, both saying: Ismail ibn Ulayyah narrated, from Ayyub, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, from Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, that Ali proposed to the daughter of Abu Jahl, and that reached the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he said: Fatimah is a part of me; what harms her harms me, and what angers her angers me."
"20507","1014 - Jafar ibn Harun al-Nawfali al-Madani narrated to us, Abd al-Aziz ibn Abdullah al-Uwaysi narrated, he said: Abdullah ibn Jafar al-Mukharrimi narrated, from Umm Bakr bint al-Miswar, from her father: That the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Fatimah is a branch of me; what angers her angers me, and what delights her delights me."
"20508","1015 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated, Abu al-Jawwab al-Ahwas ibn Jawwab narrated, from Sulayman ibn Qarm, from Harun ibn Sad, from Hurrah, from Asma bint Umays, she said: Ali ibn Abi Talib proposed to me, and that reached Fatimah, so she came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: Asma is marrying Ali. So he said: It was not for her to harm Allah and His Messenger."
"20509","1016 - Muhammad ibn Hayyan al-Mazini narrated to us, Kathir ibn Yahya narrated, Said ibn Abd al-Karim ibn Salit and Abu Awanah narrated, from Dawud ibn Abi Awf Abu al-Jahhaf, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Ziyad, that he heard Abdullah ibn al-Harith ibn Nawfal say: Abu Said al-Khudri narrated: That the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon Fatimah one day while Ali was standing, she was lying down with her sons beside her. Then Hasan asked for a drink, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went to a milch camel and milked for them, and brought it. Then Husayn woke up and kept trying to drink before him until he wept. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Your brother asked for a drink before you. So Fatimah said: Is Hasan dearer to you? He said: He is not dearer to me than him; rather they are both with me in one station. And I, you, they two, and this sleeping one will be in one place on the Day of Resurrection."
"20510","1017 - Muhammad ibn Hayyan al-Mazini narrated to us, Kathir ibn Yahya narrated, Said ibn Abd al-Karim ibn Salit al-Hanafi narrated, from Amr ibn Abi al-Miqdam, from his father, from Abu Fakhita, from Ali, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon us while I was sleeping. Then Hasan asked for a drink, so he went to a waterskin of ours from which we pour, then brought the vessel. Then Husayn came to him asking for a drink, so he said: Your brother asked for a drink before you; he shall drink, then you shall drink. So Fatimah said: As if he is dearer of the two to you. So he said: He is not dearer of the two to me, and they are both with me in one station. I, you, they two, and this lying one will be in one place."
"20511","1018 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami and al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, both saying: Abu Kurayb narrated, Muawiyah ibn Hisham narrated, from Amr ibn Ghiyath, from Asim, from Zirr, from Abdullah ibn Masud, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Fatimah guarded her chastity, so Allah has forbidden her and her offspring to the Fire."
"20512","1019 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Shayban ibn Farrukh narrated, Dawud ibn Abi al-Furat narrated, Ilba ibn Ahmad narrated, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) drew four lines on the ground and said: Do you know what this is? They said: Allah and His Messenger know best. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The best women of Paradise are: Maryam bint Imran, Asiyah bint Muzahim, Khadijah bint Khuwaylid, and Fatimah bint Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"20513","Mention of the marriage of Fatimah, may Allah be pleased with her. 1020 - Ali ibn Said al-Razi and Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Husayn al-Sabuni al-Tustari narrated to us, both saying: Ismail ibn Musa al-Suddi narrated, Bishr ibn al-Walid al-Hashimi narrated, Abd al-Nur ibn Abdullah al-Mismai narrated, from Shubah ibn al-Hajjaj, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Ibrahim, he said: Masruq narrated to me, from Abdullah ibn Masud, he said: I shall narrate to you a hadith I heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and I kept seeking testimony for the hadith but was not granted it. I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) during the expedition of Tabuk say, while we were traveling with him: Allah commanded me to marry Fatimah to Ali, so I did. Jibril (peace be upon him) said: Allah has built a Paradise of hollow pearls; between every reed and reed is a pearl of ruby inlaid with gold, and He made its roofs green peridot, and made in it windows of pearl crowned with ruby, then made upon it chambers, brick of silver and brick of gold and brick of pearl and brick of ruby and brick of peridot, then made in it springs gushing in its sides, surrounded by rivers, and made upon the rivers domes of pearl fastened with chains of gold, surrounded by kinds of trees, and built in every branch a dome, and made in every dome a couch of white pearl whose covering is silk and brocade, and its floor strewn with saffron and suffused with musk and ambergris, and made in every dome a houri, and each dome has a hundred doors, upon every door two guards and two trees; in every dome is a bedding and a book, and around the domes is written Ayat al-Kursi. I said to Jibril: For whom did Allah build this Paradise? He said: He built it for your daughter Fatimah and Ali ibn Abi Talib, besides her other Paradises, as a gift He has gifted, and that your eyes may be comforted, O Messenger of Allah."
"20514","1021 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Hammad al-Hadrami narrated, Yahya ibn Yala al-Aslami narrated, from Said ibn Abi Arubah, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: Abu Bakr came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and sat before him, saying: O Messenger of Allah, you know my good counsel and my precedence in Islam, and so and so. He said: And what is that? He said: Marry Fatimah to me. So he remained silent toward him, or he turned away from him. So Abu Bakr returned to Umar and said: I am ruined and have ruined. He said: And what is that? He said: I proposed to Fatimah to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he turned away from me. So he said: Stay here until I come to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and seek the like of what you sought. So Umar came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and sat before him, saying: O Messenger of Allah, you know my good counsel and my precedence in Islam, and so and so. He said: And what is that? He said: Marry Fatimah to me. So he turned away from him. So Umar returned to Abu Bakr and said: He is awaiting the command of Allah concerning her. Come with us to Ali that we may bid him seek the like of what we sought. Ali said: So they came to me while I was on a path, and said: Your cousin is being proposed to. So they alerted me to a matter. So I stood up dragging my cloak, one end upon my shoulder and the other end on the ground, until I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So I sat before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, you know my precedence in Islam and my good counsel, and so and so. He said: And what is that, O Ali? I said: Marry Fatimah to me. He said: And what do you have? I said: My horse and my armor, meaning my coat of mail. He said: As for your horse, you cannot do without it, but as for your armor, sell it. So I sold it for four hundred and eighty, and brought it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and placed it in his lap. So he took a handful from it and said: O Bilal, seek perfume for us with it, and order them to prepare her trousseau. So a couch laced with strips was made for her, and a pillow of leather stuffed with palm fiber, and the house was filled with sand, meaning gravel. And he said: When she comes to you, do not do anything until I come to you. So she came with Umm Ayman and sat on one side of the house, and I on one side. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came and said: Is my brother here? So Umm Ayman said: Your brother, whom you have married to your daughter. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered and said to Fatimah: Bring me water. So she stood to a wooden cup in the house and put water in it, and brought it to him. So he rinsed his mouth into it, then said to her: Stand. So he sprinkled between her breasts and upon her head, then said: O Allah, I seek refuge for her in You and her offspring from the accursed Satan. Then he said to her: Turn back. So she turned back, and he sprinkled between her shoulders, then said: O Allah, I seek refuge for her in You and her offspring from the accursed Satan. Then he said: Bring me water. So I did what he wanted; I filled the cup with water and brought it to him. So he took some of it in his mouth, then spat it into it, then poured upon my head and before me, then said: O Allah, I seek refuge for him and his offspring from the accursed Satan. Then he said: Enter upon your wife in the name of Allah and with blessing."
"20515","1022 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Yahya ibn al-Ala, from his uncle Shuayb ibn Khalid, from Hanzalah ibn Sabrah ibn al-Musayyib ibn Najiyah, from his father, from his grandfather, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Fatimah would be mentioned to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), but no one mentioned her except that he turned away from him, until they despaired of her. Then Sad ibn Muadh met Ali and said: By Allah, I do not see the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) withholding her except for you. So Ali said to him: Why do you think that? By Allah, I am not one of the two men: I am not a man of worldly means seeking what is with me, and he knows I have neither yellow nor white coin, nor am I the disbeliever whom he conciliates with her concerning his religion, meaning he wins him over with her. I am the first who embraced Islam. So Sad said: I adjure you to relieve me of it, for I have relief in that. He said: What shall I say? He said: Say: I have come as a suitor to Allah and His Messenger for Fatimah bint Muhammad. He said: So Ali went, heavy and tongue-tied. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: It is as if you have a need, O Ali? He said: Yes, I have come to you as a suitor to Allah and to His Messenger for Fatimah bint Muhammad. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Welcome, a faint word. Then he returned to Sad ibn Muadh and said to him: I did what you bade me, and he did no more than welcome me with a faint word. So Sad said: He has married her to you, by Him who sent him with the truth; there is no breaking now, nor falsehood with him. And I adjure you to come to him tomorrow and say: O Prophet of Allah, when will you send me to my wife? So Ali said: This is harder upon me than the first. Shall I not rather say: O Messenger of Allah, my need? He said: Say as I bade you. So Ali went and said: O Messenger of Allah, when will you send me to my wife? He said: Tonight, if Allah wills. Then he called Bilal and said: O Bilal, I have married my daughter to my cousin, and I love that part of the practice of my ummah be food at marriage. So go to the sheep, take a sheep and four mudds, and make a bowl for me, that I may gather the Emigrants and Helpers upon it. Then when you finish, inform me. So he went and did what he bade him, then brought him a bowl and placed it before him. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) thrust into its top and said: Bring people in to me group by group, and do not pass a group to another, meaning when a group finishes, let it not return a second time. So people kept coming, each time a group finished another came, until people finished. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned to what remained of it, spat into it and blessed it, and said: O Bilal, carry it to your mothers and say to them: Eat and feed whoever visits you. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood until he entered upon the women and said: I have married my daughter to my cousin, and you know her station with me, and I am handing her to him, so here is your daughter. So the women stood and perfumed her from their perfume, dressed her from their clothes, and adorned her from their jewelry. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered, and when the women saw him they left, with a curtain between them and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), while Asma bint Umays stayed behind. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: Gently, who are you? She said: I am she who guards your daughter; the girl on the night she is sent to her husband must have a woman near her, if she needs something or wants anything she confides it to her. He said: I ask my God to guard you from before you and behind you, from your right and your left, from the accursed Satan. Then he called Fatimah, and she came. When she saw Ali sitting with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), she was abashed and wept. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) feared her weeping was because Ali had no wealth. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What makes you weep? I have not fallen short for you in myself, and I have chosen for you the best of my household. By Him in whose hand is my soul, I have married you to one fortunate in this world, and in the Hereafter he is among the righteous. So she softened. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Asma, bring me the washing basin and fill it with water. So Asma brought the basin and filled it. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rinsed his mouth into it and wiped his face and feet in it. Then he called Fatimah and took a handful of water and struck it upon her head and a handful between her breasts, then sprinkled her skin and his skin, then embraced her and said: O Allah, she is from me and I am from her. O Allah, just as You removed impurity from me and purified me, so purify her. Then he called for another basin, then called Ali and did with him as he did with her, then supplicated for him as he supplicated for her, then said to them both: Go to your house; may Allah join you, bless your journey, and set your affairs right. Then he stood and closed their door upon them with his hand. Ibn Abbas said: Asma bint Umays informed me that she watched the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he kept supplicating for them both exclusively, associating none with them in his supplication, until he disappeared into his chamber (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"20516","What Fatimah, may Allah be pleased with her, narrated. Ali ibn Abi Talib from Fatimah, peace be upon them both. 1023 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Said ibn Mansur narrated, Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad al-Darawardi narrated, from Jafar ibn Muhammad, from his father, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, he said: Ali ibn Abi Talib entered upon Fatimah and saw her wearing kohl and dyed garments, meaning during their Hajj with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So he said to her: Who commanded you with this? She said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Ali said: So I went, complaining against Fatimah, seeking a ruling from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: She spoke the truth, she spoke the truth."
"20517","Abdullah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, from Fatimah, may Allah be pleased with her. 1024 - Bakr ibn Muqbil al-Basri narrated to us, al-Khalil ibn Asad al-Nushajani narrated, Ruwaym ibn Yazid al-Muqri narrated, Sawwar ibn Musab al-Hamdani narrated, from Amr ibn Qays al-Mulai, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Shaqiq ibn Salamah, from Ibn Masud, he said: A man came to Fatimah bint the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O daughter of the Messenger of Allah, did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) leave with you anything with which you may present me a gift? So she said: O maid, bring that palm stalk. So she sought it but did not find it. So she said: Woe to you, seek it, for it equals Hasan and Husayn with me. So she sought it, and it was found swept into her sweepings. And in it was: Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He is not of the believers whose neighbor is not safe from his harms. Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him honor his guest. And whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him not harm his neighbor. And whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him speak good or be silent. And Allah loves the modest, forbearing, weak, chaste one, and hates the obscene, foul-tongued, begging, importunate one. Modesty is of faith, and faith is in Paradise. And obscenity is of foul speech, and foul speech is in the Fire."
"20518","Al-Husayn ibn Ali from Fatimah. 1025 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, al-Hakam ibn Musa narrated, Yahya ibn Hamzah narrated, from al-Hakam ibn Abdullah al-Ayli, that he heard Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Husayn say: Al-Husayn went out intending his land on the outskirts of al-Harrah, while we were walking, when al-Numan ibn Bashir caught up with us upon a mule. So he alighted and brought it near to al-Husayn, saying: Ride, O Abdullah. But he disliked that, and al-Numans oaths upon him persisted until al-Husayn complied with riding. He said: Since you have sworn, you have burdened me with what I dislike, so ride upon the front of your beast, and I shall ride behind you. For I heard Fatimah bint Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A man has most right to the front of his beast, the front of his bedding, and prayer in his house, except an imam who gathers people under him. So al-Numan said: The daughter of Muhammad, the Messenger of Allah, spoke the truth. I heard my father Bashir say the like of what Fatimah said. And the Messenger of Allah said: Except one who gives permission. So Husayn rode upon the saddle, with the Ansari behind him."
"20519","1026 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Jandal ibn Waliq narrated, Muhammad ibn Umar al-Mazini narrated, from Abbad al-Kalbi, from Jafar ibn Muhammad, from his father, from Ali ibn Husayn, from Fatimah al-Sughra, from Husayn ibn Ali, from his mother Fatimah bint the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us on the evening of Arafah and said: Allah has boasted of you and forgiven you generally, and Ali specifically. And I am the Messenger of Allah to you, not favoring my kin. This is Jibril informing me that the truly fortunate is he who loves Ali in his life and after his death, and that the utterly wretched is he who hates Ali in his life and after his death."
"20520","Abdullah ibn Abbas from Fatimah. 1027 - Ahmad ibn Yahya al-Hulwani narrated to us, Said ibn Sulayman narrated, from Abbad ibn al-Awwam, from Hilal ibn Khabbab, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: When {When the help of Allah and victory come} was revealed, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called Fatimah and said: My soul has been announced to me. So she wept. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: Do not weep, for you are the first of my household to join me. So she laughed. Then one of the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw her and said to her: I saw you weep and laugh. She said: He said to me: My soul has been announced to me, so I wept. Then he said: Do not weep, for you are the first of my household to join me. So I laughed."
"20521","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, from Fatimah, may Allah be pleased with her. 1028 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Mamar, from Thabit al-Bunani, from Anas ibn Malik, that Fatimah wept over the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: O my father, how near he is to his Lord! O my father, to Jibril we announce his death! O my father, the Garden of Firdaws is his abode!"
"20522","1029 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Arim narrated, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated, from Thabit, from Anas, that Fatimah said: O Anas, how could your hearts bear to throw dust upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? O my father, how near he is to his Lord! O my father, the Garden of Firdaws is his abode! O my father, to Jibril we announce his death! O my father, he answered a Lord who called him!"
"20523","What Aishah, Mother of the Believers, narrated from Fatimah, may Allah be pleased with them both. 1030 - Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ubayd al-Muharibi narrated, Abd al-Karim ibn Yaqub narrated, from Jabir, from Abu al-Tufayl, he said: Aishah said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fell ill in my house, and Fatimah came to him walking; by Him in whose hand is the soul of Aishah, her gait was as the gait of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) confided to her and she wept, then confided to her and she laughed. So I said: I have never seen laughter nearer to weeping than today. So I said: O Fatimah, inform me what he said to you? She said: I would not do so while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has seen your place. Then when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed away, she was asked, and she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Jibril used to review the Quran with me once each year, and this year he reviewed it with me twice, and I see myself only as one called and answering. So fear Allah. She said: So I grieved. Then he confided to me again and said: Are you not pleased that your husband is the first of the Muslims in Islam and the most knowledgeable of them in knowledge, and that you are the mistress of the women of my ummah, just as Maryam was mistress of the women of her people."
"20524","1031 - Yahya ibn Ayyub al-Allaf al-Misri narrated to us, Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated, Nafi ibn Yazid narrated, Umarah ibn Ghaziyyah narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Amr ibn Uthman, that his mother Fatimah bint Husayn narrated to him, that Aishah used to say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), in his illness in which he passed away, said to Fatimah: O my daughter, bend over me. So she bent over him and he confided to her for a while, then she drew back weeping, while Aishah was present. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said after that after a while: Bend over me, O my daughter. So she bent over him, and he confided to her for a while, then she drew back from him laughing. Aishah said: So I said: O my daughter, inform me what your father confided to you? So Fatimah said: He confided to me in confidence; did you think I would disclose his secret while he is alive? That was hard upon Aishah, that it be a secret besides her. Then when Allah took him, Aishah said to Fatimah: O my daughter, will you not inform me of that report? She said: As for now, yes. The first time he confided to me and informed me that Jibril (peace be upon him) used to review the Quran with him once each year, and that this year he reviewed the Quran with him twice. And he informed me that he informed him that no prophet lived except half the lifespan of the one before him, and that he informed me that Isa ibn Maryam lived one hundred and twenty years, and I see myself only as departing at the head of sixty. So that made me weep. And he said: O my daughter, there is no woman among the Muslim women greater in calamity than you, so be not less than any woman in patience. And the last time he confided to me and informed me that I am the first of his household to join him, and said: You are the mistress of the women of Paradise, except for what was of the pure Maryam bint Imran. So I laughed at that."
"20525","1032 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Nuaym narrated, Zakariyya ibn Abi Zaidah narrated, from Firas, from al-Shabi, from Masruq, from Aishah, she said: Fatimah came walking as if her gait were the gait of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So he said: Welcome to my daughter. Then he seated her on his right or on his left, then confided a word to her and she wept. So I said to her: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) singled you out with his word, then you weep. Then he confided a word to her and she laughed. So I said: I have never seen joy nearer to grief than today. So I asked her what he said? So she said: I would not disclose the secret of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then when he passed away, I asked her, and she said: He confided to me saying: Jibril used to review the Quran with me each year once, and this year he reviewed it with me twice, and I see it only as that my term has come. And you are the first of the people of my house to join me, and what an excellent predecessor I am for you. So I wept at that. Then he said: Are you not pleased to be the mistress of the women of the ummah, or the women of the Muslims? So I laughed at that."
"20526","1033 - Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, Sahl ibn Bakkar narrated, Abu Awanah narrated, from Firas, from Amir, from Masruq, from Aishah, she said: The wives of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gathered, none of them absent. Then Fatimah came walking, her gait not differing from the gait of her father (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So he said: Welcome, O my daughter. Then he seated her on his right or on his left, then confided something to her and she wept, then confided to her and she laughed. So we said to her: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) singled you out from among us for a secret, and you weep. Then when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood, I said to her: Inform me of what he confided to you. She said: I would not disclose the secret of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed away, I said to her: I ask you by what right I have upon you to inform me. So she said: As for now, yes. He confided to me saying: Jibril used to review the Quran with me each year once, and this year he reviewed it with me twice, and I see that only as the approach of my term. So fear Allah and be patient, for what an excellent predecessor I am for you. So I wept. Then he confided to me saying: Are you not pleased to be the mistress of the believing women, or he said: the women of this ummah? So I laughed."
"20527","1034 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated, and Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Minjab ibn al-Harith narrated, both saying: Ali ibn Mushir narrated, from Muhammad ibn Amr, from Abu Salamah, from Aishah, she said: I said to Fatimah bint the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): I saw you lean over the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his illness and weep, then lean over him a second time and laugh. She said: I leaned over him and he informed me that he would die, so I wept. Then I leaned over him and he informed me that I am the first of the people of his house to join him, and that I am the mistress of the women of Paradise, except for what was of Maryam bint Imran. So I laughed."
"20528","1035 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Abu Kamil al-Jahdari narrated to me, Rawh ibn Ata ibn Abi Maymunah narrated, Ali ibn Zayd ibn Judan narrated, from Umm Muhammad, from Aishah, she said: While I was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in a house, he playing with me and I playing with him, Fatimah entered upon us. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took her hand and seated her behind him, and confided something to her which I do not know. Then I looked at Fatimah weeping. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned to me and spoke to me and played with me. Then he turned to her and played with her and confided something to her. Then I looked at Fatimah, and she was laughing. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood and went out. So I said to Fatimah: What did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) confide to you? She said: Each time the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) confides to me, I do not inform you of it. I said: I remind you by Allah and kinship. She said: He informed me that he has been taken, his term has come, so I wept at parting from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then he turned to me and confided to me that I am the first of the people of his house to join him, so I laughed at meeting the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"20529","1036 - al-Hasan ibn Ali al-Mamari narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Humayd al-Razi narrated, Salamah ibn al-Fadl narrated, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Yahya ibn Abbad ibn Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, from his father, from Aishah, she said: Fatimah bint the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered, and he confided something to her and she wept. Then he called her with something and she laughed. Then when he passed away, I said: Inform me, what made you weep then laugh? She said: His death was announced to him, so I wept. Then he confided to me saying: You are the first of my household to join me. So I laughed. Aishah said: So she lived after him only six months."
"20530","1037 - Abu Zurah Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr al-Dimashqi narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Dawud al-Hashimi narrated, and al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari narrated to us, Yahya ibn Abd al-Hamid al-Himmani narrated, both saying: Ibrahim ibn Sad narrated, from his father, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Aishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called Fatimah in his illness and confided to her and she wept. Then he confided to her and she laughed. So I asked her about that, what was it you saw that he confided to you and you wept, then confided to you and you laughed? So she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed me of his death, so I wept. Then he informed me that I am the first of his household to follow him, so I laughed."
"20531","1038 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Yahya ibn Main narrated, Uthman ibn Umar narrated, Israil narrated, from Maysarah ibn Habib, from al-Minhal ibn Amr, from Aishah bint Talhah, from Aishah, Mother of the Believers, she said: Fatimah said: And he informed me - meaning the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - that I am the first of his household to join him."
"20532","Umm Salamah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from Fatimah. 1039 - Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Jafar ibn Musafir al-Tinnisi narrated, Muhammad ibn Ismail ibn Abi Fudayk narrated, from Musa ibn Yaqub al-Zamai, from Hashim ibn Hashim: That Abdullah ibn Wahb ibn Zamah informed him, that Umm Salamah informed him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called Fatimah after the Conquest and confided to her and she wept, then spoke to her and she laughed. Umm Salamah said: So I did not ask her until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed away, then I asked Fatimah about her weeping and laughing. So Fatimah said: He informed me that he would die, then he informed me that I am the mistress of the women of Paradise after Maryam bint Imran. So that is why I laughed."
"20533","Asma bint Umays from Fatimah. 1040 - Ismail ibn al-Hasan al-Khaffaf al-Misri narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Salih narrated, Ibn Abi Fudayk narrated, Musa ibn Yaqub narrated to me, from Awn ibn Muhammad, from his mother Umm Jafar, from her grandmother Asma bint Umays, from Fatimah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to her one day and said: Where are my two sons? Meaning Hasan and Husayn. She said: We woke with nothing in our house for any taster to taste. So Ali said: I shall take them, for I fear they will weep over you while you have nothing. So he went to a certain Jew and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) headed toward him and found them playing in a drinking place with some dates before them. So he said: O Ali, will you not turn my two sons back before the heat intensifies upon them? So Ali said: We woke with nothing in our house, so if you would sit, O Prophet of Allah, until I gather some dates for Fatimah. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sat until something of dates was gathered for Fatimah. Then he placed it in his bundle and came forward. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) carried one of them and Ali the other, until he brought them back."
"20534","Zaynab bint Abi Rafi from Fatimah. 1041 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Yaqub ibn Humayd ibn Kasib narrated to me, Ibrahim ibn Hasan ibn Ali narrated, from his father, he said: Zaynab bint Abi Rafi narrated to me, from Fatimah bint the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that she brought Hasan and Husayn to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his complaint in which he passed away, saying: O Messenger of Allah, these two are your sons, so bequeath them something. So he said: As for Hasan, his is my dignity and mastery; and as for Husayn, his is my courage and generosity."
"20535","Mursal reports from Fatimah. 1042 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated, Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated, from Shaybah ibn Naamah, from Fatimah al-Sughra, from Fatimah al-Kubra, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Every daughter has paternal kin to whom they are ascribed, except the children of Fatimah, for I am their guardian and I am their paternal kin."
"20536","1043 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Qays ibn al-Rabi, from Abdullah ibn al-Hasan, from Fatimah bint al-Husayn, from Fatimah al-Kubra, she said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered the mosque he would say: O Allah, forgive me my sins and open for me the doors of Your mercy. And when he left he would say the like, except that he would say: the doors of Your bounty."
"20537","1044 - Muadh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Musaddad narrated, Abd al-Warith ibn Said narrated, from Layth ibn Abi Sulaym, from Abdullah ibn Hasan, from his mother Fatimah bint Husayn, from her grandmother Fatimah, she said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered the mosque he would send blessings upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and say: O Allah, forgive me my sins and open for me the doors of Your mercy. And when he left he would send blessings upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and say: O Allah, forgive me my sins and open for me the doors of Your bounty."
"20538","Zaynab bint the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was under Abu al-As ibn al-Rabi ibn Abd al-Uzza ibn Abd Shams ibn Abd Manaf, and she bore him Ali ibn Abi al-As, who died young, and Umamah bint Abi al-As. Mention of the age of Zaynab, her death, and some of her reports. 1045 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: More than one said to me: Zaynab was the eldest daughter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"20539","1046 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, al-Zubayr ibn Bakkar narrated, he said: There was born to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) al-Qasim, and he was his eldest child, then Zaynab. He said: And Zaynab bint the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was with Abu al-As ibn al-Rabi ibn Abd al-Uzza ibn Abd Shams, and she bore him Ali and Umamah. Ali was nursed among Banu Ghadrah, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took him away while his father was then a polytheist. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever shares with me in something, I have most right to it; and whichever disbeliever shares with a Muslim in something, he has most right to it than him. Al-Zubayr said: And Umar ibn Abi Bakr al-Muammali narrated to me, he said: Ali ibn Abi al-As ibn al-Rabi, son of Zaynab bint the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), died near puberty, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had mounted him behind him on his mount on the day of the Conquest."
"20540","1047 - Abu Yazid al-Qaratis narrated to us, Abdullah ibn al-Hakam narrated, he said: Ibn Lahiah narrated, from Musa ibn Jubayr al-Ansari, from Irak ibn Malik, from Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Umm Salamah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): That Zaynab bint the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sought permission from Abu al-As ibn al-Rabi when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) left emigrating, to go to him, so he permitted her. So she came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then Abu al-As ibn al-Rabi caught up with her in Madinah, so she sent to her asking to take protection from her father. So she put her head out of the door of her chamber while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was praying the dawn prayer with the people, saying: O people, I am Zaynab, and I have granted protection to Abu al-As. Then when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished the prayer, he said: I did not know of this until I heard it just now. And the lowest of the Muslims grants protection on their behalf."
"20541","1048 - Abu al-Zinba Ruh ibn al-Faraj and Ahmad ibn Rashdin al-Misriyyan narrated to us, both saying: Yahya ibn Bukayr narrated, Abdullah ibn al-Samh narrated to me, from Abbad ibn Kathir, from Uqayl ibn Khalid, from Ibn Shihab, from Anas ibn Malik: That Zaynab bint the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) granted protection to Abu al-As, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upheld her protection; and that Umm Hani bint Abi Talib granted protection to her brother Aqil, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upheld her protection."
"20542","1049 - Ahmad ibn Yahya Thalab al-Nahwi narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Shabib narrated, Ayyub ibn Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Uways narrated, from Sulayman ibn Bilal, from Salih ibn Kaysan, from al-Zuhri, from Anas: That Zaynab emigrated to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while her husband was a disbeliever, then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) captured Ibn al-Rabi. So Zaynab said: I have granted protection to Abu al-As. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upheld her protection, and said: The lowest of the Muslims grants protection on their behalf."
"20543","1050 - Abdullah ibn al-Hasan ibn Ahmad ibn Abi Shuayb narrated to us, Abu Jafar al-Nufayli narrated, Muhammad ibn Salamah narrated, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, he said: Among the captives on the day of Badr was Abu al-As ibn al-Rabi ibn Abd al-Uzza ibn Abd Shams, son-in-law of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), husband of his daughter Zaynab. Abu al-As was among the counted men of Makkah in wealth and trustworthiness, and he belonged to Halah bint Khuwaylid, and Khadijah was his maternal aunt. So Khadijah asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to marry Zaynab to him, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not oppose her, and that was before revelation came to him, and she regarded him as her own son. Then when Allah honored His Prophet with prophethood, Khadijah and his daughters believed in him, affirmed him, bore witness that what he brought is the truth, and followed his religion, while Abu al-As remained upon his polytheism. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had married Utbah ibn Abi Lahab to one of his two daughters, Ruqayyah or Umm Kulthum. Then when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) openly confronted Quraysh by Allah's command and they confronted him, they said: You have relieved Muhammad of his concern, so return his daughters to him and preoccupy him with them. So they walked to Abu al-As ibn al-Rabi and said: Part from your wife, and we shall marry you to any woman you wish. So he said: No, by Allah, I shall not part from my wife, nor would I love to have for my wife any woman of Quraysh in exchange. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would praise him well concerning his son-in-lawship, as has reached me. So they walked to the wicked Utbah ibn Abi Lahab and said: Divorce the daughter of Muhammad's wife and we shall marry you to any woman you wish of Quraysh. So he said: If you marry me to the daughter of Aban ibn Said ibn al-As, or the daughter of Said ibn al-As, I shall part from her. So they married him to the daughter of Said ibn al-As, and he parted from her, while the enemy of Allah had not consummated with her. So Allah removed her from his hand as an honor for her and disgrace for him, and Uthman ibn Affan succeeded him with her afterwards. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in Makkah neither permitted nor prohibited, overpowered in his affair. Islam had separated Zaynab bint the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when she embraced Islam from Abu al-As ibn al-Rabi, except that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) could not separate them. So she remained with him upon her Islam while he was upon his polytheism, until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) emigrated to Madinah, while she remained with him in Makkah. Then when Quraysh marched to Badr, Abu al-As ibn al-Rabi marched among them and was taken among the captives on the day of Badr, and he was in Madinah with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Ibn Ishaq said: Yahya ibn Abbad ibn Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr narrated to me, from his father Abbad ibn Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, from Aishah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), she said: When the people of Makkah sent concerning the ransom of their captives, Zaynab bint the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent concerning the ransom of Abu al-As, sending therein a necklace of hers which Khadijah had given her to wear with Abu al-As when he built with her. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw it, he felt intense tenderness for her, and said: If you see fit to release her captive to her and return her property to her, then do so. So they said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. So they released him and returned to her what was hers. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had taken upon him and he promised that he would let Zaynab go to him, as part of what he stipulated upon him in his release, though that did not appear from him nor from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to be known. When Abu al-As went to Makkah and was set free, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Zayd ibn Harithah and a man of the Ansar in his place, saying: Be at the belly of Yajij until Zaynab passes by you, then accompany her until you bring her to me. So they went to their place, and that was a month or so after Badr. When Abu al-As arrived in Makkah, he bade her join her father, so she left openly. Ibn Ishaq said: Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm said: I was told from Zaynab that she said: While I was preparing in Makkah to join my father, Hind bint Utbah met me and said: O daughter of my uncle, if you need any goods to ease your journey, or wealth to reach your father with, then your need is with me, so do not withhold from me, for what enters between women does not enter between men. She said: By Allah, I do not think she said that except to do so, but I feared her and denied that I intended that. So I prepared, and when I finished my preparation, my brother-in-law Kinanah ibn al-Rabi, brother of my husband, brought me a camel and I rode it, and he took his bow and quiver, then went out with her by day leading her, while she was in her litter. The men of Quraysh spoke of that, so they went in pursuit of her until they caught her at Dhi Tuwa. The first to reach her were Habbar ibn al-Aswad ibn al-Muttalib ibn Asad ibn Abd al-Uzza ibn Qusayy, and Nafi ibn Abd Qays al-Zuhri. So Habbar frightened her with the spear while she was in her litter, and the woman was pregnant as they claim, so when she was frightened she cast what was in her womb. Then her brother-in-law alighted and scattered his arrows, saying: By Allah, no man shall approach me except that I put an arrow in him. So people drew back from him. Then Abu Sufyan came among the notables of Quraysh and said: O man, withhold your arrows from us that we may speak to you. So he withheld, and Abu Sufyan came to him saying: You have done wrong; you went out with a woman openly before the people, while you know our calamity and setback and what has befallen us from Muhammad. So people will think, when his daughter goes out to him openly from among us, that it is from humiliation that befell us from our calamity, and that it is weakness and feebleness from us. By my life, we have no need to withhold her from her father, but return the woman until the noise subsides and people say we have sent her back, then slip her out secretly and join her to her father. He said: So he did so. She remained some nights until people calmed, then he went out with her by night until he handed her to Zayd ibn Harithah and his companion, and they brought her to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Abu al-As remained in Makkah, while Zaynab was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Islam having separated them, until shortly before the Conquest, when Abu al-As went out trading to Sham. He was a trusted man with his own wealth and wealths of men of Quraysh entrusted with him. When he finished his trade he came returning, and a detachment of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) met him and took what was with him, while he escaped them fleeing. When the detachment arrived with what they had taken of his wealth, Abu al-As ibn al-Rabi came under the night until he entered upon Zaynab bint the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and sought refuge with her, so she granted him refuge. He had come seeking his wealth. Then when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out to dawn prayer, as Yazid ibn Ruman narrated to me, and he said takbir and people said takbir, Zaynab came out from the women's gallery saying: O people, I have granted protection to Abu al-As ibn al-Rabi. Then when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished the prayer, he turned to the people and said: O people, did you hear? They said: Yes. He said: By Him in whose hand is the soul of Muhammad, I knew nothing of this until I heard it just now. And the lowest of the Muslims grants protection on their behalf. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) left until he entered upon his daughter, saying: O my daughter, honor his stay, but let him not be alone with you, for you are not lawful to him. Ibn Ishaq said: Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent to the detachment who had taken the wealth of Abu al-As ibn al-Rabi: This man is of us as you know, and you have taken wealth of his. If you do good and return to him what is his, we would love that; and if you refuse, it is Allah's spoil which He has bestowed upon you, and you have most right to it. They said: O Messenger of Allah, rather we shall return it. So they returned his wealth to him, until a man would bring the rope, and a man would bring the water-skin and the inkwell, until one of them would bring the tent-peg, until when they returned his wealth to him entirely, missing nothing of it, he carried to Makkah and returned to every owner of wealth of Quraysh his wealth from those who had entrusted with him. Then he said: O company of Quraysh, does any of you have wealth with me he has not taken? They said: No, and may Allah reward you well, for we found you chaste and noble. He said: I bear witness that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is His servant and Messenger. By Allah, nothing kept me from Islam with him except fear that you would think I only sought to consume your wealth. Now that Allah has restored it to you and I am free of it, I embrace Islam. And he went out until he came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"20544","1051 - Yahya ibn Ayyub al-Allaf al-Misri narrated to us, Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated, Yahya ibn Ayyub informed, Yazid ibn Abdullah ibn al-Had narrated, Umar ibn Abdullah ibn Urwah ibn al-Zubayr narrated to me, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Aishah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to Madinah, his daughter Zaynab left Makkah with Kinanah, or Ibn Kinanah. So they went out after her, and Habbar ibn al-Aswad caught her and kept thrusting her camel with his spear until he threw her down. She cast what was in her womb and bled, though she endured. Banu Hashim and Banu Umayyah disputed over her. Banu Umayyah said: We have most right to her, for she was under their son Abu al-As. She was with Hind bint Utbah ibn Rabiah, and Hind would say to her: This is because of your father. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Zayd ibn Harithah: Will you not go and bring Zaynab? He said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Take my ring and give it to her. So Zayd went and kept using subtlety until he met a shepherd. He said: For whom do you graze? He said: For Abu al-As. He said: Whose are these sheep? He said: Zaynab bint Muhammad's. So he walked with him a while, then said: Will you take something to give her without mentioning it to anyone? He said: Yes. So he gave him the ring. The shepherd went, brought in his sheep and gave her the ring, and she recognized it. So she said: Who gave you this? He said: A man. She said: Where did you leave him? He said: In such-and-such place. So she remained silent until night came, then went out to him. When she came to him, he said to her: Ride before me upon his camel. She said: No, but ride you before me. So he rode and she rode behind him until she arrived. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say: She is the best of my daughters; she was afflicted for my sake. That reached Ali ibn Husayn, so he went to Urwah and said: What hadith has reached me from you that you narrate, detracting from the right of Fatimah? So Urwah said: By Allah, I would not love to have what is between the east and west if I were to detract from Fatimah a right that is hers. As for after that, I shall never narrate it again."
"20545","1052 - Ahmad ibn al-Mualla al-Dimashqi narrated to us, Hisham ibn Ammar narrated, al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated, Abd al-Ghaffar ibn Ismail ibn Ubaydullah narrated, al-Walid ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Harashi narrated, al-Harith ibn al-Harith al-Ghamidi narrated, he said: I said to my father: What is this gathering? He said: These are people gathered around a Sabian of theirs. He said: So I looked, and there was the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) calling people to the unity of Allah and faith in Him until midday rose and people dispersed from him. Then a woman whose upper chest was visible came carrying a cup and a cloth. He took from her, drank and performed ablution, then raised his head to her and said: O my daughter, cover your upper chest and fear neither defeat nor humiliation for your father. So I said: Who is this? They said: This is Zaynab bint the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"20546","1053 - Muhammad ibn Muadh al-Halabi narrated to us, Musa ibn Ismail narrated, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father: That a man came forward with Zaynab bint the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and two men of Quraysh caught him and fought him until they overcame him over her. So they pushed her and she fell upon a rock, miscarried and bled. They took her to Abu Sufyan, then the women of Banu Hashim came to him and he handed her to them. Then she came afterwards emigrating, and remained in pain until she died of that pain. So they used to regard her as a martyr."
"20547","1054 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Imran ibn Abi al-Ratil narrated, Habib ibn Khalid al-Asadi narrated, from al-Amash, from Abdullah ibn al-Mughirah, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: Zaynab bint the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died, so we went out with him. We saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) anxious, intensely grieved, so we said: We shall not speak to him. Until we reached the grave, and he had not finished its niche. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sat and we sat around him. He spoke to himself a while and kept looking to the sky. Then the grave was finished, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) descended into it. I saw him growing in grief, then he finished and came out, and I saw relief come over him and he smiled. So we said: O Messenger of Allah, we saw you anxious and grieved such that we could not speak to you, then we saw relief come over you; what was that? He said: I was remembering the narrowness of the grave and its distress, and the frailty of Zaynab, and that was hard upon me. So I supplicated Allah to lighten it for her, and He did. She was indeed pressed with a pressing heard by all between the east and west, except jinn and men."
"20548","1055 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated, Ishaq ibn Sulayman al-Razi narrated, from Zakariyya ibn Sallam, from Said ibn Masruq, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: When Zaynab bint the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died, grief appeared from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then relief came over him. So we said: O Messenger of Allah, we saw you grieved, then relief came over you. So he said: I saw Zaynab and her frailty, and that was made light upon her and upon me. She was indeed pressed with a pressing that reached the east and west."
"20549","Ruqayyah bint the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed away in his lifetime, peace be upon him. 1056 - Muhammad ibn Jafar ibn Ayan al-Baghdadi narrated to us, Abu al-Ashath Ahmad ibn al-Miqdam narrated, Zuhayr ibn al-Ala al-Absi narrated, from Said ibn Abi Arubah, from Qatadah ibn Diamah, he said: Ruqayyah was with Utbah ibn Abi Lahab. Then when Allah, blessed and exalted, revealed: {Perish the hands of Abu Lahab}, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked Utbah for the divorce of Ruqayyah, and Ruqayyah asked him that, so he divorced her. Then Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, married Ruqayyah, and she died with him."
"20550","1057 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, al-Zubayr ibn Bakkar narrated, he said: Ruqayyah bint the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was with Utbah ibn Abi Lahab, then he parted from her. Then Uthman ibn Affan married Ruqayyah in Makkah, and she emigrated with him to the land of Abyssinia, and bore him Abdullah, by whom he was known. She came with him to Madinah. He stayed back from Badr over her, with the permission of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) struck for him along with the shares of the people of Badr. He said: And my reward, O Messenger of Allah? He said: And your reward."
"20551","1058 - Abu Usamah Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Usamah al-Halabi narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Abi Mani narrated, my grandfather narrated, from al-Zuhri, he said: Ruqayyah died on the day Zayd ibn Harithah, freedman of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), came with the glad tidings of Badr."
"20552","1059 - al-Hasan ibn Harun ibn Sulayman al-Asbahani narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Musayyibi narrated, Muhammad ibn Fulayh narrated, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, stayed back from Badr over his wife Ruqayyah. He mentioned the story thus in his book."
"20553","Umm Kulthum bint the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died in his lifetime. 1060 - Muhammad ibn Jafar ibn Ayan al-Baghdadi narrated to us, Abu al-Ashath Ahmad ibn al-Miqdam narrated, Zuhayr ibn al-Ala narrated, Said ibn Abi Arubah narrated, from Qatadah ibn Diamah, he said: Utaybah ibn Abi Lahab married Umm Kulthum bint the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), but he did not consummate with her before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was commissioned. Ruqayyah was with his brother Utbah ibn Abi Lahab. Then when Allah, mighty and exalted, revealed: {Perish the hands of Abu Lahab}, Abu Lahab said to his two sons Utaybah and Utbah: My head is forbidden to your heads unless you divorce the two daughters of Muhammad. Their mother, daughter of Harb ibn Umayyah, the carrier of firewood, said: Divorce them, O my sons, for they have become empty burden. So they divorced them. When Utaybah divorced Umm Kulthum, he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after parting from Umm Kulthum, saying: I disbelieve in your religion and I have parted from your daughter; you do not love me nor do I love you. Then he lunged at him and tore the shirt of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was heading toward Sham trading. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I ask Allah to set His dog upon you. So he went out in a caravan of Quraysh until they alighted at a place in Sham called al-Zarqa by night. A lion circled them that night, and Utaybah kept saying: Woe to my mother! By Allah, he will eat me, as Muhammad supplicated against me. He made the son of Abu Kabshah weep while he is in Makkah and I am in Sham. Then the lion pounced upon him from among the people, seized him by the head and crushed it, killing him. Zuhayr ibn al-Ala said: Hisham ibn Urwah narrated to us from his father: That when the lion circled them that night, they turned and slept, placing Utaybah among them. So the lion came stepping over until it seized Utaybah by the head and mangled him. And Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah have mercy on him, succeeded after Ruqayyah with Umm Kulthum, may Allah be pleased with them both."
"20554","1061 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, al-Zubayr ibn Bakkar narrated, he said: Umm Kulthum bint the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was with Utaybah ibn Abi Lahab, whom the lion ate, then he parted from her. When Ruqayyah died with Uthman, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married him to Umm Kulthum, and she died with him, bearing him nothing. And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: If I had ten, I would marry them to you."
"20555","1062 - Abu Usamah Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Usamah al-Halabi narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Abi Mani al-Rusafi narrated, my grandfather narrated, from al-Zuhri, he said: Uthman married Umm Kulthum bint the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and she died with him, bearing him nothing."
"20556","1063 - Abu al-Zinba Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, Abu Marwan Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Khalid al-Uthmani narrated, my father narrated, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad, from his father, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood over the grave of his second daughter who was with Uthman, saying: Is there no widower father, no widower brother, whom Uthman may marry? Had they been ten, I would marry them to Uthman. I married her to him only by revelation from heaven. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) met Uthman at the door of the mosque and said: O Uthman, this is Jibril informing me that Allah, mighty and exalted, has married you to Umm Kulthum upon the like of the dower of Ruqayyah and the like of her companionship."
"20557","1064 - Ubaydullah ibn Muhammad al-Mamari the judge narrated to us, Ismail ibn Abi Uways narrated, my brother narrated to me, from Sulayman ibn Bilal, from Ibn Abi Atiq and Yahya ibn Said, from Ibn Shihab, that Anas ibn Malik informed him that he saw upon Umm Kulthum bint the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a striped silk cloak."
"20558","1065 - Ahmad ibn Sahl ibn Ayyub al-Ahwazi narrated to us, Ali ibn Bahr narrated, Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid narrated, from al-Zubayri, from al-Zuhri, from Anas ibn Malik, that he saw upon Zaynab bint the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a striped cloak of silk. Abu al-Qasim said: Thus Mamar narrated it as upon Zaynab, erring in it; the correct view is Umm Kulthum."
"20559","Umamah bint Abi al-As ibn al-Rabi ibn Abd al-Uzza ibn Abd Shams ibn Abd Manaf, and Umamah is the daughter of Zaynab bint the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 1066 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amir ibn Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr informed me, that Amr ibn Sulaym al-Zuraqi informed him that he heard Abu Qatadah say: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would pray with Umamah bint Zaynab bint the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), daughter of Abu al-As ibn al-Rabi ibn Abd al-Uzza, upon his neck. When he bowed he would put her down, and when he rose from prostration he would take her and return her to his neck."
"20560","1067 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, and Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, al-Qanabi narrated, and Bakr ibn Sahl narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated, all from Malik ibn Anas, from Amir ibn Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, from Amr ibn Sulaym, from Abu Qatadah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed carrying Umamah bint Abi al-As ibn al-Rabi. When he bowed he would put her down, and when he stood he would carry her."
"20561","1068 - Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us, al-Humaydi narrated, Sufyan narrated, from Uthman ibn Abi Sulayman and Ibn Ajlan, both hearing Amir ibn Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr narrate, from Amr ibn Sulaym, from Abu Qatadah, he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) leading people with Umamah bint Abi al-As ibn al-Rabi upon his shoulder. When he bowed he would put her down, and when he rose from prostration he would return her."
"20562","1069 - Muhammad ibn al-Abbas al-Muaddib narrated to us, Surayj ibn al-Numan al-Jawhari narrated, Fulayh ibn Sulayman narrated, Amir ibn Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr narrated, from Amr ibn Sulaym, from Abu Qatadah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would pray for people carrying Umamah bint Abi al-As upon his neck. When he bowed he would put her down, and when he rose he would lift her."
"20563","1070 - Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Musaffa narrated, Muhammad ibn Harb narrated, Muhammad ibn al-Walid al-Zubaydi narrated, from Amir ibn Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, from Amr ibn Sulaym, from Abu Qatadah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out to prayer carrying upon his shoulder Umamah bint Abi al-As ibn al-Rabi, whose mother was Zaynab bint the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). When he bowed he would put her down from his shoulder, and when he finished his prostration he would carry her upon his shoulder. He kept doing that with her until he finished his prayer."
"20564","1071 - Muadh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Musaddad narrated, Yahya ibn Said narrated, from Muhammad ibn Ajlan, from Said ibn Abi Said al-Maqburi and Amir ibn Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, from Amr ibn Sulaym, from Abu Qatadah, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would come out carrying the daughter of Zaynab upon his shoulder while leading people. When he stood he would carry her, and when he bowed he would put her down."
"20565","1072 - Abu Muslim al-Kashshi narrated to us, Abu Asim narrated, from Ibn Ajlan, from al-Maqburi, from Amr ibn Sulaym, from Abu Qatadah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would pray with Umamah bint Abi al-As upon his shoulder. When he bowed he would put her down, and when he stood he would carry her."
"20566","1073 - Muttalib ibn Shuayb al-Azdi narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Salih narrated, al-Layth narrated to me, and Amr ibn Hafs al-Sadusi narrated to us, Asim ibn Ali narrated, al-Layth ibn Sad narrated, from Said al-Maqburi, from Amr ibn Sulaym al-Zuraqi, that he heard Abu Qatadah say: While we were sitting in the mosque, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us carrying Umamah bint Abi al-As upon his shoulder. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed while she was upon his shoulder when he stood, until he finished his prayer."
"20567","1074 - Ahmad ibn Rashdin al-Misri narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Salih narrated, Ibn Wahb narrated, Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Said ibn Abi Hilal, from Ibn al-Maqburi, from Amr ibn Sulaym al-Zuraqi, that he heard Abu Qatadah say: While we were sitting in the mosque awaiting prayer, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us with his daughter's daughter upon his shoulder, Umamah bint Abi al-As ibn al-Rabi, whose mother was Zaynab, carrying her upon his shoulder, saying takbir, while she was upon his shoulder. When he bowed he would put her down upon the ground, and when he stood he would return her to his shoulder, until he finished his prayer doing that."
"20568","1075 - Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Yahya ibn Khalaf Abu Salamah al-Jawbari narrated, Abd al-Ala narrated, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Said al-Maqburi, from Amr ibn Sulaym al-Zuraqi, he said: I heard Abu Qatadah al-Ansari say: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) praying with people, with Umamah bint Abi al-As upon his shoulder. When he prostrated he would put her down."
"20569","1076 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, al-Zubayr ibn Bakkar narrated, Abd al-Jabbar ibn Said narrated to me, from Sulayman ibn Muhammad, from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Sabrah, from Abd al-Hamid ibn Jafar, from Said al-Maqburi, from Amr ibn Sulaym, from Abu Qatadah, he said: While we were sitting in the mosque awaiting prayer, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us with Umamah bint Abi al-As ibn al-Rabi upon his shoulder, whose mother was Zaynab bint the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). When he bowed he would put her down upon the ground, and when he stood he would return her to his shoulder, until he finished his prayer doing that with her."
"20570","1077 - Mahmud ibn Muhammad al-Wasiti narrated to us, Wahb ibn Baqiyyah narrated, Khalid informed, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ishaq, from Zayd ibn Abi al-Attab, from Amr ibn Sulaym, from Abu Qatadah, he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) praying with his daughter's daughter, Umaymah or Umamah bint Abi al-As, with him. He would carry her when he stood, and put her down when he sat, until he finished his prayer."
"20571","1078 - Hakim ibn Yahya al-Mattuti narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Ali al-Wasiti narrated, Abdullah ibn Jafar narrated, Said ibn Amr ibn Sulaym al-Zuraqi informed me, from his father, from Abu Qatadah ibn Ribi, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would pray carrying Umamah bint Zaynab bint the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), daughter of Abu al-As ibn al-Rabi. When he bowed he would put her down, and when he stood he would carry her, until he finished his prayer."
"20572","1079 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, al-Zubayr ibn Bakkar narrated, Abd al-Jabbar ibn Said al-Masahqi narrated, from Sulayman ibn Muhammad, from Abu Bakr ibn Abdullah, from Ibn Jurayj, from Zayd ibn Abi al-Attab, from Amr ibn Sulaym al-Zuraqi, that the prayer which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed carrying Umamah was the dawn prayer."
"20573","1080 - Masadah ibn Sad al-Attar al-Makki and Ahmad ibn Abd al-Aziz al-Makki narrated to us, both saying: Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir al-Hizami narrated, Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Urwah narrated, Hisham ibn Urwah narrated, from his father, from Aishah, she said: A necklace of onyx inlaid with gold was gifted to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), while his wives were gathered in a house, all of them, and Umamah bint Abi al-As ibn al-Rabi was a small girl playing on one side of the house with dust. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: How do you see this? So we looked at it and said: O Messenger of Allah, we have never seen anything more beautiful nor more wonderful than this. So he said: Return it to me. When he took it, he said: By Allah, I shall place it upon the neck of the dearest of the household to me. Aishah said: The earth darkened upon me between me and him, fearing he would place it upon the neck of another than me among them. I see them as afflicted with the like of what afflicted me, and we all fell silent in gloom. So he came forward with it until he placed it upon the neck of Umamah bint Abi al-As, and relief came over us."
"20574","1081 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, al-Zubayr ibn Bakkar narrated, he said: Abu al-As ibn al-Rabi entrusted his daughter Umamah and his estate to al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam. So al-Zubayr married her to Ali ibn Abi Talib after the death of Fatimah bint the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Fatimah, may Allah be pleased with her, having enjoined that upon him. Ali ibn Abi Talib was killed while Umamah bint Abi al-As was with him, and she bore him nothing. Umm al-Haytham al-Nakhaiyyah said concerning that:"
"20575","1082 - Abu Yazid al-Qaratisi narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim al-Azraq narrated, Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: Abu al-Hayyaj ibn Abi Sufyan ibn al-Harith ibn Abd al-Muttalib succeeded with Umamah bint Abi al-As ibn al-Rabi, whose mother was Zaynab bint the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"20576","1083 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, al-Zubayr ibn Bakkar narrated, Muhammad ibn Hasan narrated to me, Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Mughirah, he said: Umamah bint Abi al-As, whose mother was Zaynab bint the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), was with Ali ibn Abi Talib. When he passed away from her, he said to her: Do not marry; if you wish to marry, do not go beyond the counsel of al-Mughirah ibn Nawfal. So Muawiyah ibn Abi Sufyan proposed to her, and she came to al-Mughirah seeking his counsel. So he said to her: I am better for you than him, so entrust your affair to me. So she did, and he called men and married her. So Umamah bint Abi al-As died with al-Mughirah ibn Nawfal, bearing him nothing. So Zaynab has no descendants."
"20577","Mention of the wives of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Among them Khadijah bint Khuwaylid ibn Asad ibn Abd al-Uzza ibn Qusayy, and she was the first woman the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married. She is the mother of his male and female children, except Ibrahim, peace be upon him, for he is from his concubine Mariyah the Copt. 1084 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated, he said: Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, he said: Khadijah bint Khuwaylid ibn Asad ibn Abd al-Uzza ibn Qusayy."
"20578","Mention of the marriage of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Khadijah, her age, her death, and some of her reports. 1085 - Yusuf the judge narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Harb narrated, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated, from Ammar ibn Abi Ammar, from Ibn Abbas, as Hammad reckons: That the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned Khadijah bint Khuwaylid. Her father wished to marry her to him. She prepared food and drink and invited her father and a group of Quraysh. They ate and drank until intoxicated. So Khadijah said to her father: Muhammad ibn Abdullah proposes to me; stand and marry me to him. So he married her to him. Then she perfumed him and dressed him in a robe, as they used to do with fathers when they married their daughters. Then when intoxication left him, he looked, and there he was perfumed and wearing a robe. So he said: What is my affair? He said: So she said: You have married me to Muhammad. So he said: Did I marry you to the orphan of Abu Talib? No, by my life. So Khadijah said: Are you not ashamed? You seek to disgrace yourself, telling Quraysh and people that you were drunk. She kept at him until he was pleased."
"20579","1086 - Muhammad ibn Jafar ibn Ayan al-Baghdadi narrated to us, Abu al-Ashath Ahmad ibn al-Miqdam narrated, Zuhayr ibn al-Ala al-Absi narrated, Said ibn Abi Arubah narrated, from Qatadah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married fifteen women; six of them of Quraysh, one of the allies of Quraysh, seven of the rest of the Arabs, and one of the Children of Israel. He married none of them in pre-Islamic times except one, and none of them bore him children but her. The first he married in pre-Islamic times was Khadijah bint Khuwaylid ibn Asad ibn Abd al-Uzza ibn Qusayy. She was before him with Atiq ibn Aidh ibn Abdullah ibn Umar ibn Makhzum. Then Abu Halah Hind ibn Zurarah ibn Nabbash ibn Habib ibn Surad ibn Salamah ibn Jarwah ibn Usayyid ibn Amr ibn Tamim succeeded with her after Atiq, and she bore him Hind ibn Hind. Zuhayr said: Yunus ibn Ubayd said: Hind passed by Basra traveling and died there, with neither market nor pasture standing that day. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married her after them both, and she bore him in pre-Islamic times Abd Manaf, and bore him in Islam two boys and four girls."
"20580","1087 - al-Qasim ibn Abdullah ibn Muhammad al-Ikhmimi al-Misri narrated to us, my uncle Muhammad ibn Mahdi narrated, Anbasah ibn Khalid ibn Yazid narrated, my uncle Yunus ibn Yazid narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Umamah ibn Sahl, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married in Makkah Khadijah bint Khuwaylid, who was before him with Atiq ibn Aidh al-Makhzumi. Then he married in Makkah Aishah; he married no virgin besides her. Then he married Hafsah bint Umar, who was before him under Khunays ibn Hudhafah al-Sahmi. Then he married Sawdah bint Zamah, who was before him under al-Sakan ibn Amr, brother of Banu Amir ibn Luayy. Then he married Umm Habibah bint Abi Sufyan, who was before him with Ubaydullah ibn Jahsh al-Asadi of Asad Khuzaymah. Then he married Umm Salamah bint Abi Umayyah, whose name was Hind, who was before him under Abu Salamah Abdullah ibn Abd al-Asad ibn Abd al-Uzza. Then he married Zaynab bint Jahsh, who was before him under Zayd ibn Harithah. Then he married Maymunah bint al-Harith, and took captive Juwayriyah bint al-Harith ibn Abi Dirar of Banu al-Mustaliq of Khuzaah in his expedition in which he demolished Manat of al-Muraysi. And took captive Safiyyah bint Huyayy ibn Akhtab of Banu al-Nadir; she was of what Allah bestowed upon him, and he allotted for her. And took Rayhanah of Banu Qurayzah as concubine, then freed her and she joined her family, veiling while with her family. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) divorced al-Aliyah bint Zibyan, parted from the sister of Banu Amr ibn Kilab, and parted from the sister of Banu al-Jawn al-Kindiyyah on account of whiteness in her. Zaynab bint Khuzaymah al-Hilaliyyah died while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was alive. It has reached us that al-Aliyah bint Zibyan married before Allah forbade his wives, wedding a cousin of hers of her people, and bearing among them."
"20581","1088 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Mamar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, he said: The first woman the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married was Khadijah. Then he married Sawdah bint Zamah. Then he wed Aishah in Makkah and consummated with her in Madinah. He wed in Madinah Zaynab bint Khuzaymah al-Hilaliyyah. Then he wed Umm Salamah. Then he wed Juwayriyah bint al-Harith; she was of what Allah bestowed upon him. Then he wed Maymunah bint al-Harith, she who gave herself to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then he wed Safiyyah bint Huyayy; she was of what Allah bestowed upon him on the day of Khaybar. Then he wed Zaynab bint Jahsh; she was the wife of Zayd ibn Harithah. Zaynab bint Khuzaymah died with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Khadijah also died in Makkah. He wed a woman of Banu Kilab of Banu Rabiah called al-Aliyah bint Zibyan, then divorced her when she was brought to him."
"20582","1089 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, from Abd al-Razzaq, from Mamar, from al-Zuhri, that the wives of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) were: Khadijah bint Khuwaylid, Aishah bint Abi Bakr, Umm Salamah bint Abi Umayyah, Hafsah bint Umar, Umm Habibah bint Abi Sufyan, Juwayriyah bint al-Harith, Maymunah bint al-Harith, Zaynab bint Jahsh, Sawdah bint Zamah, and Safiyyah bint Huyayy, until nine gathered with him after Khadijah; and the Kindiyyah of Banu al-Jawn, al-Aliyah bint Zibyan of Banu Amir of Banu Kilab, and Zaynab bint Khuzaymah, a woman of Banu Hilal."
"20583","Al-Zuhri said: Urwah ibn al-Zubayr informed me, he said: When the Kindiyyah was brought to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), she said: I seek refuge in Allah from you. So he said: You have sought refuge in a Mighty One; join your family."
"20584","1090 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, Abd al-Razzaq informed, Ibn Jurayj informed, he said: Ibn Abi Mulaykah and Amr ibn Dinar said: Nine women gathered with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), along with Safiyyah, after Khadijah; he died leaving all of them. He said: Uthman ibn Abi Sulayman added two women besides the nine, of Banu Amir ibn Sasaah, both of whom he consummated with. One of them was called Umm al-Masakin, and she was the best of his wives to the poor. He wed a woman of Banu al-Jawn, and when she came to him she sought refuge from him, so he divorced her. He wed a woman of Kindah but did not consummate with her. She married after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so Umar separated them and struck her husband. So she said: Fear Allah, O Umar; if I am of the Mothers of the Believers, then cast the veil upon me and give me what you gave them. So he said: As for that, no. She said: Then let me marry. He said: No, nor any favor, and I shall make none aspire to that."
"20585","1091 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, al-Zubayr ibn Bakkar narrated, he said: The mother of the sons and daughters of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), except Ibrahim, was Khadijah bint Khuwaylid. She was called in pre-Islamic times al-Tahirah bint Asad ibn Abd al-Uzza ibn Qusayy. Her mother was Fatimah bint Zaidah bint Jundub, that is al-Asamm ibn Sakhr ibn Abd Mays ibn Amir ibn Luayy. Her mother was Halah bint Abd Manaf ibn al-Harith ibn Munqidh ibn Amr ibn Mays ibn Amr ibn Luayy. Her mother was al-Ariqah, whose name was Qilabah bint Sad ibn Sahm ibn Amr ibn Husays ibn Kab ibn Luayy. Hibban ibn Abd Manaf, brother of Halah by her father and mother, is he who shot Sad ibn Muadh, may Allah have mercy on him, on the day of the Trench, saying: Take it, and I am Ibn al-Ariqah. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: May Allah scorch your face in the Fire. So it struck the medial vein of Sad; may Allah have mercy on Sad, and he died a martyr. Khadijah bint Khuwaylid was before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with Atiq ibn Aidh ibn Abdullah ibn Amr ibn Makhzum, and she bore him Hind ibn Atiq. Then Abu Halah Malik ibn Nabbash ibn Zurarah ibn Wafdan ibn Habib ibn Salamah ibn Adi of Banu Usayyid ibn Amr ibn Tamim, ally of Banu Abd al-Dar ibn Qusayy, succeeded with her, and she bore him Hind ibn Abi Halah. So Hind ibn Atiq, and Hind and Halah, sons of Abu Halah Malik ibn Nabbash ibn Zurarah, are brothers of the children of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from Khadijah bint Khuwaylid through their mother."
"20586","1092 - Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, al-Zubayr ibn Bakkar narrated, Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Makhzumi narrated to me, from Usamah ibn Hafs and others, from Yunus, from Ibn Shihab, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married Khadijah in Makkah, and she was the first woman he married. She was before him with Abu Halah al-Tamimi. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married her when he was twenty-one years old, and she died seven years after his mission."
"20587","Al-Zubayr said: Umar ibn Abi Bakr al-Muammali narrated to me that Amr ibn Asad married Khadijah to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married her when he was twenty-five years old, while Quraysh were building the Kabah."
"20588","Al-Zubayr said: Muhammad ibn Hasan narrated to me, from Khalid ibn Ismail, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married when he was thirty-seven years old."
"20589","Al-Zubayr said: Muhammad ibn Hasan narrated to me, from Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Urwah, from his father, he said: Amr ibn Asad said: Muhammad ibn Abdullah proposes to Khadijah bint Khuwaylid; this is a stallion whose nose is never struck."
"20590","Al-Zubayr said: Muhammad ibn Hasan narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Fulayh, from Yazid ibn Iyad, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Khadijah bint Khuwaylid was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) before the Quran descended upon him. Then the Quran descended upon him while she was with him. She was the first to affirm the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and believe in him. She died in Makkah three years before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) left for Madinah."
"20591","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami from Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Numayr from Yunus ibn Bukayr from Ibn Ishaq who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not marry over Khadija until she died."
"20592","Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim from Abd al-Razzaq from Mamar who said: I heard al-Zuhri saying: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not marry over Khadija until she died."
"20593","Narrated Ishaq from Abd al-Razzaq from Mamar from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father who said: Khadija died three years or thereabouts before the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) departure to Medina, and he married Aisha soon after Khadija's death, and he had not married over Khadija until she died."
"20594","Narrated Muhammad ibn Jafar ibn Ayan al-Baghdadi from Abu al-Ashath Ahmad ibn al-Miqdam from Zuhayr ibn al-Ala from Said ibn Abi Aruba from Qatada ibn Diama who said: Khadija died in Mecca three years before the Hijra, and she was the first of women and men to believe in the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he married no one else besides her in the pre-Islamic era, and none bore him children except her."
"20595","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami from Uthman ibn Abi Shayba from Abu Usama from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father who said: Khadija died three years before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) set out."
"20596","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami from Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Numayr from Yunus ibn Bukayr from Muhammad ibn Ishaq who said: Khadija died three years before the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) migration."
"20597","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from al-Zubayr ibn Bakkar from Muhammad ibn Hasan from Usama ibn Hafs from Yunus from Ibn Shihab from Urwa from Aisha who said: Khadija died before prayer was made obligatory."
"20598","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from al-Zubayr ibn Bakkar from Muhammad ibn Hasan from Yala ibn al-Mughira from Ibn Abi Rawwad who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) visited Khadija during the illness in which she died and said to her: It grieves me to see you as I do, O Khadija, yet Allah may place much good in hardship; do you not know that Allah has joined me with you in Paradise together with Maryam bint Imran, Kulthum sister of Musa, and Asiya wife of Firawn? She said: Has Allah done that, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Yes. She said: With blessing and sons."
"20599","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal from Hammad ibn Salama from Humayd from Abd Allah ibn Ubayd ibn Umayr who said: When Khadija died it weighed so heavily on the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he was feared for, until he married Aisha."
"20600","Narrated al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Asfati from Abd al-Aziz ibn al-Khattab from Ali ibn Ghurab from Yusuf ibn Suhayb from Ibn Burayda from his father who said: Khadija was the first to accept Islam with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), along with Ali ibn Abi Talib."
"20601","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami from Muhammad ibn Ubayd al-Muharibi from Said ibn Khuthaym al-Hilali, and narrated al-Husayn ibn Muhammad al-Ramahurmuzi from Ahmad ibn Rashad ibn Khuthaym al-Hilali from his uncle Said ibn Khuthaym from Asad ibn Ubayda al-Bajali from Yahya ibn Afif from his father from his grandfather Afif, brother of al-Ashath ibn Qays by his father, who said: I came to Mecca to buy perfume for my family and stayed with al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib, a merchant. As the sun rose I watched when a young man came and glanced at the sky, then turned toward the Kaba; soon a boy came and stood at his right, then a woman came and stood behind them; the young man said the takbir and they repeated it, he bowed and they bowed, he prostrated and they prostrated. I said: O Abbas, a momentous matter. Al-Abbas said: Momentous indeed. Do you know the young man? I said: No. He said: This is Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Muttalib, my nephew. Do you know the woman? I said: No. He said: This is Khadija bint Khuwaylid ibn Asad ibn Abd al-Uzza, the chief lady of Quraysh, my nephew's wife; and this is Ali ibn Abi Talib, my nephew. This nephew of mine claims that his Lord, Lord of the heavens and the earth, commanded him with this religion; by Allah I know no one on earth upon this religion but these three."
"20602","On the virtues of Khadija (may Allah be pleased with her). Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal and Arim Abu al-Numan, and narrated Ahmad ibn Ali al-Abbar from Ali ibn Uthman al-Lahiqi, who said: Narrated Dawud ibn Abi al-Furat al-Kindi from Ilba ibn Ahmar al-Yashkuri from Ikrima from Ibn Abbas who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) drew four lines on the ground then said: Do you know what these are? They said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: The best women of Paradise are Khadija bint Khuwaylid, Fatima bint Muhammad, Maryam bint Imran, and Asiya bint Muzahim wife of Firawn."
"20603","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from al-Zubayr ibn Bakkar from Muhammad ibn Hasan from Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad from Musa ibn Uqba from Kurayb from Ibn Abbas who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The leading ladies of the women of Paradise are Maryam bint Imran, then Fatima bint Muhammad, then Khadija, then Asiya wife of Firawn."
"20604","Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari from Abd al-Razzaq from Mamar from Qatada from Anas that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Sufficient for you among the women of the worlds are Maryam bint Imran, Khadija bint Khuwaylid, Fatima bint Muhammad, and Asiya wife of Firawn."
"20605","Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari from Abd al-Razzaq from Mamar and Ibn Jurayj from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Abd Allah ibn Jafar that Ali ibn Abi Talib said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: The best of their women is Maryam bint Imran, and the best of their women is Khadija bint Khuwaylid."
"20606","Narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba from Abd Allah ibn Numayr, Abu Usama and Abu Muawiya from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Abd Allah ibn Jafar from Ali who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: The best of their women is Maryam bint Imran, and Khadija bint Khuwaylid."
"20607","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abdus ibn Kamil from Said ibn Yahya ibn Said al-Umawi from his father from Mujalid from al-Shabi from Jabir who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about Khadija bint Khuwaylid; he said: I saw her by a river of the rivers of Paradise, in a house of reed, with no idle talk in it and no toil."
"20608","Narrated al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Asfati from Abd al-Aziz ibn al-Khattab from Isa ibn Muslim al-Hamdani from Maysara ibn Uthman al-Ashjai from Ikrima from Ibn Abbas who said: While the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was seated with Khadija, Jibril (peace be upon him) came to him and said: O Muhammad, convey greetings to Khadija from me and give her glad tidings of a house in Paradise of reed, with no harm in it and no toil."
"20609","Narrated Muhammad ibn Hisham ibn Abi al-Dumayk al-Mustamli from Ahmad ibn Janab al-Massisi from Isa ibn Yunus from al-Amash from Abu Salih from Abu Hurayra who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave Khadija glad tidings of a house in Paradise of reed, with no clamour in it and no toil."
"20610","Narrated Khalaf ibn Amr al-Ukbari from Abu Bakr al-Zuhayri from Amr ibn Asim al-Kilabi from Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad from al-Amash from Abu Salih from Abu Hurayra and Abu Said who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave Khadija glad tidings of a house in Paradise of reed with no clamour in it and no toil."
"20611","Narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba from Muhammad ibn Fudayl from Umara ibn al-Qaqa from Abu Zura ibn Amr ibn Jarir from Abu Hurayra who said: Jibril came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: This is Khadija, coming to you with a vessel containing seasoning, food and drink; when she comes to you convey to her greetings of peace from her Lord, and give her glad tidings of a house in Paradise of reed with no clamour in it and no toil."
"20612","Narrated Bishr ibn Musa from al-Humaydi from Sufyan, and narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba from Yala ibn Ubayd, and narrated Mahmud ibn Muhammad al-Wasiti from Wahb ibn Baqiyya from Khalid ibn Abd Allah, and narrated Ahmad ibn Muhammad al-Shafii from his uncle Ibrahim ibn Muhammad al-Shafii from Isa ibn Yunus, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid from Abd Allah ibn Abi Awfa who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave Khadija glad tidings of a house in Paradise of reed with no clamour in it and no toil."
"20613","Narrated Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Yunus al-Raqqi from Ibn Abi Samina from Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash from al-Shaybani from Ibn Abi Awfa that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Jibril said to me: Give Khadija glad tidings of a house in Paradise of reed with no clamour in it and no toil."
"20614","Narrated al-Qasim ibn al-Layth Abu Salih al-Rasibi from Muhammad ibn Abi Safwan al-Thaqafi, and Wahb ibn Jarir ibn Hazim from his father from Muhammad ibn Ishaq from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Abd Allah ibn Jafar who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I was commanded to give Khadija glad tidings of a house in Paradise of reed with no clamour in it and no toil."
"20615","Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Fadl al-Saqati from Said ibn Sulayman from Mubarak ibn Fadala from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Aisha that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would mention Khadija often, so I said: How often you mention Khadija, an old woman with red gums who perished long ago, while Allah has replaced her for you. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) became angry as I never saw him angry, and said: Allah granted her to me what He granted to none of you. I said: O Messenger of Allah, pardon me, may Allah pardon you; by Allah you shall never hear me mention Khadija after this day with anything you dislike."
"20616","Narrated Masada ibn Sad al-Attar and Ahmad ibn Zayd ibn Harun from Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir al-Hizami from Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Urwa ibn al-Zubayr from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Aisha who said: I was never jealous of any wife of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as I was of Khadija, though she perished three years before he married me, because of what I heard of his mentioning her; and his Lord commanded him to give her glad tidings of a house in Paradise of reed, and he would slaughter a sheep and keep tracing her friends with its portions."
"20617","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Asim ibn Ali from Qays ibn al-Rabi from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Aisha who said: I was never jealous of anyone as I was of Khadija, from what I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mention of her; and she said: Allah commanded him to give her glad tidings of a house in Paradise of reed."
"20618","Narrated Ahmad ibn Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najda al-Hawti from his father from Ismail ibn Ayyash from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Aisha who said: I was never jealous of any of the wives of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as I was of Khadija, because of his frequent mention of her (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"20619","Narrated Bishr ibn Musa from Muhammad ibn Said al-Asbahani, and narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami from Minjab ibn al-Harith, who said: Narrated Ali ibn Mushir from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Aisha who said: Hala bint Khuwaylid, sister of Khadija, asked permission of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and it recalled Khadija's asking permission, so he was gladdened by that and said: O Allah, Hala; and I became jealous, as I was never jealous of anyone as of Khadija. Minjab added in his hadith: Because of his frequent mention of her; and he married me three years after her."
"20620","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami from Abu Kurayb from Abu Muawiya from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Aisha who said: I was never jealous of any woman as I was of Khadija, and that was only because of the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) frequent mention of her."
"20621","Narrated al-Miqdam ibn Dawud from Asad ibn Musa from Mubarak ibn Fadala from Thabit from Anas who said: When something was brought to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he would say: Take it to so-and-so, for she was a friend of Khadija."
"20622","Narrated Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal from Yahya ibn Main from Marwan ibn Muawiya al-Fazari from Wail ibn Dawud from Abd Allah from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Aisha said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned Khadija, he would never tire of praising her and seeking forgiveness for her. One day he mentioned her and jealousy overcame me, until I said: Allah has compensated you with one advanced in age. She said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) angered with an anger that sank into my skin, so I said to myself: O Allah, if You remove from me the anger of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), I shall never mention her ill as long as I live. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw what had befallen me, he said: How did you speak? By Allah, she believed in me when people disbelieved in me, and she affirmed me when people denied me, and she was granted children from me while you were deprived of them from me; and he held it against me morning and evening for a month."
"20623","Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Yahya al-Himmani from Ibn al-Mubarak from Mujalid from al-Shabi from Masruq from Aisha who said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned Khadija, he praised her well, so I said: Why do you mention her when Allah has replaced her for you with better? He said: Allah has not replaced her for me with better than her; she affirmed me when people denied me, and she supported me with her wealth when people withheld from me, and Allah granted me children from her while He did not grant me from others."
"20624","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Said ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Makhzumi from Sufyan ibn Uyayna from Abd al-Wahid ibn Ayman from Ibn Abi Najih from Aisha who said: A woman visited the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and food was brought, and he ate of it and placed portions before her, so I said: O Messenger of Allah, do not overload your hands. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: She used to visit us in Khadija's days, and keeping good covenant, or preserving covenant, is part of faith. And when he mentioned Khadija, what seizes women of jealousy seized me, so I said: O Messenger of Allah, Allah has replaced for you one advanced in age with one young in age; so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) became angry, then said: What fault of mine is it that Allah granted her children from me and did not grant you? I said: By Him who sent you with the truth, I shall never mention her after this except with good."
"20625","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal from Hammad ibn Salama from Thabit al-Bunani from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla that Jibril was with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when Khadija came, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Jibril, this is Khadija; and Jibril (peace be upon him) said: Convey to her greetings of peace from Allah and from me."
"20626","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from al-Zubayr ibn Bakkar from Muhammad ibn Hasan from Ibrahim ibn Said ibn Kathir from his father who said: Jibril came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was at Hira and said: This is Khadija, come with hays in her waterskin; tell her that Allah conveys greetings of peace to her. When she came, he told her: Jibril informed me of you and of the hays in your waterskin before you came, and said: Allah conveys greetings of peace to her. She said: He is al-Salam, and from Him is peace, and upon Jibril be peace."
"20627","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal from Hammad ibn Salama from Ammar ibn Abi Ammar from Ibn Abbas, as Hammad reckoned, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Khadija: I hear a voice and see a light, and I fear there may be madness in me. Khadija said: Allah would never do that to you, O son of Abd Allah. Then she went to Waraqa ibn Nawfal and mentioned that to him, and he said: If he is truthful, this is a Namus like the Namus of Musa; if he is sent while I am alive, I shall strengthen him, aid him and support him."
"20628","What Khadija transmitted. Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami from Ahmad ibn Asad al-Bajali from Sahl ibn Ziyad from al-Azraq ibn Qays from Abd Allah ibn al-Harith from Khadija who said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, where are my children from you? He said: In Paradise. I said: Without deeds? He said: Allah knows best what they would have done. I said: Where are my children before you? He said: In the Fire. I said: Without deeds? He said: Allah knew what they would have done."
"20629","Aisha bint Abi Bakr al-Siddiq, wife of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave her the kunya Umm Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr; and the mother of Aisha and her brother Abd al-Rahman was Umm Ruman bint Amir ibn Uwaymir ibn Abd Shams ibn Abd Manaf ibn Udhayna ibn Subay ibn Ruhman ibn al-Harith ibn Abd ibn Malik ibn Kinana. Narrated with this lineage by Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from al-Zubayr ibn Bakkar who said: Musab ibn Abd Allah narrated it to me."
"20630","Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari from Abd al-Razzaq from Mamar from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father who said: Khadija died three years, or thereabouts, before the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) departure to Medina, and he married Aisha soon after Khadija's death."
"20631","Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari from Abd al-Razzaq from Mamar from al-Zuhri from Urwa who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married Aisha when she was six, and she was consigned to him when she was nine, with her dolls with her, and he died leaving her when she was eighteen."
"20632","Narrated Ishaq from Abd al-Razzaq from Mamar from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father, the like of it."
"20633","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami from Yahya ibn Hassan al-Kufi from Sufyan ibn Uyayna from Hisham ibn Urwa who said: Aisha died in the year fifty-seven."
"20634","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami from Minjab ibn al-Harith from Ali ibn Mushir from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Aisha that she enjoined Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr, saying: Do not bury me with them; bury me with my companions at al-Baqi, never to be eulogized."
"20635","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami from Jumhur ibn Mansur from Sayf ibn Muhammad from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Aisha who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave me the kunya Umm Abd Allah, though I never had any child at all."
"20636","Narrated Ishaq from Abd al-Razzaq from Mamar from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father that Aisha said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): All your women have a kunya but me; so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: Take the kunya Umm Abd Allah; and she was called Umm Abd Allah until she died, never having given birth at all."
"20637","Narrated Abu Muslim al-Kashshi from Sulayman ibn al-Faraj al-Hashimi from Abu Usama from Hisham ibn Urwa from Abbad ibn Hamza ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr from Aisha that she said: O Messenger of Allah, will you not give me a kunya, when all your women have a kunya? He said: Yes, take a kunya by your son Abd Allah; and she was called Umm Abd Allah."
"20638","Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Abbas al-Muaddib from Affan ibn Muslim from Wuhayb ibn Khalid from Hisham ibn Urwa from Abbad ibn Hamza from Aisha, the like of it."
"20639","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami from Harun ibn Ishaq from Waki from Hisham ibn Urwa from a man of al-Zubayr's progeny from Aisha who said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, all your women have a kunya but me. He said: Then you are Umm Abd Allah."
"20640","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami from Ubayd ibn Asbat ibn Muhammad from his father from Sufyan from Hisham ibn Urwa from one of his companions who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave Aisha a kunya though no child was born to her."
"20641","Narrated Muhammad ibn Jafar ibn Ayan al-Baghdadi from Abu al-Ashath Ahmad ibn al-Miqdam from Zuhayr ibn al-Ala al-Qaysi from Said ibn Abi Aruba from Qatada who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married Aisha bint Abi Bakr al-Siddiq, and he married no virgin besides her, when she was six years old; and they claimed that Jibril said: This is your wife, before he married her. He married her in Mecca before the Hijra, after Khadija's death, then consummated with her in Medina when she was nine, and he died leaving her when she was eighteen."
"20642","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz and Abu Muslim al-Kashshi from Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal from Hammad ibn Salama from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Aisha that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I was brought a girl in a saraqa of silk after Khadija's death, and behold it was you; so I said: If this is from Allah He will carry it out. Then I was brought another girl in a saraqa of silk, and I uncovered her and behold it was you, so I said: If this is from Allah He will carry it out. Aisha said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married me after Khadija's death before his departure from Mecca, when I was seven or six years old; and when we arrived in Medina, women came to me while I played on a swing with my hair in locks, and they prepared and adorned me, then brought me to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when I was nine."
"20643","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Arim Abu al-Numan from Hammad ibn Zayd from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Aisha who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You were brought to me in a cloth of silk in dreams three nights, and it was said: This is your wife; so I uncovered the cloth and behold it was you, and I say: If this is from Allah He will carry it out."
"20644","Narrated Zakariyya ibn Yahya al-Saji from Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Zabala al-Makhzumi from Ishaq ibn Muhammad al-Farwi from Malik ibn Anas from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Aisha who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I saw you in dreams twice; I see a man carrying you in a saraqa of silk, saying: This is your wife, so uncover her; and behold it is you, so I say: If this is from Allah He will carry it out."
"20645","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami from Muhammad ibn Sahl ibn Askar from Abd al-Razzaq from Mamar from al-Zuhri from Urwa from Aisha, and from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Aisha who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married me when I was seven, and I was consigned to him when I was nine, and he died when I was eighteen."
"20646","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Arim Abu al-Numan from Hammad ibn Zayd from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Aisha who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married me when I was seven, and consummated with me when I was nine."
"20647","Narrated Yahya ibn Ayyub al-Allaf and Abu Yazid al-Qaratisli from Said ibn Abi Maryam from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Aisha who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married me when I was seven, and I was taken to him when I was nine; he came to me while I played with dolls, and I had friends who played with me, and when they were shy of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he would send them to me."
"20648","Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Abbas al-Muaddib from Affan ibn Muslim al-Saffar from Wuhayb ibn Khalid from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Aisha that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married her when she was six, consummated with her when she was nine, and she was with him nine years."
"20649","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami from Hannad ibn al-Sari from Abda ibn Sulayman from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Aisha who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married me when I was six, and consummated with me when I was nine."
"20650","Narrated Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Said ibn Abi Maryam from Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi from Sufyan from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Aisha that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married her when she was six, and she was taken to him when she was nine, and she stayed with him nine years."
"20651","Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Yaqub ibn Humayd ibn Kasib from Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Urwa from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Aisha that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married her before his departure from Mecca by three years, or close to that; he married her when she was six, then consummated with her after his arrival in Medina when she was nine on the day of consummation."
"20652","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami from Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Numayr, Yahya al-Himmani, Hannad ibn al-Sari and Ibrahim ibn Abi Muawiya from Abu Muawiya from al-Amash from Ibrahim from al-Aswad from Aisha that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married her when she was seven, consummated with her when she was nine, and died leaving her when she was eighteen."
"20653","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami from al-Hasan ibn Sahl al-Hannat from Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Asadi from Sufyan from Sad ibn Ibrahim from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad from Aisha who said: I married the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when I was six, and he consummated with me when I was nine."
"20654","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami from Said ibn Amr al-Ashathi from Abthar ibn al-Qasim from Mutarrif from Abu Ishaq from Abu Ubayda from Aisha who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married me at nine, and I accompanied him nine years."
"20655","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami from Yahya al-Himmani from Sharik from Abu Ishaq from Abu Ubayda from Aisha that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married her when she was seven, and consummated with her when she was nine."
"20656","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami from Abu Kurayb from Muawiya ibn Hisham from Sufyan from Abu Ishaq from Abu Ubayda from Aisha who said: I was taken to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when I was nine, and I stayed with him nine years."
"20657","Narrated Hafs ibn Umar al-Raqqi from Qabisa from Sufyan from Abu Ishaq from Abu Ubayda that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married Aisha when she was nine, and she stayed with him nine years."
"20658","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abdus ibn Kamil al-Sarraj from Said ibn Yahya ibn Said al-Umawi from his father from Muhammad ibn Amr from Yahya ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Hatib from Aisha who said: When Khadija died, Khawla bint Hakim ibn Umayya ibn al-Awqas, wife of Uthman ibn Mazun, said in Mecca: O Messenger of Allah, will you not marry? He said: Whom? She said: If you wish a virgin, and if you wish a previously married woman. He said: Who is the virgin? She said: The daughter of the most beloved of Allah's creation to you, Aisha bint Abi Bakr. He said: And who is the previously married? She said: Sawda bint Zama; she believed in you and followed you in what you are upon. He said: Go and mention them to me. So she came and entered Abu Bakr's house and found Umm Ruman, mother of Aisha, and said: O Umm Ruman, what good and blessing has Allah brought upon you? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me to propose Aisha for him. She said: I wish you would wait for Abu Bakr, for he is coming. Abu Bakr came, and she said: O Abu Bakr, what good and blessing has Allah brought upon you? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me to propose Aisha for him. He said: Is she lawful for him when she is but his brother's daughter? So she returned me to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mentioned that to him, and he said: Return to him and tell him: You are my brother in Islam, and I am your brother, and your daughter is lawful for me. So she went to Abu Bakr, and Abu Bakr said to Khawla: Call the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for me; so he came and married him to her, while I was that day six years old."
"20659","Narrated Bishr ibn Musa from al-Humaydi from Sufyan from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Aisha who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married me when I was six, and consummated with me when I was nine."
"20660","Narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba from Abu Muawiya from al-Amash from Ibrahim from al-Aswad from Aisha that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married her when she was nine, and died leaving her when she was eighteen."
"20661","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from al-Zubayr ibn Bakkar from Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Zabala al-Makhzumi from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Aisha who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) migrated, he left us and his daughters behind; when he settled in Medina he sent Zayd ibn Haritha, and sent with him Abu Rafi his freedman, giving them two camels and five hundred dirhams taken from Abu Bakr to buy what they needed of mounts; and Abu Bakr sent with them Abd Allah ibn Urayqit al-Duali with two or three camels, and wrote to Abd Allah ibn Abi Bakr to carry his family: the mother of Abu Bakr, Umm Ruman, me and my brother, and Asma wife of al-Zubayr. They set out escorted until they reached Qudayd; Zayd ibn Haritha bought three camels with those five hundred dirhams, then they all entered Mecca, where they met Talha ibn Ubayd Allah intending migration, so we all set out together; and Zayd and Abu Rafi set out with Fatima, Umm Kulthum and Sawda bint Zama, and Zayd carried Umm Ayman and her son Ayman, and Usama. We travelled together until at al-Bayd of Namir my camel bolted while I was in a litter with my mother in it, and my mother kept crying: O my daughter, O bride, until our camel was caught after descending the pass, the pass of Harsha, and Allah kept us safe. Then we arrived in Medina; I stayed with Abu Bakr's household, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came down to me, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was then building the mosque and chambers around it, and settled his family in them. We stayed there some days, then Abu Bakr said: O Messenger of Allah, what prevents you from consummating with your wife? He said: The dower. So Abu Bakr gave him twelve uqiyya and a nash, and he sent them to us; and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) consummated with me in this house of mine in which I am, in which he died and was buried; and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) settled Sawda bint Zama with him in one of those chambers, and he would stay with her. And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had married me while I played with girls; I was not told that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had married me until my mother took me and confined me in the house, and it occurred to me that I was married; I did not ask her until she herself told me."
"20662","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami from Abd Allah ibn Umar ibn Aban from Abu Usama from al-Ajlah from Ibn Abi Mulayka who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) proposed to Aisha from Abu Bakr, while Abu Bakr had betrothed her to Jubayr ibn Mutim, so he released her from him and married her to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when she was six; he left her three years, then consummated with her when she was nine."
"20663","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from al-Zubayr ibn Bakkar from Muhammad ibn Hasan al-Makhzumi from Abd al-Majid ibn Abd al-Aziz from Uthman ibn Ata from his father from Asma bint Yazid ibn al-Sakan who said: I adorned Aisha for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until I brought her to him; when she entered to him, we were brought a bowl of milk, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) drank from it then handed it to Aisha, but she turned away, so I said: Take from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). She took from it and drank, then handed it to me and I drank, and I kept turning the vessel seeking the spot from which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had drunk; then I handed it to a woman with me, and she said: I do not desire it. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not combine lying and hunger."
"20664","Narrated Bishr ibn Musa from al-Humaydi from Sufyan from Said ibn al-Marzuban from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Aswad from his father from Aisha who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married me while I wore a hawf, and no sooner had he married me than modesty was cast upon me. Sufyan said: Al-hawf is a garment of straps that bedouin dress their children in."
"20665","Narrated Zakariyya ibn Yahya al-Saji from Umar ibn Musa al-Harithi from Ibrahim ibn Sad from Muhammad ibn Ishaq from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Aisha who said: They fattened me with everything but I did not gain weight, then they fattened me with cucumber and fresh dates, and I gained weight."
"20666","Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Layth al-Jawhari from al-Hasan ibn al-Sabbah al-Bazzar from Zayd ibn al-Hubab from Hammad ibn Salama from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Aisha who said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married me, my family fattened me with everything but I did not gain; then they fed me cucumber and fresh dates, and I gained upon them the finest weight."
"20667","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami from Muhammad ibn Bakkar al-Saadi from Ibrahim ibn Sad from his father from Urwa from Aisha who said: My mother treated me with all that women are treated with, but I did not respond to that; so they fed me cucumber and dates when they intended to present me to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and I took to him most finely."
"20668","Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim from Abd al-Razzaq from al-Thawri, and narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Abu Nuaym from Sufyan from Ismail ibn Umayya from Abd Allah ibn Urwa from Aisha who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married me and I was taken to him in Shawwal, so which of the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wives was dearer to him than me? He said: And she liked her women to enter in Shawwal."
"20669","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami from al-Hasan ibn Sahl al-Hannat from Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Asadi, and narrated Zakariyya ibn Yahya al-Saji from Umar ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hasan from Sufyan from Sad ibn Ibrahim from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad from Aisha who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married me when I was six, consummated with me when I was nine, and consummated with me in Shawwal; so which of your women was dearer than me? And she liked her women to enter in Shawwal."
"20670","Narrated Ibrahim ibn Naila al-Asbahani from Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr al-Muqaddami from Abd Allah ibn Arada al-Shaybani from Sulayman ibn Abi Dawud from al-Zuhri from Urwa from Aisha that she said, regarding those who dislike marrying in Shawwal: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married me in Shawwal, and I was taken to him in Shawwal."
"20671","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from al-Zubayr ibn Bakkar from Muhammad ibn Hasan from Usama ibn Hafs from Yunus from Ibn Shihab that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married Aisha bint Abi Bakr in Shawwal, and consummated with her in Medina in Shawwal sixteen months after his migration to Medina; and Aisha died on Tuesday night, seventeen days past of Ramadan after the witr, in the year fifty-eight, and was buried that night."
"20672","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from al-Zubayr ibn Bakkar from Muhammad ibn Hasan from Abd Allah ibn Wahb from Ibn Jurayj from Nafi and others of the people of knowledge who said: We prayed over Aisha and Umm Salama, wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), in the middle of al-Baqi; the imam on the day we prayed over Aisha was Abu Hurayra, and Abd Allah ibn Umar attended; and those who entered Aisha's grave were Abd Allah and Urwa sons of Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr, and Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr; and she died in the year fifty-eight in Ramadan, seventeen days past of it after the witr, and was buried that night."
"20673","Narrated Ibrahim ibn Hashim al-Baghawi from Kathir ibn Yahya from al-Qasim ibn Abd Allah ibn Umar from Abd Allah ibn Dinar from Ibn Umar that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) unveiled Aisha among her family before consummating with her."
"20674","Narrated Abu Muslim al-Kashshi from Sahl ibn Bakkar from Abu Awana from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr from Aisha who said: I was given qualities no woman was given: he took me in marriage when I was six, and the angel came to him with my image in his palm and he looked at it; and he consummated with me when I was nine; and I saw Jibril, whom no other woman saw; and I was the dearest of his wives to him, and my father was the dearest of his companions to him; and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fell ill with his illness with none but me and the angels present."
"20675","Narrated Ishaq ibn Dawud al-Sawwaf al-Tustari from Yahya ibn Ghaylan from Abd Allah ibn Bazigh from Abu Hanifa from Abu Ishaq al-Shaybani from Amir al-Shabi from Masruq from Aisha who said: I was given seven which the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) women were not given: I was among the dearest people to him and my father the dearest people to him; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married me a virgin, and married no virgin besides me; and Jibril would descend upon him with revelation while I was with him in one coverlet, and that was not done for any other; and I had two days and two nights while his women had a day and a night; and verses were revealed in my exoneration over which a multitude of people nearly perished; and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was taken between my breast and my throat."
"20676","Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Sari ibn Sahl al-Qantari from Bishr ibn al-Walid al-Kindi the judge from Umar ibn Hafs from Sulayman al-Shaybani from Ali ibn Zayd from his grandmother from Aisha who said: I was given six which no woman was given after Maryam: Jibril descended with my image in his palm when he commanded the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to marry me; and he married me a virgin, marrying no virgin besides me; and my exoneration descended from heaven; and she mentioned the hadith similarly."
"20677","Narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba from Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman, and narrated Musa ibn Harun from Khalaf ibn Hisham al-Bazzar from Abu Shihab, both from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Dahhak from Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn Zayd ibn Judan from Aisha who said: There are seven traits in me found in no woman except what Allah gave Maryam bint Imran; by Allah I do not say this boasting over any of my companions. Abd Allah ibn Safwan said to her: What are they, O Mother of the Believers? She said: The angel descended with my image; and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married me at seven, and I was consigned to him at nine; and he married me a virgin, with no one of people sharing me with him; and revelation would come to him while he and I were in one coverlet; and I was the dearest people to him, and daughter of the dearest people to him; and verses of the Quran were revealed concerning me, and the community nearly perished over me; and I saw Jibril, whom none of his women saw besides me; and he was taken in my house, attended by none but me and the angel."
"20678","Narrated Abu Muslim al-Kashshi from al-Qanabi from Isa ibn Yunus from Umar ibn Said from Ibn Abi Mulayka that Abu Amr Dhakwan, freedman of Aisha, informed him that Aisha said: Among what Allah bestowed upon me is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was taken in my house, on my day, between my breast and my throat; and Allah joined my saliva with his at death. She said: My brother Abd al-Rahman came to me while I supported the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) against my chest, with a siwak in his hand, and he kept looking at it, so I said: Shall I take it for you? He gestured with his head: Yes. So I softened it for him, then handed it to him, and he passed it over his teeth, with a rakwa, or she said: an ulba, of water before him; he would dip his hand in it then wipe his face with it, saying: There is no god but Allah; death has agonies; then he would pour upon his hand, then say: To the Highest Companion, until he was taken and his hand slumped (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"20679","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Abu Nuaym from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr from Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Mulayka from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad from Aisha who said: Among Allah's favour upon me and His fine blessing with me is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died in my house, on my day, and was taken between my breast and my throat; and my saliva was joined with his on the day he was taken. He said: I said to her: This in your house, on your day, between your breast and throat; how was your saliva joined with his? She said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr came to me on Monday morning with a moist siwak in his hand, and I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fixing his gaze upon it, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was fond of the siwak. So I said: Break it and hand it to me. He broke it in two and handed it to me; I chewed it to soften it, then put it in the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mouth, and he cleaned his teeth with it; and my saliva was joined with his."
"20680","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Ayyub, author of al-Maghazi, from Ibrahim ibn Sad from Muhammad ibn Ishaq from Yaqub ibn Utba from Urwa from Aisha that she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fell ill that day when he returned from the mosque and reclined in my lap; then a man of Abu Bakr's family came to me with a green siwak in his hand. She said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) looked at his hand, and I knew he wanted it, so I said: O Messenger of Allah, would you like me to give you this siwak? He said: Yes. She said: I took it and chewed it for him until I softened it, then gave it to him, and he cleaned his teeth with it as vigorously as I ever saw him clean with a siwak before; then he put it down, and I found the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) growing heavy in my lap, so I looked at his face and his gaze had fixed, while he said: Rather the Highest Companion of Paradise. I said: You were given choice and you chose, by Him who sent you with the truth. She said: And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was taken."
"20681","Narrated Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal from his father from Ibrahim ibn Khalid al-Sanani from Rabah ibn Zayd from Mamar from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Aisha that she said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was taken, his back was supported against me; then Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr entered with a siwak in his hand, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called for it, so I took the siwak and softened it, then handed it to him, and he kept cleaning his teeth with it; then his hand grew heavy and he grew heavy upon me, while he said: O Allah, to the Highest Companion. She said: Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was taken, between my breast and my throat."
"20682","Narrated Amr ibn Abi al-Tahir ibn al-Sarh and Yahya ibn Ayyub al-Allaf, the two Egyptians, from Said ibn Abi Maryam from Nafi ibn Umar al-Jumahi from Ibn Abi Mulayka who said: I heard Aisha saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died on my day, in my house, between my breast and my throat; and Allah joined my saliva with his. She said: Abd al-Rahman came to me with a siwak, and the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) hand weakened, so I took it and chewed it, then cleaned his teeth with it."
"20683","Narrated Muttalib ibn Shuayb al-Azdi from Abd Allah ibn Salih from al-Layth, and narrated Abu al-Zinba Rawh ibn al-Faraj and Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn Rishdin ibn Sad from Yahya ibn Bukayr from al-Layth from Yazid ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Had from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim from his father from Aisha who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died between my sides; so I never dislike the severity of death for anyone ever, after seeing the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) dying with a cup of water beside him, dipping his hand in the cup then wiping his face with the water, then saying: O Allah, help me against the agonies of death."
"20684","On Aisha's seeing Jibril (peace be upon him). Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Dawud ibn Amr al-Dabbi from Ibrahim ibn Sulayman Abu Ismail al-Muaddib from Said ibn Kathir, freedman of Umar ibn al-Khattab, from his father who said: I heard Aisha saying: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) standing with a rider of a white horse holding its forelock; when he came to me I said: Who is the horseman? He said: Did you see him? I said: Yes. He said: Whom does he resemble? I said: Dihya ibn Khalifa al-Kalbi. He said: That is Jibril, and he conveys greetings of peace to you. I said: And upon him who sent him, and upon you and him, be peace."
"20685","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abdus ibn Kamil al-Sarraj from Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Numayr from Rawh ibn Ubada, and narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami from Abd Allah ibn al-Hakam ibn Abi al-Zinad al-Qatawani from Khalid ibn Makhlad from Abd Allah ibn Umar al-Umari from Yahya ibn Said and Ubayd Allah ibn Umar from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad from Aisha who said: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a man on a mule wearing a turban tied behind him, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) placing his hand on the mule's forelock. I said: O Messenger of Allah, who is this? He said: Did you see him? I said: Yes. He said: Who is he? I said: Dihya al-Kalbi. He said: That is Jibril (peace be upon him)."
"20686","Narrated Ibrahim ibn Naila al-Asbahani from Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr al-Muqaddami from Wahb ibn Jarir ibn Hazim from his father from al-Numan ibn Rashid from Abu Salama from Aisha who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: O Aisha, this is Jibril conveying greetings of peace to you. I said: And upon him be peace and Allah's mercy and blessings."
"20687","Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari from Abd al-Razzaq from Mamar from al-Zuhri from Urwa from Aisha that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: This is Jibril, and he conveys greetings of peace to you. I said: And upon him be peace and Allah's mercy and blessings; you see whom we do not see."
"20688","Narrated Muttalib ibn Shuayb al-Azdi from Abd Allah ibn Salih from al-Layth from Abd al-Rahman ibn Khalid ibn Musafir from Ibn Shihab from Abu Salama from Aisha who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This is Jibril conveying greetings of peace to you. I said: And upon him be peace and Allah's mercy and blessings."
"20689","Narrated Harun ibn Kamil al-Misri from Abd Allah ibn Salih from al-Layth ibn Sad from Yunus from Ibn Shihab from Abu Salama from Aisha who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: This is Jibril conveying greetings of peace to you. I said: And upon him be peace and Allah's mercy and blessings."
"20690","Narrated Bishr ibn Musa from al-Humaydi, and narrated Musa ibn Harun from Ubayd Allah ibn Umar al-Qawariri, who said: Narrated Sufyan ibn Uyayna from Mujalid from al-Shabi from Abu Salama from Aisha who said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, I saw you speaking to Dihya al-Kalbi with your hand on the horse's forelock. He said: Did you see him? I said: Yes. He said: That is Jibril, and he conveys greetings of peace to you. I said: And upon him be peace and Allah's mercy and blessings, from an intimate companion; what a fine companion and fine intimate."
"20691","Narrated Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal from his father from Waki ibn al-Jarrah from Zakariyya ibn Abi Zaida from al-Shabi from Abu Salama from Aisha that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Aisha, Jibril conveys greetings of peace to you. I said: And upon him be peace and Allah's mercy and blessings."
"20692","Narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba from Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman from Zakariyya ibn Abi Zaida from al-Shabi from Abu Salama from Aisha that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Aisha: This is Jibril conveying greetings of peace to you; so Aisha said to him: And upon him be peace and Allah's mercy and blessings."
"20693","Narrated al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Asfati from Ismail ibn Abi Uways from Sulayman ibn Bilal from Abd al-Aziz ibn Abd Allah from Muhammad ibn Abd Allah from Aisha that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: Jibril conveys greetings of peace to you. She said: I said: Then to my Lord be peace, and from Him is peace, and upon Jibril be peace."
"20694","Narrated Abu Husayn the judge and al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Yahya al-Himmani from Sharik from Muti al-Ghazzal from al-Shabi from Masruq from Aisha who said: Jibril asked permission of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: Who is this with you? He said: Aisha. He said: Then convey to her greetings of peace from me. He said: Glad tidings, O Aisha, this is Jibril conveying greetings of peace to you."
"20695","Narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba from Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman from Mujalid from al-Shabi from Masruq who said: Aisha informed me, saying: While the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sat in the house, a man on a horse entered upon us; then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rose and returned, so I said: O Messenger of Allah, who is this? He said: This is Jibril, and he commanded me to convey greetings of peace to you. I said: Return greetings to him; to him from me be peace and Allah's mercy and blessings."
"20696","Narrated Zakariyya ibn Yahya al-Saji from Abd al-Wahid ibn Ghiyath from Hammad ibn Salama from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Aisha that when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished with al-Ahzab, he entered a washroom to bathe; then Jibril came and said: O Muhammad, you have laid down your arms, but we have not laid down ours yet; go to Banu Qurayza. Aisha said: O Messenger of Allah, I saw him through the dust with dust binding his head."
"20697","Narrated Musa ibn Harun from Abu Musa Ishaq ibn Musa al-Ansari and Uthman ibn Abi Shayba from Abda ibn Sulayman from Hisham ibn Urwa from Salih ibn Rabi a ibn Hudayr from Aisha who said: Revelation came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while I was with him, so I closed the door between me and him; when it was lifted from him, he said: O Aisha, Jibril (peace be upon him) conveys greetings of peace to you."
"20698","Narrated Bakr ibn Sahl al-Dimyati from Abd Allah ibn Yusuf from Abu Muawiya Muhammad ibn Khazim from Abu Hanifa al-Numan ibn Thabit from Hammad from Ibrahim from al-Aswad from Aisha who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: What lightens death for me is that I was shown you as my wife in Paradise."
"20699","Narrated Musa ibn Harun from Muhammad ibn Bakkar from Yusuf ibn al-Majishun from his father from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd Allah ibn Kab ibn Malik from Aisha who said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, who are your wives in Paradise? He said: You are among them, or he said: Indeed you are among them; so it occurred to me that that was because he married no virgin besides me."
"20700","Narrated Ahmad ibn al-Qasim ibn Musawir al-Jawhari from Yazid ibn Mihran al-Khabbaz from Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash from Abu Husayn from Abu Maryam Abd Allah ibn Ziyad al-Asadi who said: Ammar addressed us and mentioned Aisha, saying: She is the wife of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this world and the Hereafter."
"20701","Narrated Abu Muslim al-Kashshi from Abu al-Rabi al-Zahrani from Abu Muawiya from al-Amash from Shimr ibn Atiyya from Abd Allah ibn Ziyad al-Asadi who said: I heard Ammar ibn Yasir saying: Our mother has gone her course, and she knows she is the wife of our Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this world and the Hereafter; but Allah has tried us, to know whether we obey her or Him."
"20702","Narrated Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal from Muhammad ibn Aban al-Wasiti from Abu Shihab al-Hannat from Amr ibn Qays and Sufyan al-Thawri from Abu Ishaq from Amr ibn Ghalib that a man disparaged Aisha in Ali's presence, so Ammar said to him: Be silent, disgraced and reviled; do you harm the beloved of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)?"
"20703","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami from Ali ibn al-Jad from Zuhayr from Abu Ishaq from Arib ibn Humayd, or Humayd ibn Arib, who said: A man was with Ali and spoke ill of Aisha, so Ammar said to him: Who is this whose Prophet's wife in Paradise you disparage? Be silent, disgraced."
"20704","Narrated Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal from Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr al-Muqaddami from Hammad ibn Zayd from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Aisha who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Umm Salama: O Umm Salama, do not harm me regarding Aisha; for by Allah, revelation never descended upon me in the coverlet of any of your women besides her."
"20705","Narrated Abd Allah ibn Ali ibn al-Jarud al-Naysaburi from Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Naysaburi from Yaqub ibn Ibrahim ibn Sad from his father from Salih ibn Kaysan from al-Zuhri from Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham that Aisha, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), said: The Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wives sent Fatima to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); she asked permission while he reclined with me in my cloak, and he admitted her while I was silent. She said: O Messenger of Allah, they sent me asking you for justice regarding the daughter of Abu Quhafa. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: O my daughter, do you not love me? She said: Yes. He said: Then love her."
"20706","Narrated Yusuf ibn Yaqub the judge from Amr ibn Marzuq from Shuba from Amr ibn Murra from Abu Musa (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Many men have attained perfection, but of women none attained perfection but Maryam bint Imran and Asiya wife of Firawn; and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The excellence of Aisha over women is like the excellence of tharid over all food."
"20707","Narrated Talib ibn Qurra al-Adhani from Muhammad ibn Isa al-Tabba from Abu Sufyan al-Mamari from Shuba from Muawiya ibn Qurra from his father who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The excellence of Aisha over women is like the excellence of tharid over all food."
"20708","Narrated Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal from Shabab al-Usfuri from Ali ibn Muhammad al-Madaini from Ibn Abi Dhib from al-Harith ibn Abd al-Rahman from Abu Salama ibn Abd al-Rahman from his father who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The excellence of Aisha over women is like the excellence of tharid over all food."
"20709","Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Azdi from Muawiya ibn Amr from Zaida from Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Mamar who said: I heard Anas saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The excellence of Aisha over women is like the excellence of tharid over all food."
"20710","Narrated Yahya ibn Ayyub al-Allaf al-Misri from Said ibn Abi Maryam from Muhammad ibn Jafar from Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Mamar ibn Hazm al-Ansari who said: I heard Anas ibn Malik saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would say: The excellence of Aisha over women is like the excellence of tharid over all food."
"20711","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from al-Qanabi from Sulayman ibn Bilal, and narrated Ali ibn al-Mubarak al-Sanani from Ismail ibn Abi Uways from Sulayman ibn Bilal from Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Mamar Abu Tuwala from Anas ibn Malik who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: The excellence of Aisha over women is like the excellence of tharid over food, or the excellence of tharid over food is like the excellence of Aisha over women."
"20712","Narrated Ahmad ibn Yazid al-Sijzi from Yahya ibn Yahya al-Naysaburi from Ismail ibn Ayyash from Abu Tuwala and Yahya ibn Said from Anas ibn Malik who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The excellence of Aisha over women is like the excellence of tharid over food."
"20713","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal from Hammad ibn Salama from Said al-Jurayri from Abd Allah ibn Shaqiq that Amr ibn al-As said: O Messenger of Allah, who is dearest to you among people? He said: Aisha. He said: Among men? He said: Abu Bakr."
"20714","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Mualla ibn Asad al-Ammi from Abd al-Aziz ibn al-Mukhtar from Khalid al-Hadhdha from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi who said: Amr ibn al-As narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent him with the army of Dhat al-Salasil, so I came to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, who is dearest to you among people? He said: Aisha. I said: Among men? He said: Her father. I said: Then whom? He said: Umar. I said: Then whom, and he enumerated men."
"20715","Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Wahb ibn Baqiyya from Khalid ibn Abd Allah from Khalid al-Hadhdha from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi who said: Amr ibn al-As narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent him with an army, and he mentioned the like of it."
"20716","Narrated Muhammad ibn Abdus ibn Kamil al-Sarraj from Said ibn Yahya ibn Said al-Umawi from his father from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid from Qays ibn Abi Hazim from Amr ibn al-As who said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, who is dearest to you among people? He said: And why? I said: That I may love whom you love. He said: Aisha."
"20717","Narrated Muadh ibn al-Muthanna ibn Muadh ibn Muadh al-Anbari from his father from Ibn Awn from Ali ibn Zayd ibn Judan from Umm Muhammad from Aisha from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"20718","Narrated Bakr ibn Sahl from Abd Allah ibn Salih from Musa ibn Ulayy ibn Rabah from his father from Abu Qays, freedman of Amr ibn al-As, from Umm Salama that she said regarding Aisha: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) could not restrain himself for love of her."
"20719","Narrated Ali ibn al-Mubarak al-Sanani from Ismail ibn Abi Uways, and narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Dawud ibn Amr al-Dabbi, and narrated Abu Shuayb Abd Allah ibn al-Hasan al-Harrani from Said ibn Mansur, who said: Narrated Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Aisha who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said one day: By Allah, O Aisha, it is not hidden from me when you are pleased nor when you are angry. I said: By my father and mother, O Messenger of Allah, how do you know that? He said: When you are pleased, you say when you swear: No, by the Lord of Muhammad; and when you are angry, you say: No, by the Lord of Ibrahim. I said: You have spoken truly, O Messenger of Allah."
"20720","Narrated Masada ibn Sad al-Attar al-Makki from Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir al-Hizami from Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Urwa from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Aisha who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: By Allah, it is not hidden from me, O Aisha, when you are pleased and when you are angry. I said: How do you know that, O Messenger of Allah? He said: When you are pleased with me and swear, you say: No, by the Lord of Muhammad; and when you are angry and swear, you say: No, by the Lord of Ibrahim. I said: You have spoken truly; by Him in whose hand is my soul, O Messenger of Allah, I only forsake your name."
"20721","Narrated al-Husayn ibn Jafar al-Qattat al-Kufi from Minjab ibn al-Harith from Ali ibn Mushir from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Aisha who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I know when you are pleased with me and when you are angry. I said: How do you know that, O Messenger of Allah? He said: When you are pleased with me and swear, you say: No, by the Lord of Muhammad; and when you are angry and swear, you say: No, by the Lord of Ibrahim. I said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah, I only forsake your name."
"20722","Narrated Abu Muslim al-Kashshi from Sulayman ibn al-Faraj al-Hashimi from Abu Usama from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Aisha who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: I know when you are pleased with me and when you are angry with me. I said: How do you know that? He said: When you are pleased with me, you say: No, by the Lord of Muhammad; and when you are angry with me, you say: No, by the Lord of Ibrahim. I said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah, I only forsake your name."
"20723","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz and Abu Muslim al-Kashshi from Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal from Hammad ibn Salama from Ali ibn Zayd ibn Judan from Abu Salama from Aisha who said: I raced the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and outran him."
"20724","Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Uthman ibn Abi Shayba from Abu Usama from Hisham ibn Urwa from Abu Salama from Aisha who said: I set out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a journey, and he halted at a stop and said to his companions: Go ahead; then he said: Come, let me race you. So I raced him and outran him. Then I set out with him later on a journey, having gained flesh; we halted at a stop, and he said to his companions: Go ahead; then he said: Come, let me race you. So I raced him and he outran me; then he struck between my shoulders and said: This for that."
"20725","Narrated Bishr ibn Musa from al-Humaydi from Sufyan from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Aisha who said: I raced the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and outran him; then when I carried flesh, he raced me and outran me, and said: O Aisha, this for that."
"20726","Narrated Abu Yazid Yusuf ibn Yazid al-Qaratisli from Said ibn Abi Maryam from Yahya ibn Ayyub from Yahya ibn Said who said: I heard Said ibn al-Musayyib saying: Aisha said to Abu Bakr: I saw three moons fall into my chamber. Abu Bakr said: Three shall be buried in your house, the best of earth's people. Yahya said: I heard people narrating that when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was taken in her house, Abu Bakr said: One of your moons, and the best of them."
"20727","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal from Hammad ibn Salama from Ayyub from Nafi, or Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Aisha that she said: I saw as though three moons fell into my chamber; so Abu Bakr said: If your vision is true, the best of earth's people, three, shall be buried in your house. When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died, Abu Bakr said to her: The best of your moons, O Aisha; and Abu Bakr and Umar were buried in her house."
"20728","Narrated al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Asfati from Musa ibn Abd Allah al-Salafi from Umar ibn Said al-Abahh from Said ibn Abi Aruba from Qatada from al-Hasan from Abu Bakr that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Has any of you seen a vision? So Aisha said: O Messenger of Allah, I saw three moons descend into my chamber. He said to her: If your vision is true, there shall be buried in your house, I think he said: the best of Paradise's people. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was taken, he being the best of her moons; then Abu Bakr was taken, then Umar was taken, and they were buried in her house."
"20729","On the story of tayammum, and that it was revealed on Aisha's account. Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari from Abd al-Razzaq from Malik from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim from his father from Aisha who said: We set out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on one of his journeys, until at al-Bayda or Dhat al-Jaysh my necklace broke; so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stayed seeking it, and the people stayed with him, with no water with them. The people came to Abu Bakr and said: Do you not see what Aisha has done? She has detained the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the people, with no water with them. So he reproached me and said to me what Allah willed him to say, prodding me with his hand in my side, and nothing prevented me from moving but the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) place upon my thigh; and he slept until morning without ablution. So Allah revealed the verse of tayammum, and Usayd ibn Hudayr said: This is not your first blessing, O family of Abu Bakr. Then we sent the camel upon which I was, and found the necklace beneath it."
"20730","Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim from Abd al-Razzaq from Mamar from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father, or another, who said: Aisha's necklace fell, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent men seeking it; morning overtook them with no water with them, so they prayed without purification, and complained of that to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and tayammum was revealed. Mamar said: And Ayyub informed me, saying: Abu Bakr passed by Aisha and said: You have detained people and burdened them. Mamar said: And Hisham said from his father, and Ayyub said it too: when tayammum was revealed, Abu Bakr was gladdened by that and said: I knew you blessed; no matter you disliked befell you but Allah made therein good for Muslims."
"20731","Narrated Yusuf the judge from Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr al-Muqaddami from Humayd ibn al-Aswad from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Aisha that she borrowed a necklace from Asma, and it fell from her neck; she mentioned that to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he sent men seeking it, and they sought it and found it; then prayer came and they prayed without purification, then returned to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mentioned that to him, so Allah revealed the concession. Usayd ibn Hudayr said: May Allah reward you well; by Allah, no matter you disliked ever befell you but Allah made therein good for you and the believers."
"20732","Narrated al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Asfati from Ismail ibn Abi Uways from his brother from Sulayman ibn Bilal from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Aisha that the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wives were two parties: a party with Aisha, Hafsa, Safiyya and Sawda; and a party with Umm Salama and the rest of the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wives."
"20733","On the story of al-ifk, and what Allah revealed of her exoneration. Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari from Abd al-Razzaq from Mamar from al-Zuhri who said: Said ibn al-Musayyib, Urwa ibn al-Zubayr, Alqama ibn Waqqas and Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Utba ibn Masud informed me of the hadith of Aisha, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when the people of al-ifk said of her what they said; and Allah exonerated her. Each narrated to me a portion of her hadith, some more attentive to her hadith and firmer in narration, and I retained from each the hadith he narrated to me, parts of their hadith confirming parts. They mentioned that Aisha, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended to set out on a journey, he cast lots among his wives; whichever one's lot came out, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) set out with her. Aisha said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cast lots among us on a campaign he waged, and my lot came out; so I set out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after Allah had revealed the veil upon us. I was carried in my howdah and taken down in it. We marched until, when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished and we neared Medina, he announced departure one night. I rose when they announced departure and walked past the army; when I had relieved myself, I returned to the camp and felt my neck, and my necklace of Zafar onyx had broken. I returned seeking my necklace, and its search detained me. The party who used to mount me came and carried my howdah, lifting it onto my camel upon which I rode, thinking I was in it; women in those days were light, not heavy with flesh, eating but a morsel of food, so the people did not notice the howdah's lightness, and I was a young girl. They sent the camel and marched; and I found my necklace after the army had gone on. I came to their camp with none calling or answering in it, so I made for my spot in which I had been, thinking they would miss me and return to me. While I sat in my place, sleep overcame me and I slept. Safwan ibn al-Muattal al-Sulami then al-Dhakwani was behind the army; he journeyed by night and reached my place in the morning, seeing the shape of a sleeping person. He came to me and recognized me when he saw me, having seen me before the veil; I awoke at his istirja upon recognizing me, and covered my face with my cloak. He spoke not a word to me, nor did I hear from him a word besides his istirja, until he made his she-camel kneel, trod upon its foreleg, and I mounted it; and he set off leading the mount until we reached the army after they had halted. Then those perished who perished, and the one who took upon himself the chief part of al-ifk was Abd Allah ibn Ubayy ibn Salul. We arrived in Medina, and I fell ill for a month after arriving, with people spreading the words of the people of al-ifk while I sensed nothing of it. What troubled me in my illness was that I did not recognize from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the tenderness I used to see from him when I complained; he would only enter upon me then say: How is she? Then leave. That troubled me, sensing no evil, until I recovered and went out with Umm Mistah toward al-Manasi, our place of relieving, going out only by night, before privies were made near our houses; our practice was the practice of the early Arabs in relieving in the open, and we disliked making privies by our houses. Umm Mistah and I set out, she being the daughter of Abu Ruhm ibn al-Muttalib ibn Abd Manaf, her mother the daughter of Sakhr ibn Amir, grandmother of Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, and her son Mistah ibn Uthatha ibn al-Muttalib. As Umm Mistah and I headed home after relieving ourselves, Umm Mistah stumbled in her cloak and said: Woe to Mistah. I said to her: Evil is what you said, cursing a man who witnessed Badr? She said: O dear, have you not heard what he said? I said: What did he say? She informed me of the words of the people of al-ifk. So my sickness increased upon my sickness. When we returned to my house, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to us, greeted, then said: How is she? I said to him: Will you permit me to go to my parents, then wishing to verify the news from them. She said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted me, and I came to my parents and said to my mother: O mother, what are people saying? She said: O daughter, make light of it; by Allah, seldom is there a beautiful woman with one who loves her, with co-wives, but they speak much against her. She said: I said: Glory be to Allah, have people spoken of this? I spent the night until morning with tears unceasing and no sleep; she said: Then morning came while I wept. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called Ali ibn Abi Talib and Usama ibn Zayd when revelation tarried, consulting them about parting with his wife. As for Usama, he advised the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with what he knew of his family's innocence and what he knew in himself of affection for them; Usama said: O Messenger of Allah, your family, and we know but good. As for Ali, he said: O Messenger of Allah, Allah has not constrained you; women besides her are many, and if you ask the maid she will tell you truly. She said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called Barira and said to her: O Barira, have you seen anything that makes you doubt? Barira said: By Him who sent you with the truth, I saw nothing in her I could fault beyond that she is a young girl who sleeps over her family's dough, and the household goat comes and eats it. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rose that day seeking excuse regarding Abd Allah ibn Ubayy ibn Salul; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said upon the pulpit: O assembly of Muslims, who will excuse me regarding a man whose harm against my household has reached me? By Allah, I know of my family but good, and they mentioned a man of whom I know but good, and he would not enter upon my family but with me. Sa'd ibn Muadh al-Ansari rose and said: O Messenger of Allah, I excuse you regarding him; if he is of al-Aws I strike his neck, and if he is of our brothers al-Khazraj, command us and we carry out your command. Sa'd ibn Ubada rose, then chief of al-Khazraj, previously a righteous man, but zeal carried him; he said to Sa'd ibn Muadh: You lie, by Allah's life you shall not kill him nor can you kill him. Usayd ibn Hudayr rose, cousin of Sa'd ibn Muadh, and said to Sa'd ibn Ubada: You lie, by Allah's life he shall surely kill him, for you are a hypocrite disputing for the hypocrites. The two tribes, al-Aws and al-Khazraj, flared up until they nearly fought, with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upon the pulpit; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kept calming them until they fell silent, and he fell silent. I spent my day with tears unceasing and no sleep, with my parents weeping until I thought weeping would split my liver. She said: While they sat with me and I wept, a woman of the Ansar asked permission; I admitted her and she sat weeping with me. While thus, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to us, greeted, then sat. She said: He had not sat with me since what was said of me was said, having spent a month with nothing revealed to him concerning me. She said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bore witness when he sat, then said: As for what follows, O Aisha, such-and-such has reached me about you; if you are innocent, Allah will exonerate you, and if you committed a sin, seek Allah's forgiveness and repent to Him, for when a servant confesses a sin then repents, Allah turns to him. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished his words, my tears dried so I felt not a drop of them; I said to my father: Answer the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for me regarding what he said. He said: By Allah, I know not what to say to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). She said: I said, a young girl reading little of the Quran: By Allah, I know you have heard this matter until it settled in your souls and you believed it; if I tell you I am innocent of it, and Allah knows I am innocent, you will not believe me in that; and if I confess to you a matter, and Allah knows I am innocent, you will believe me. By Allah, I find no likeness for me and you but as Abu Yusuf said: Beautiful patience. She said: Then I turned and lay upon my bed; by Allah I then knew I was innocent and that Allah would exonerate me with my innocence, but by Allah I never thought revelation to be recited concerning me would descend, my affair being too slight in myself for Allah to speak of me a matter to be recited; but I hoped the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would see in sleep a vision by which Allah would exonerate me. She said: By Allah, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had not left his seat, nor had anyone left the house, before Allah revealed to His Prophet; he was seized with what seized him of heaviness at revelation, until drops like pearls ran from him on a wintry day from the weight of the revelation sent upon him. She said: When it was lifted from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), smiling, the first word he spoke was to say: Rejoice, O Aisha; by Allah, Allah has exonerated you. My mother said to me: Rise to him. I said: By Allah, I shall not rise to him, nor praise but Allah, who revealed my exoneration. She said: Then Allah revealed: Those who brought al-ifk, ten verses; so Allah revealed these verses as my exoneration. She said: Abu Bakr, who spent upon Mistah for kinship and poverty, said: By Allah, I shall never spend upon him anything after what he said of Aisha. So Allah revealed: Let not those of virtue among you, to the verse. Abu Bakr said: By Allah, I love that Allah forgive me; so he restored to Mistah the spending he used to spend upon him, saying: By Allah, I shall never withdraw it from him. Aisha said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had asked Zaynab bint Jahsh, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): What do you know and what did you see? She said: O Messenger of Allah, I guard my hearing and sight; by Allah, I know but good. Aisha said: She was the one who rivalled me among the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wives, but Allah preserved her with piety; and her sister Hamna bint Jahsh took up fighting for her and perished among those who perished. Al-Zuhri said: This is what reached me of the affair of these people."
"20734","Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz and Abu Muslim al-Kashshi from Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal from Abd Allah ibn Umar al-Numayri from Yunus ibn Yazid al-Ayli who said: I heard al-Zuhri saying: I heard Urwa ibn al-Zubayr, Said ibn al-Musayyib, Alqama ibn Waqqas and Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Utba narrating the hadith of Aisha, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when the people of al-ifk said of her what they said, and Allah exonerated her. He said: Each narrated to me a portion of the hadith he narrated to me from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her), parts of their hadith confirming parts, though some retained it better than others. They alleged that Aisha, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended to set out, he cast lots among his wives; whichever one's lot came out, he set out with her. Aisha said: So he cast lots among us on a campaign he waged, and my lot came out; so I set out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after Allah revealed the veil. I alight and am carried in my howdah. We marched until, when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished that campaign of his and we neared Medina returning, he announced departure one night. I rose when he announced departure for my need; when I relieved myself, I returned to my camp and found a necklace of mine of striped onyx broken. I returned to my necklace seeking it, while the party who used to mount me came and lifted my howdah, mounting it upon my camel upon which I rode, thinking me in it; women in those days were light, not laden with flesh, eating but a morsel of food, so the people did not notice the howdah's lightness, and I was a young girl. They sent the camel and marched; and I found my necklace after the army had gone on. I came to their camp with neither caller nor answerer in it, so I made for my spot in which I had been, thinking they would miss me and return to me. While I sat in my place, sleep overcame me and I slept. Safwan ibn al-Muattal al-Sulami then al-Dhakwani was behind the army; he journeyed by night and reached my place in the morning, seeing the shape of a sleeping person. He came to me and recognized me when he saw me, having seen me before the veil; I awoke at his istirja when he recognized me, and covered my face with my cloak. He spoke not a word to me, nor did I hear from him a word besides his istirja, until he made his mount kneel, trod upon its hand, and I mounted it; and he set off leading the mount until we reached the army after they had halted. Then those perished who perished, and the one who took upon himself the chief part of al-ifk was Abd Allah ibn Ubayy ibn Salul. We arrived in Medina, and I fell ill when I arrived for a month, with people spreading the words of the companions of al-ifk while I sensed nothing of it. What troubled me in my pain was that I did not recognize from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the tenderness I used to see from him when I complained; he would only enter upon me then say: How is she? Then leave. That troubled me, sensing no evil, until after I recovered and went out with Umm Mistah toward al-Manasi, our place of relieving, going out only by night, before privies were made near our houses; our practice was the practice of the early Arabs in relieving in the open, and we disliked making privies by our houses. Umm Mistah and I set out, she being the daughter of Abu Ruhm ibn al-Muttalib ibn Abd Manaf, her mother the daughter of Sakhr ibn Amir, grandmother of Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, and her son Mistah ibn Uthatha ibn al-Muttalib. As Umm Mistah and I headed home after relieving ourselves, Umm Mistah stumbled in her cloak and said: Woe to Mistah. I said to her: Evil is what you said, cursing a man who witnessed Badr? She said: O dear, have you not heard what he said? I said: What did he say? She informed me of the words of the people of al-ifk. So my sickness increased upon my sickness. When we returned to my house, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon us, greeted, then said: How is she? I said to him: Will you permit me to come to my parents, then wishing to verify the news from them. She said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted me, and I came to my parents and said to my mother: O mother, what are people saying? She said: O daughter, make light of it; by Allah, seldom is there ever a beautiful woman with one who loves her, with co-wives, but they speak much against her. She said: I said: Glory be to Allah, have people spoken of this? I spent the night until morning with tears unceasing and no sleep; she said: Then morning came while I wept. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called Ali ibn Abi Talib and Usama ibn Zayd when revelation tarried, consulting them about parting with his wife. As for Usama, he advised the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with what he knew of his family's innocence and what he knew in himself of affection for them; Usama said: O Messenger of Allah, your family, and we know but good. As for Ali, he said: O Messenger of Allah, Allah has not constrained you; women besides her are many, and if you ask the maid she will tell you truly. She said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called Barira and said to her: O Barira, have you seen anything that makes you doubt? Barira said: By Him who sent you with the truth, I saw in her nothing I could fault beyond that she is a young girl who sleeps over her family's dough, and the household goat comes and eats it. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rose that day seeking excuse regarding Abd Allah ibn Ubayy ibn Salul; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said upon the pulpit: O assembly of Muslims, who will excuse me regarding a man whose harm against my household has reached me? By Allah, I know of my family but good, and they mentioned a man of whom I know but good, and he would not enter upon my family but with me. Sa'd ibn Muadh al-Ansari rose and said: O Messenger of Allah, I excuse you regarding him; if he is of al-Aws I strike his neck, and if he is of our brothers al-Khazraj, command us and we carry out your command. Sa'd ibn Ubada rose, then chief of al-Khazraj, previously a righteous man, but zeal carried him; Sa'd ibn Muadh said to him: You lie, by Allah's life you shall not kill him nor can you kill him. Usayd ibn Hudayr rose, cousin of Sa'd ibn Muadh, and said to Sa'd ibn Ubada: You lie, by Allah's life he shall surely kill him, for you are a hypocrite disputing for the hypocrites. The two tribes, al-Aws and al-Khazraj, flared up until they nearly fought, with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) calming them until they fell silent, and he fell silent. She said: I spent that day of mine with tears unceasing and no sleep. She said: My parents came to me in the morning, having wept my night with no sleep and tears unceasing, thinking weeping would split my liver. While they sat with me and I wept, a woman of the Ansar asked permission; I admitted her and she sat weeping with me. She said: While thus, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered and sat. She said: He had not sat with me since what was said to me was said before it, having spent a month with nothing revealed to him in anything. She said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bore witness when he sat, then said: As for what follows, O Aisha, such-and-such has reached me about you; if you are innocent, Allah will exonerate you, and if you committed a sin, seek Allah's forgiveness and repent to Him, for when a servant confesses his sin then repents, Allah turns to him. She said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished his words, my tears dried so I felt not a drop of them; I said to my father: Answer the Messenger of Allah for me regarding what he said. She said: He said: By Allah, I know not what to say to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). I said to my mother: Answer the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding what he said. She said: By Allah, I know not what to say to the Messenger of Allah. She said: I said, a young girl reading little of the Quran: By Allah, I know you have heard this report until it settled in your souls and you believed it; if I tell you I am innocent of it, and Allah knows I am innocent of it, you will not believe me in that; and if I confess to you a matter, and Allah knows I am innocent of it, you will believe me. By Allah, I find no likeness for me and you but Abu Yusuf, who said: Beautiful patience, and Allah is the one sought for help. She said: Then I turned and lay upon my bed. She said: By Allah, then I knew I was innocent and that Allah would exonerate me with my innocence, but by Allah I never thought Allah would send down revelation to be recited concerning me, my affair in myself being too slight for Allah to speak of me a matter to be recited; but I hoped the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would see in sleep a vision by which Allah would exonerate me. She said: By Allah, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had not left his seat, nor had anyone of the household left, before Allah, blessed and exalted, revealed to him; he was seized with what seized him of heaviness, until drops like pearls ran from him of sweat on a wintry day from the weight of the Quran sent down upon him. She said: When it was lifted from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what was lifted from him, smiling, the first word he spoke was: As for Allah, He has exonerated you. My mother said: Rise to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). I said: By Allah, I shall not rise to him, nor praise but Allah. And Allah, majestic His mention, revealed: Those who brought al-ifk, all ten verses. When Allah revealed this concerning my exoneration, Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, who spent upon Mistah ibn Uthatha for kinship and poverty, said: By Allah, I shall never spend upon Mistah anything after what he said of Aisha. So Allah revealed: Let not those of virtue among you and abundance give to kin, the poor and emigrants in Allah's path; let them pardon and overlook; do you not love that Allah forgive you, and Allah is Forgiving, Merciful, to the verse. Abu Bakr said: Yes, by Allah, I love that Allah forgive me; so he restored to Mistah the spending he used to spend upon him, saying: By Allah, I shall never withdraw it from him. Aisha said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would ask Zaynab bint Jahsh, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), about my affair, saying: O Zaynab, what did you know or see? She said: O Messenger of Allah, I guard my hearing and sight; by Allah, I know but good. She said: She was the one who rivalled me among the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wives, but Allah preserved her with piety. She said: Her sister Hamna kept fighting for her and perished among those who perished of the companions of al-ifk. Al-Zuhri said: This is what reached us of the report of these people from Aisha's hadith."
"20735","Narrated Abu Yazid Yusuf ibn Yazid al-Qaratisli from Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim al-Azraq, and narrated Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal and Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hadrami from Abu al-Rabi al-Zahrani, who said: Narrated Fulayh ibn Sulayman from al-Zuhri from Urwa ibn al-Zubayr, Said ibn al-Musayyib, Alqama ibn Waqqas and Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Utba from Aisha, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); each narrated to me a portion of her hadith, some retaining better than others and firmer in narration, and I retained from each man the hadith he narrated to me, parts of their hadith confirming parts. They alleged that Aisha, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended to set out on a journey, he cast lots among his wives; whichever one's lot came out, he set out with her. She said: So he cast lots among us on a campaign he waged, and my lot came out; so I set out with him after the veil was revealed. I am carried in a howdah and taken down in it, until, when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished that campaign of his, turned back, and we neared Medina, he announced departure one night. I rose when they announced departure and walked past the army; when I relieved myself, I returned to the camp and felt my chest, and a necklace of mine of striped onyx had broken. I returned seeking my necklace, and its search detained me. Those who used to mount me came and carried my howdah, mounting it upon my camel upon which I rode, thinking me in it; women in those days were light, not heavy, with flesh not covering them, eating but a morsel of food, so they lifted it, and I was a young girl. They sent the camel and marched; and I found my necklace after the army had gone on. I came to their camp with none in it, so I made for my spot in which I had been, thinking they would miss me and return to me. While I sat in my place, my eyes overcame me. Safwan ibn al-Muattal al-Sulami then al-Dhakwani was behind the army; he reached my place in the morning, seeing the shape of a sleeping person. He came to me, having seen me before the veil; I awoke at his istirja when he made his mount kneel, and he trod its hand and I mounted it, and he set off leading the mount until we reached the army after they had halted resting at midday. Then those perished who perished, and the one who took upon himself the chief part of al-ifk was Abd Allah ibn Ubayy ibn Salul. I arrived in Medina and fell ill there for a month, with people spreading the words of the companions of al-ifk while I sensed nothing of it. What troubled me in my pain was that I did not recognize from the Messenger of Allah the tenderness I used to see from him when I fell ill; he would only enter, greet, then say: How is she? That troubled me, sensing nothing, until I recovered. I went out with Umm Mistah toward al-Manasi, our place of relieving, going out only by night, before privies were made near our houses; our practice was the practice of the early Arabs in the desert, or in relieving. Umm Mistah bint Abi Ruhm and I came walking; she stumbled in her cloak and said: Woe to Mistah. I said to her: Evil is what you said, cursing a man who witnessed Badr? She said: O dear, have you not heard what they said? I said: What did they say? She informed me of the words of the people of al-ifk, and my sickness increased upon my sickness. When I returned to my house and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon me, he said: How is she? I said: Permit me to come to my parents. She said: Then wishing to verify the news from them. She said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted me, and I came to my parents and said to my mother: What are people saying? She said: O daughter, make light of the matter upon yourself; by Allah, seldom is there ever a beautiful woman with a man who loves her, with co-wives, but they speak much against her. I said: Glory be to Allah, have people spoken of this? She said: I spent that night until morning with tears unceasing and no sleep until morning. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called Ali ibn Abi Talib and Usama ibn Zayd when revelation tarried, consulting them about parting with his wife. She said: As for Usama, he advised the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with what he knew of his family's innocence and what he knew in himself of affection for them; Usama said: Your family, O Messenger of Allah, and we know but good. As for Ali, he said: O Messenger of Allah, Allah has not constrained you; women besides her are many, and ask the maid, she will tell you truly. She said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called Barira and said to her: Have you seen of her anything that makes you doubt? Barira said: No, by Him who sent you with the truth, I saw of her nothing I could fault beyond that she is a young girl who sleeps over the dough until the household goat comes and eats it. She said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rose that day seeking excuse regarding Abd Allah ibn Ubayy ibn Salul. She said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Who will excuse me regarding a man whose harm against my family has reached me? By Allah, he said it thrice, I know of my family but good, and they mentioned a man of whom I know but good, and he would not enter upon my family but with me. Sa'd ibn Muadh rose and said: I, by Allah, O Messenger of Allah, excuse you regarding him; if he is of al-Aws we strike his neck, and if he is of our brothers of al-Khazraj, command us and we carry out concerning him your command. Sa'd ibn Ubada rose, then chief of al-Khazraj, previously a righteous man, but zeal carried him; he said: You lie, by Allah's life you shall not kill him nor can you. Usayd ibn Hudayr said: You lie, by Allah's life he shall surely kill him, for you are a hypocrite disputing for the hypocrites. The two tribes, al-Aws and al-Khazraj, flared up until they nearly, with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) standing upon the pulpit; then he descended calming them until they fell silent, and he fell silent. I wept my day with tears unceasing and no sleep, with my parents at me, having wept my day and night until I thought weeping would split my eye. She said: While they sat with me and I wept, a woman of the Ansar asked permission, and I admitted her; she sat weeping with me. While thus, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered and sat, having not sat with me since what was said was said before it, having spent a month with nothing revealed to him concerning me. He bore witness then said: As for what follows, O Aisha, such-and-such has reached me about you; if you are innocent, Allah will exonerate you, and if you committed a sin, seek Allah's forgiveness and repent to Him, for when a servant confesses a sin then repents, Allah turns to him. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished his words, my tears dried so I felt not a drop of them; I said to my father: Answer the Messenger of Allah for me regarding what he said. He said: By Allah, I know not what to say to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). I said to my mother, and she said the like of that. I said, a young girl reading little of the Quran: By Allah, I know you have heard what is narrated and it settled in your souls and you believed it; if I say I am innocent, and Allah knows I am innocent, you will not believe me in that; and if I confess to you a matter, and Allah knows I am innocent of it, you will believe me. By Allah, I find no likeness for me and you but Abu Yusuf when he said: Beautiful patience. She said: Then I turned upon my bed, hoping Allah would exonerate me with my innocence, but by Allah I never thought revelation to be recited concerning me would descend, I being too slight in myself for the Quran to speak concerning my affair; but I hoped the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would see a vision exonerating me. She said: By Allah, he had not left his seat, nor had anyone left the house, before it was revealed to him; he was seized with what seized him of heaviness, until drops like pearls of sweat ran from him on a wintry day. She said: It was lifted from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), smiling, and the first word he spoke was to say: O Aisha, praise Allah, for Allah has exonerated you. My mother said to me: Rise to the Messenger of Allah. I said: By Allah, I shall not rise to him, nor praise but Allah. And Allah revealed: Those who brought al-ifk, all the verses. When Allah revealed this concerning my exoneration, Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, who spent upon Mistah ibn Uthatha for kinship, said: By Allah, I shall never spend upon Mistah anything after what he said of Aisha. So Allah revealed this verse: Let not those of virtue, to the end of the verse. Abu Bakr said: Yes, I love that Allah forgive me; so he restored to Mistah what he used to grant him. She said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had asked Zaynab bint Jahsh about my affair, saying: O Zaynab, what did you know and what did you see? She said: I guard my hearing and sight; by Allah, I know of her but good. Aisha said: She was the one who rivalled me, but Allah preserved her with piety."
"20736","136 - Narrated Abu Yazid al-Qaratisî from Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim al-Azraq (H) and narrated Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal and Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami, both saying: narrated Abu al-Rabi al-Zahrani, both saying: narrated Fulayh ibn Sulayman from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Aisha and Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, the same as it."
"20737","137 - Narrated Abu Yazid, saying: narrated Hajjaj al-Azraq (H) and narrated Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal and Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hadrami, both saying: narrated Abu al-Rabi, both saying: narrated Fulayh ibn Sulayman from Rabia ibn Abi Abd al-Rahman from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, the same as it."
"20738","138 - Narrated Ali ibn al-Mubarak al-Sanani from Zayd ibn al-Mubarak from Muhammad ibn Thawr from Ibn Jurayj, saying: And Ibn Shihab said, from Urwa and Ubayd Allah ibn Adi and Alqama ibn Waqqas, some of them adding to others, from Aisha, saying: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended a journey, he would cast lots among his wives; whichever of them her lot came out, he travelled with her. He went on an expedition in which my lot came out. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished his expedition and we drew near Madina, he announced one night departure. I went out until I passed beyond the army. When I finished my business, I returned to my place and felt for a necklace of mine of onyx beads, and it had broken. I went back, and seeking it detained me. The party who used to mount my howdah came and mounted it on my camel, thinking I was in it, while women then were light, not heavy with flesh. The army went on. I came to their dwellings with neither caller nor answerer among them. I headed for my place, knowing they would miss me and look for me. While I sat, my eye overcame me. Safwan ibn al-Muattal had rested behind the army, saw the black shape of a sleeping person, and headed for me. When he saw me, he said: To Allah we belong and to Him we return. He used to visit me before the veil was imposed on me. I was startled by his returning and covered my face. He spoke nothing but it, nor spoke to me, until he made his camel kneel, stepped on its foreleg, and I mounted. We reached the army at midday, exhausted, while those who perished perished. I arrived at Madina and fell ill a month, unaware of what the people of the slander said. What made me suspicious of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was that I did not see from him the kindness I used to see from him when I fell ill; he would only stand and say: How is that one? Until I recovered, I went out with Umm Mistah to al-Manasi, our place of relieving, our custom being the custom of the early Arabs: we ate only a morsel of food and relieved ourselves from night to night. When we finished, I returned home, and Umm Mistah stumbled in her wrap and said: May Mistah perish. I said: Evil is what you said; you reviled a righteous man who witnessed Badr. She said: O dear, do you not know what they said? I said: What did they say? She told me the report. I waited for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to come, asked his permission to go to my parents to verify the report from them, and came to their house. I found my father praying. I said: O mother, what did they say? She said: O my little daughter, seldom is a beautiful woman with a man who loves her, having co-wives, but they speak much against her. She said: Did they really say that? My father turned from praying. By Allah, I wished I had been mere menstrual blood. My eye would not be lined with sleep, nor my tear stop. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ascended the pulpit and said: What is with men whose harm in my family has reached me? They mentioned a righteous man who witnessed Badr; he never entered upon my family except while I was present, nor did I ever travel a journey but he was with me. Sad ibn Muadh stood and said: I am for you against him, O Messenger of Allah; if he is of al-Aws, we strike his neck, and if he is of Banu al-Harith ibn al-Khazraj, we bring him to you bound. Sad ibn Ubada stood and said: You lie; by Allah, you will not kill him nor can you kill him. Usayd ibn Hudayr ibn Simak stood and said: You lie; by Allah, he shall surely kill him, for you are a hypocrite disputing for the hypocrites. The two tribes, al-Aws and al-Khazraj, lunged at each other. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came down and kept quieting and calming them until they fell silent. Ibn Jurayj said: A freedman of Ibn Abbas said: Some of them said to others: Your appointment is al-Harra. They put on weapons and went out to it. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to them and kept reciting to them this verse: And remember Allah’s favor upon you, until its end, repeating it to them until they embraced one another, with sobbing among them. Then they left reconciled. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) delayed revelation, called Ali and Usama ibn Zayd, and consulted them about separating from his wife. Ali said: Allah has not constrained you; women are many; if you ask the slave-girl, meaning Barira, she will tell you the truth. Usama advised what he knew in himself of love for his family and of their innocence. He called Barira and said: Did you see anything in this girl you hold against her? She said: No, by Him who sent you with the truth, except that she is a young girl who sleeps until the household sheep eats her dough or leaven. Aisha said: I remained two days and two nights; my eye was not lined with sleep, nor my tear stopped. My parents were with me, thinking weeping would split my liver. A woman of the Ansar asked permission; I permitted her and she sat. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came and sat from me a sitting he had not sat since what was said was said. He praised Allah and extolled Him, then said: Now then, O Aisha, if you are innocent, Allah will exonerate you; if you committed a sin, repent of it and confess it, for when a person repents of a sin and confesses it, he is forgiven. She said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished his speech, my tear dried until I felt not a drop of it. I said: O father, answer the Messenger of Allah. He said: I do not know what to answer him. I said: O mother, answer the Messenger of Allah. She said: I do not know what to answer him. She said: I was a young girl, not reciting much of the Quran. I said: By Allah, you heard something of this that settled in yourselves. If I say I am innocent, and Allah knows I am innocent, you will not believe me. If I confess a sin of which I am innocent, you will believe me. My example and yours is only as the father of Yusuf, whose name was taken from me: So patience is beautiful, and Allah is the One sought for help. Then I turned my face toward the wall, myself too slight to me that Quran be sent down about me to be recited, but I hoped Allah would show His Messenger a vision in sleep. The people of the sitting had not dispersed before the heaviness that used to seize him at revelation seized the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), until there poured from him like pearls of sweat. He covered himself with his garment. Abu Bakr said: I looked at the Messenger of Allah, fearing there would come from heaven what none can repel, and looked at Aisha’s face, and it was brightening, feeding me hope in that from her. I looked here and here. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) removed the garment from himself, wiping his forehead of sweat, saying: Receive glad tidings, O Aisha; as for Allah, He has exonerated you. I said: By the praise of Allah, and your blame. My mother said: Rise and kiss the head of the Messenger of Allah. I said: No, by Allah, I shall not do it, nor praise but Allah. Among what pressed upon him was that she said: By Allah, I am not shy before the Ansari woman that she should say to me what she said."
"20739","139 - Narrated Ahmad ibn Muhammad al-Shafii from his father Ibrahim ibn Muhammad al-Shafii from Muslim ibn Khalid al-Zanji from Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Atiq from Ibn Shihab, saying: Urwa ibn al-Zubayr and Said ibn al-Musayyib and Alqama ibn Waqqas and Ubayd Allah ibn Abdullah ibn Utba narrated to me from Aisha, when the people of the slander said to her what they said and Allah exonerated her. Each narrated to me a portion of the report he narrated to me from Aisha, and part of their reports confirms part, though some of them retained her report better than others. They mentioned that Aisha, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended to go out on a journey, he would cast lots among his wives; whichever of them her lot came out, he went out with her. Aisha said: He cast lots among us in an expedition he undertook, and my lot came out. I went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after the veil was sent down. I was carried in my howdah and set down in it. We travelled until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished that expedition of his, turned back, and we drew near Madina; he announced one night departure. I rose when departure was announced, walked until I passed beyond the army. When I finished my business and came toward my mount, there was a necklace of mine of onyx beads broken. I returned seeking my necklace, and seeking it detained me. The party who used to mount me came, carried my howdah onto my camel I used to ride, thinking I was in it. We were then light; flesh had not made us heavy, for each of us ate only a morsel of food. The people did not notice the lightness of the howdah when they lifted it, for I was a young girl. They sent the camel and travelled. I found my necklace after the army had gone on. I came to their dwelling with neither caller nor answerer in it. I headed for my dwelling I had been in, thinking they would miss me and return to me. While I sat in my dwelling, my eye overcame me and I slept. Safwan ibn al-Muattal al-Sulami was behind the army; he travelled late and reached my dwelling in the morning. He saw the black shape of a sleeping person and knew me when he saw me; he used to see me before the veil. I woke at his returning when he knew me, and covered my face with my cloak. By Allah, he did not speak to me a word, nor did I hear from him a word, until he made his camel kneel, stepped on its hand, and I mounted it. He set out leading the mount with me until he reached the army after they had halted, travelling at speed in midday heat. Those of the people of the slander who perished perished. He who took upon himself the greater burden of sin was Abdullah ibn Ubayy ibn Salul. We arrived at Madina; I complained when we arrived at Madina a month. People spread the saying of the people of the slander, and I perceived nothing of that. What made me suspicious in my sickness was that I did not recognize from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the kindness I used to see from him when I complained; he would only enter upon me and say: How is that one? Then leave. That made me suspicious, and I perceived no evil, until we went out after I recovered, I and Umm Mistah, toward al-Manasi, our place of relieving. We went out only from night to night, before privies were taken near our houses. Our custom was the custom of the early Arabs in relieving toward the open land; we were harmed by taking privies at our houses. I and Umm Mistah set out toward my house when we finished our business. Umm Mistah stumbled in her wrap and said: May Mistah perish. I said: Evil is what you said; do you revile a man who witnessed Badr? She said: O dear, did you not hear what he said? I said: What did he say? She told me the saying of the people of the slander. My sickness increased upon my sickness. When I returned home, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon me and said: How is that one? I said: Do you permit me to go to my parents? She said: I then wished to verify the report from them. He permitted me. I came to my mother and father and said: O my mother, what are people saying? She said: O my little daughter, make light of it; seldom have I seen a radiant woman with a man who loves her, having co-wives, but they spoke much against her. I said: Glory be to Allah, have people spoken of that? She said: I wept that night until morning; my tear did not stop, nor was my eye lined with sleep. She said: Then I awoke weeping. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called Ali ibn Abi Talib and Usama ibn Zayd when revelation delayed, consulting them about separating from his wife. Usama advised the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with what he knew of his family’s innocence and what affection he had for them in himself. Usama said: O Messenger of Allah, your wife, and we know only good. Ali ibn Abi Talib said: O Messenger of Allah, Allah has not constrained you; women besides her are many; if you ask the slave-girl, she will tell you the truth. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called Barira and said to her: O Barira, did you see anything that makes you suspicious? Barira said: No, by Him who sent you with the truth; I never saw in her anything I hold against her more than that she is a young girl who sleeps past her family’s dough, and the household sheep comes and eats it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood that day seeking excuse from Abdullah ibn Ubayy. She said: He said while on the pulpit: O company of Muslims, who will excuse me concerning a man whose harm in my household has reached me? By Allah, I knew in my household only good. They mentioned a man about whom I knew only good; he would enter upon my family only with me. Sad ibn Muadh stood and said: O Messenger of Allah, I excuse you from him; if he is of al-Aws, I strike his neck, and if he is of our brothers of al-Khazraj, command us and we do your command about him. Sad ibn Ubada stood, chief of al-Khazraj; before that he had been a righteous man, but zeal seized him. He said to Sad ibn Muadh: You lie; by your life, you will not kill him nor can you kill him. Usayd ibn Hudayr, cousin of Sad ibn Muadh, stood and said to Sad ibn Ubada: You lie; by your life, he shall surely kill him, for you are a hypocrite disputing for the hypocrites. She said: The two tribes, al-Aws and al-Khazraj, flared until they nearly fought, with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) standing on the pulpit. He kept calming them until they fell silent, and he fell silent. She said: I spent that day of mine; my tear did not stop, nor was my eye lined with sleep. My parents were with me; I had spent two nights and a day, neither lined with sleep nor my tear stopping, thinking weeping would split my liver. While they sat with me and I wept, a woman of the Ansar asked permission; I permitted her and she wept with me. While we were so, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon us and sat. She said: He had not sat with me since what was said was said before it. A month had passed with nothing revealed to him concerning me. She said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) testified when he sat, then said: Now then, O Aisha, such-and-such reached me about you. If you are innocent, Allah will exonerate you. If you committed a sin, seek Allah’s forgiveness then repent to Him, for when a servant confesses a sin then repents to Allah, Allah turns to him. She said: When he finished his speech, my tear dried; I felt not a drop of it. I said to my father: Answer the Messenger of Allah in what he said. He said: By Allah, I do not know what to say to the Messenger of Allah. I said to my mother: Answer the Messenger of Allah. She said: By Allah, I do not know what to say to the Messenger of Allah. I said, a young girl not reciting much of the Quran: By Allah, you heard this report until it settled in yourselves and you believed it. If I tell you I am innocent, and Allah knows I am innocent, you will not believe me in that. If I confess a matter of which Allah knows I am innocent, you will surely believe me. By Allah, I find for me and you no example but the father of Yusuf, who said: So patience is beautiful, and Allah is the One sought for help against what you describe. Then I turned and lay on my bed. She said: I then knew I was innocent, and that Allah would exonerate me with my innocence. But by Allah, I did not think Allah Almighty would send down concerning me revelation to be recited; my affair in myself was too slight for Allah to speak about me a matter to be recited. But I hoped Allah would show His Messenger in sleep a vision exonerating me with it. She said: By Allah, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had not moved from his sitting, nor had anyone of the household gone out, before Allah sent down upon him. There seized him what used to seize him of heaviness, until there poured from him like pearls of sweat on a cold day, from the weight of the Quran sent down upon him. When the Messenger of Allah was relieved, relieved of him while laughing, the first word he spoke was to say: O Aisha, as for Allah, He has exonerated you. My mother said: Rise to him. I said: By Allah, I shall not rise to him, nor praise but Allah. Allah sent down: Those who brought the slander are a band among you, the ten verses all of them. When Allah sent down this concerning my exoneration, Abu Bakr, who used to spend on Mistah for his kinship and poverty, said: I shall never spend on him anything ever, for what he said about Aisha. Allah sent down: And let not those of virtue among you swear, the verse. Abu Bakr said: Yes, by Allah, I love that Allah should forgive me. He returned to Mistah the spending he used to spend on him, saying: By Allah, I shall never take it from him ever. Aisha said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had asked Zaynab bint Jahsh about my affair, saying: O Zaynab, what did you know or see? Zaynab said: I guard my hearing and sight; by Allah, I knew only good. She is the one who used to rival me among the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); Allah protected her with piety. Her sister Hamna set out fighting for her, and perished among those of the companions of the slander who perished. Ibn Shihab said: This is what reached us of the report of these people."
"20740","140 - Narrated Bakr ibn Sahl al-Dimyati from Abdullah ibn Yusuf from Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid ibn Jabir, saying: I heard Ata ibn Abi Muslim al-Khurasani narrating from al-Zuhri from Urwa ibn al-Zubayr from Aisha (H) and narrated Ahmad ibn Shuayb Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Nasai from Ishaq ibn Rahawayh from Kulthum ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Sidra from Ata al-Khurasani from Ibn Shihab from Alqama ibn Waqqas and Urwa ibn al-Zubayr from Aisha (H) and narrated Abd al-Aziz ibn Sulayman al-Harmali al-Antaki from Yaqub ibn Kab al-Halabi from Kulthum ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Sidra from Ata al-Khurasani from Muhammad ibn Muslim al-Zuhri from Urwa ibn al-Zubayr and Ubayd Allah ibn Abdullah and Said ibn al-Musayyib and Alqama ibn Waqqas from Aisha, saying: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out on a journey, he would cast lots among his wives; whichever of them her lot came out, he went out with her. She said: He cast lots among us in an expedition he undertook, and my lot came out. I went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), after the veil was sent down. I was carried in a howdah and set down in it. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned back and drew near Madina, he announced departure. I rose when departure was permitted, walked until I passed beyond the army to relieve my need. I felt my chest, and a necklace of mine of nails had broken. I returned seeking it, and seeking it detained me. The party who carried my howdah came and mounted it on my camel, thinking I was in it. Women then were light, not fleshy; we ate only a morsel of food. I was a young girl, so the people did not notice the weight of the howdah when they mounted it on my camel. They travelled. I came to the dwelling with neither caller nor answerer among them. I headed for my dwelling I had been in, thinking they would return seeking me. She said: While I sat, my eye overcame me and I slept. Safwan ibn al-Muattal al-Sulami then al-Dhakwani was behind the army; he travelled late and reached the dwelling in the morning. He saw the black shape of a sleeping person and knew me; he had seen me before the veil was sent down. I woke at his returning and covered my face with my cloak. By Allah, I did not speak to him, nor heard from him a word besides his returning, until he made his camel kneel and I mounted it. We reached the people at midday. Those who perished perished. He who took upon himself its greater part among them was Abdullah ibn Ubayy ibn Salul. She said: We travelled until we arrived at Madina. I complained a month, unaware of what they said. What made me suspicious of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was that I did not recognize from him the kindness I used to see from him; he would only enter upon me and say: How is that one? Adding nothing to that, until I went out toward al-Manasi, with Umm Mistah. We went out only from night to night. We were harmed by taking privies near our houses; our custom was the custom of the early Arabs. When we turned back, Umm Mistah stumbled in her wrap, or with her wrap, and said: May Mistah perish. I said to her: Evil is what you said; do you revile a man who witnessed Badr? She said: Do you not know what he said? I said: What did he say? She told me the saying of the people of the slander. It added sickness to what was in me. She said: Umm Mistah was bint Sakhr ibn Amir, maternal aunt of Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, and her son was Mistah ibn Uthatha ibn Abbad ibn al-Muttalib ibn Abd Manaf. Aisha said: I wept two nights and a day until I thought weeping would split my liver. She said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) delayed revelation, he called Usama ibn Zayd and Ali ibn Abi Talib, consulting them about separating from his wife. Usama said: O Messenger of Allah, your wife, and we knew only good. Ali said: Allah has not constrained you; women besides her are many; if you ask the slave-girl, she will tell you the truth. The Messenger of Allah called Barira and said: O Barira, did you see in Aisha anything you dislike? She said: No, by Him who sent you with the truth; I saw in her nothing I hold against her more than that she is a young girl who sleeps past her family’s dough, and the household sheep enters and eats it. She said: The wife of Abu Ayyub had said to Abu Ayyub: Did you not hear what people talk of? She told him the saying of the people of the slander. He said: Glory be to You; it is not for us to speak of this; glory be to You, this is a tremendous slander. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood and said: O company of Muslims, who will excuse me concerning a man whose harm in my family has reached me? By Allah, I knew in her only good. They mentioned a righteous man; he would enter upon my family only with me. Sad ibn Muadh stood and said: I excuse you from him, O Messenger of Allah; if he is of al-Aws, we strike his neck, and if he is of our brothers al-Khazraj, command us and we do your command in him. Sad ibn Ubada stood; before that he had been a righteous man, but zeal carried him. He said to Sad ibn Muadh: You lie; by your life, you cannot kill him. Usayd ibn Hudayr, cousin of Sad ibn Muadh, stood and said to Sad ibn Ubada: You lie; by your life, he shall surely kill him, for you are a hypocrite disputing for the hypocrites. The two tribes lunged until they nearly fought. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kept interposing between them. She said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon me with my parents with me; a woman of the Ansar had entered upon me assisting me. She said: He sat, and had not sat with me since what was said was said to me. He said: Now then, O Aisha, such-and-such reached me about you. If you are innocent, Allah will exonerate you. If you committed something, seek Allah’s forgiveness and repent to Him. She said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished his speech, my tear dried until I felt not a drop of it. I said to my father: Answer the Messenger of Allah in what he said. He said: By Allah, I do not know what to say to the Messenger of Allah. I said to my mother: Answer the Messenger of Allah in what he said. My mother said: I do not know what to say to the Messenger of Allah. She said: I was a young girl, not reciting much of the Quran. I said: By Allah, if I confess to you a matter of which Allah knows I am innocent, you will believe me. If I say I am innocent, you will not believe me. By Allah, I find for me and you no example but what the father of Yusuf said: So patience is beautiful, and Allah is the One sought for help against what you describe. I said: Then I turned, Allah knowing I am innocent. My affair was smaller in myself than that Quran be sent down about me. She said: But I hoped Allah would show His Messenger in his sleep a vision exonerating me in it. She said: By Allah, the Messenger of Allah had not moved his sitting, nor had anyone of the household gone out, before heaviness seized him. She said: When revelation came to him, heaviness seized him until there poured from him like pearls of sweat on a cold day. She said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was relieved when relieved of him. The first word he spoke was to say: As for Allah, He has exonerated you, O Aisha. My mother said to me: Rise to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). I said: By Allah, I shall not rise to him, nor praise for that but Allah. Allah the Exalted revealed: Those who brought the slander are a band among you, to His saying: And Allah is Hearing, Knowing. Abu Bakr used to spend on Mistah for his need and kinship. When he spoke what he spoke, he said: By Allah, I shall never spend on him anything ever. Allah Almighty sent down: And let not those of virtue among you swear, to His saying: Do you not love that Allah should forgive you. Abu Bakr said: Yes, I love that Allah should forgive me. He returned to Mistah what he used to spend on him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked Zaynab bint Jahsh. She said: She is the one who used to rival me among the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked her about me; Allah protected her with piety. She said: I guard my hearing and sight; I saw in her nothing that makes me suspicious. The sister of Zaynab, Hamna, opposed me, and perished among those who perished."
"20741","141 - Narrated Abu Shuayb Abdullah ibn al-Hasan al-Harrani from Abdullah ibn Jafar al-Raqqi from Ubayd Allah ibn Amr from Ishaq ibn Rashid from al-Zuhri from Urwa ibn al-Zubayr and Said ibn al-Musayyib and Alqama ibn Waqqas and Ubayd Allah ibn Abdullah ibn Utba, all of them, from Aisha, concerning what the people of the slander said to her and Allah exonerated her from what they said. Al-Zuhri said: Each of them narrated to me a portion of her report; some of them retained her report better than others and were firmer in relating it precisely. I retained from each man of them the report he narrated to me from her, and part of their reports confirms part, though some of them retained it better than others. She said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended to go out on a journey, he would cast lots among his wives; whichever of them her lot came out, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out with her. Aisha said: He cast lots among us in an expedition he undertook, and my lot came out. I went out with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after Allah sent down the veil. I was carried in my howdah and set down with it. When he finished that expedition of his and we drew near Madina, we intended departure. I went out when they announced departure, relieved my need beyond the army. When I finished, I returned to my family and felt my chest, and a necklace upon me of onyx beads had broken. I went out seeking it, and seeking it detained me. The party who mounted me came, carried my howdah onto my camel I used to ride, thinking I was in it. Women then had not been made heavy with flesh; each of us ate only a morsel of food. The people did not notice the lightness of the howdah when they lifted it. I was a young girl. They sent the camel. I found my necklace after the army had gone on. I came to their dwellings with neither caller nor answerer among them. I headed for my dwelling I had been in, thinking they would miss me and return to me. While I was so in my dwelling, my eye overcame me and I slept. Safwan ibn al-Muattal was behind the army; he travelled late and reached my dwelling in the morning. He saw the black shape of a person and knew me when he saw me; he had seen me before the veil. I woke at his returning and covered my face with my cloak. By Allah, he did not speak to me a word, nor did I hear anything of his speech besides his returning, when he made his camel kneel, stepped on its hand, then I mounted it. He set out leading the mount with me until we reached the army after they had halted, travelling at speed in midday heat. Those of the people of the slander who perished perished. He who took upon himself its greater part was Abdullah ibn Ubayy ibn Salul. I complained when we arrived at Madina a month, while people spread the saying of the people of the slander, and I perceived nothing of that. What made me suspicious in my sickness was that I did not recognize from the Messenger of Allah the kindness I used to see from him when I complained; he would only enter and say: How is that one? Then leave. That is what made me suspicious of him, and I perceived nothing, until I went out after I recovered, I and Umm Mistah, daughter of Abi Ruhm ibn al-Muttalib, her mother daughter of Sakhr ibn Amir, maternal aunt of Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, her son Mistah ibn Uthatha. I and Umm Mistah came when we finished our business. Umm Mistah stumbled in her wrap and said: May Mistah perish. I said: Evil is what you said; do you revile a man who witnessed Badr? She said: Did you not hear what he said? I said: What did he say? She told me the saying of the people of the slander. My sickness increased upon my sickness. When I returned, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon me and said: How is that one? I said: Permit me to go to my parents, then wished to verify the report from them. She said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted me. I came to my parents and said to my mother: What are people saying? She said: O my little daughter, make light of it; seldom is a radiant woman with a man who loves her, having co-wives, but they speak much against her. I said: Glory be to Allah, have people spoken of this? I spent that night until morning; my tear did not stop, nor was my eye lined with sleep. Then I awoke weeping. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called Ali and Usama ibn Zayd when revelation delayed, consulting them about separating from his wife. Usama advised the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with what he knew of his family’s innocence and the affection for them in himself, saying to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Your wife, and we know only good. Ali said: O Messenger of Allah, Allah has not constrained you; women besides her are many; if you ask the slave-girl, she will tell you the truth. The Messenger of Allah called Barira and said: O Barira, did you see anything that makes you suspicious? She said: No, by Him who sent you with the truth; I never saw in her anything I hold against her more than that she is a young girl who sleeps past her family’s dough, and the household sheep comes and eats it. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ascended the pulpit seeking excuse from Abdullah, saying: Who will excuse me concerning a man whose harm in my family reached me? By Allah, I knew in my family only good. They mentioned a man about whom I knew only good; he would enter upon my family only with me. Sad ibn Muadh stood and said: O Messenger of Allah, I excuse you from him; if he is of our brothers of al-Aws, I strike his neck, and if he is of our brothers of al-Khazraj, command us and we do your command in him. Sad ibn Ubada, chief of al-Khazraj, stood and said to Sad ibn Muadh: You lie; by your life, you will not kill him nor can you kill him. Before that he had been a righteous man, but zeal made him ignorant. Usayd ibn Hudayr, cousin of Sad ibn Muadh, stood and said to Sad ibn Ubada: You lie; by your life, he shall surely kill him, for you are a hypocrite disputing for the hypocrites. The two tribes of al-Aws and al-Khazraj lunged until they nearly fought, with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the pulpit. He kept quieting them until they fell silent. I spent that day of mine weeping; my tear did not stop, nor was my eye lined with sleep. My parents awoke with me, thinking weeping would split my liver. While they sat with me and I wept, a woman of the Ansar asked permission; I permitted her and she sat with me. While we were so, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon us and sat. He had not sat before that since what was said was said. A month had passed with nothing revealed to him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) testified when he sat, saying: Now then, O Aisha, such-and-such reached me. If you are innocent, Allah will exonerate you. If you committed a sin, seek Allah’s forgiveness then repent to Him, for when a servant sins then repents, Allah turns to him. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished his speech, my tear dried until I felt not a drop of it. I said to my father: Answer the Messenger of Allah. He said: By Allah, I do not know what to say to the Messenger of Allah. I said to my mother: Answer the Messenger of Allah. She said: By Allah, I do not know what to say to the Messenger of Allah. I am a woman young in age, not reciting much of the Quran. I said: By Allah, I know you heard this report until it settled in yourselves and you believed it. If I say I am innocent, and Allah knows I am innocent, you will not believe me. I find for me and you no example but the father of Yusuf: So patience is beautiful, and Allah is the One sought for help against what you describe. Then I turned and lay on my bed. I did not see that Allah would send down concerning me revelation, and my affair was too slight in myself for Allah to speak about me a matter from heaven. But I hoped Allah would show His Prophet, peace be upon him, a vision in sleep by which Allah would exonerate me. By Allah, the Messenger of Allah had not moved from his sitting, nor had anyone of the household gone out, before there seized him what used to seize him of heaviness, which is sweat when revelation comes down upon him. When revelation came to him, heaviness seized him until there poured from him like pearls on a cold day, from the weight of the Quran sent down upon him. She said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was relieved while laughing. The first word he spoke was: As for you, O Aisha, Allah has exonerated you. I said: By the praise of Allah, not by your praise. My mother said: Rise to him. I said: By Allah, I shall not rise to him, nor praise but Allah. Allah sent down: Those who brought the slander, to the end of the ten verses all of them. When Allah sent down this concerning my exoneration, Abu Bakr, who used to spend on Mistah for his kinship and poverty, said: By Allah, I shall never spend on Mistah anything ever, after what he said about Aisha. Allah sent down: And let not those of virtue among you swear, the whole verse. Abu Bakr said: By Allah, I love that Allah should forgive me. He returned to Mistah the spending he spent. He said: I shall never take it from you ever. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had asked Zaynab bint Jahsh about my affair, saying: What did you see and know? She said: I saw and knew only good. She is the one who used to rival me among the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); Allah protected her with piety. Her sister Hamna bint Jahsh set out opposing, and perished among those of the people of the slander who perished. Al-Zuhri said: This is what reached us of the report of these people in this hadith."
"20742","142 - Narrated Muhammad ibn al-Fadl ibn Jabir al-Saqati and Zakariyya ibn Yahya al-Saji, both saying: narrated Harun ibn Musa al-Farwi from Ishaq ibn Muhammad al-Farwi from Malik ibn Anas from Yahya ibn Said and Ubayd Allah ibn Umar, both from Ibn Shihab, saying: Urwa ibn al-Zubayr and Said ibn al-Musayyib and Alqama ibn Waqqas and Ubayd Allah ibn Abdullah ibn Utba ibn Masud narrated to me from Aisha, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when the people of the slander said to her what they said and Allah exonerated her from that. Each narrated to me a portion of her report; some of them retained her report better than others and were better in relating precisely, and part of them confirms the report of part. She said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended a journey, he would cast lots among his wives; whichever of them her lot came out, he went out with her. She said: He cast lots among us in a raid he undertook, and my lot came out. I went out with him after the veil was sent down. I was carried in my howdah and set down in it. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished that expedition of his, turned back, and we drew near Madina, he announced one night departure. I rose when they announced departure, walked until I passed beyond the army. When I finished, I came to the mount and felt my chest, and a necklace of mine of onyx beads had broken. I returned seeking my necklace, and seeking it detained me. Those who mounted me came, carried my howdah and mounted it on my camel I used to ride, thinking I was in it. Women then were light, not heavy, flesh had not made them heavy; they ate only a morsel of food. The people did not notice when they lifted it the weight of the howdah, and carried it. I was a young girl. They sent the camel and travelled. I found my necklace after the army had gone on. I came to their dwelling with neither caller nor answerer in it. I headed for my dwelling I had been in, thinking they would miss me and return to me. While I sat in my dwelling, my eye overcame me. Safwan ibn al-Muattal al-Sulami then al-Dhakwani had rested behind the army; he travelled late at my dwelling. He saw the black shape of a sleeping person and came to me; he used to see me before the veil came down. I woke only at his returning when he saw me. By Allah, he did not speak to me, nor did I hear from him a word besides his returning. Then he made his camel kneel, stepped on its hand, and I mounted it. He set out leading the mount with me until we reached the army after they had halted, resting in midday heat. Those who perished perished. He who took upon himself the greater burden of the slander was Abdullah ibn Ubayy ibn Salul. We arrived at Madina; I complained there a month, while people spread the saying of the companions of the slander, and I perceived nothing of that. What made me suspicious in my sickness was that I did not see from the Messenger of Allah the kindness I used to see from him when I fell ill; he would only enter upon me, greet, then say: How is that one? That made me suspicious, and I perceived nothing, until I recovered and went out, I and Umm Mistah bint Abi Ruhm, toward al-Manasi, our place of relieving; we went out only from night to night, before privies were taken near our houses. Our custom was the custom of the early Arabs in relieving. I and Umm Mistah came walking; she stumbled in her wrap and said: May Mistah perish. I said to her: Evil is what you said; do you revile a man who witnessed Badr? She said: O dear, did you not hear what he said? I said: What did he say? She told me the saying of the people of the slander. My sickness increased to my sickness. When I returned home, the Messenger of Allah entered upon me and said: How is that one? I said: Permit me to go to my parents. She said: I then wished to verify the report from them. She said: I came to my parents and said to my mother: What are people saying? She said: O my little daughter, make light of yourself; seldom is a radiant woman ever with a man who loves her, having co-wives, but they speak much against her. I said: Glory be to Allah, have people spoken of this? She said: I spent that night until morning; my tear did not stop, nor was my eye lined with sleep, until morning. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called Ali ibn Abi Talib and Usama ibn Zayd when revelation delayed, consulting them about separating from his wife. She said: Usama advised him with what he knew of his family’s innocence and what affection he knew in himself for them. Usama said: Your wife, O Messenger of Allah, and we know only good. Ali said: O Messenger of Allah, Allah has not constrained you; women besides her are many; ask the slave-girl, she will tell you the truth. She said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called Barira and said to her: Did you know in Aisha anything that makes you suspicious? Barira said: No, by Him who sent you with the truth; I saw in her nothing I hold against her more than that she is a young girl who sleeps past the dough until the household sheep comes and eats it. She said: The Messenger of Allah rose that day seeking excuse from Abdullah ibn Ubayy ibn Salul. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O company of Muslims, who will excuse me concerning a man whose harm in my family reached me? By Allah, I knew in my family only good. They mentioned a man about whom I knew only good; he would enter upon my family only with me. Sad ibn Muadh stood and said: I excuse you from him, O Messenger of Allah; if he is of al-Aws, I strike his neck, and if he is of our brothers of al-Khazraj, command us and we do your command. Sad ibn Ubada, chief of al-Khazraj, stood; before that he had been a righteous man, but zeal made him ignorant. He said: You lie; by your life, you will not kill him, nor can you kill him. Usayd ibn Hudayr, cousin of Sad ibn Muadh, stood and said to Sad ibn Ubada: You lie; by your life, he shall surely kill him; you are a hypocrite disputing for the hypocrites. The two tribes, al-Aws and al-Khazraj, flared until they nearly fought, with the Messenger of Allah calming them until they fell silent, and he fell silent. I wept my day; my tear did not stop, nor was my eye lined with sleep, until I thought weeping would split my liver. While I was so, a woman of the Ansar asked permission; I permitted her and she sat weeping with me. While we were so, the Messenger of Allah entered and sat with me. He had not sat with me since what was said was said before it. A month had passed with nothing revealed to him concerning me. He testified, then said: Now then, O Aisha, such-and-such reached me about you. If you are innocent, Allah will exonerate you. If you committed a sin, seek Allah’s forgiveness and repent to Him, for when a servant confesses a sin then repents, Allah turns to him. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished his speech, my tear dried until I felt not a drop of it. I said to my father: Answer for me the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in what he said. He said: By Allah, I do not know what to say to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). I said to my mother; she said the like of that. I said, a young girl not reciting much of the Quran: By Allah, you heard what is spoken of and it settled in yourselves and you believed. If I say I am innocent, and Allah knows I am innocent, you will not believe me in that. If I confess to you a matter of which Allah knows I am innocent, you will surely believe me. By Allah, I find for me and you no example but what the father of Yusuf said when he said: So patience is beautiful, and Allah is the One sought for help against what you describe. She said: Then I turned on my bed, hoping Allah would exonerate me with my innocence. But I did not hope revelation to be recited would come down concerning me; I was too slight in myself for Quran to be recited to be sent down about me. But I hoped the Messenger of Allah would see in sleep a vision by which Allah would exonerate me. She said: By Allah, the Messenger of Allah had not moved from his sitting, nor had anyone of the house gone out, before Allah sent down upon him. There seized him what used to seize him of heaviness, until there poured from him like pearls of sweat on a cold day. She said: The Messenger of Allah was relieved while laughing. The first word he spoke was to say: O Aisha, praise Allah, for Allah has exonerated you. I said: By the praise of Allah, not by your praise. My mother said to me: Rise to the Messenger of Allah. I said: No, by Allah, I shall not rise to him, nor praise but Allah. Allah Almighty sent down: Those who brought the slander are a band among you, all the verses. When Allah sent down my exoneration, Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, who used to spend on Mistah, said: By Allah, I shall never spend on Mistah anything ever, after what he said about Aisha. Allah Almighty sent down: And let not those of virtue among you swear, to the end of the verse. Abu Bakr said: Yes, I love that Allah should forgive me. He returned to Mistah what he used to spend. She said: The Messenger of Allah had asked Zaynab bint Jahsh about my affair, saying: O Zaynab, what did you know and see? She said: I guard my hearing and sight; by Allah, I knew in her only good. Aisha said: She is the one who used to rival me among the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); Allah protected her with piety. Her sister Hamna bint Jahsh set out opposing for her and perished among those who perished. Al-Zuhri said: This is what reached us of the report of these people."
"20743","143 - Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Muhammad ibn Khalid ibn Abdullah al-Wasiti from Ibrahim ibn Sad from Salih ibn Kaysan from Ibn Shihab, saying: Urwa ibn al-Zubayr and Said ibn al-Musayyib and Alqama ibn Waqqas and Ubayd Allah ibn Utba narrated to me from Aisha, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), where the people of the slander said to her what they said and Allah exonerated her. Each narrated to me a portion of her report; some of them retained her report better than others and were firmer in relating precisely. I retained from each one of them the report he narrated to me from Aisha. Aisha said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended a journey, he would cast lots among his wives; whichever of them her lot came out, he went out with her. He cast lots among us in a raid he undertook, and my lot came out. I went out with him after the veil was sent down. I was carried in my howdah and set down in it. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished that expedition of his and turned back, we drew near Madina; he announced one night departure. I rose when they announced departure, walked until I passed beyond the army. When I finished, I came to the mount and felt my chest, and a necklace of mine of onyx beads had broken. I returned seeking my necklace, and seeking it detained me. Those who mounted me came, carried my howdah and mounted it on my camel I used to ride, thinking I was in it. Women then were light, not heavy, flesh had not made them heavy; they ate only a morsel of food. The people did not notice when they lifted it the weight of the howdah, and carried it. I was a young girl. They sent the camel and travelled. I found my necklace after the army had gone on. I came to their dwelling with no one in it. I headed for my dwelling I had been in, thinking they would miss me and return to me. While I sat in my dwelling, my eye overcame me and I slept. Safwan ibn al-Muattal al-Sulami then al-Dhakwani had rested behind the army; he travelled late and reached my dwelling in the morning. He saw the black shape of a sleeping person and came to me; he used to see me before the veil. I woke at his returning. By Allah, he did not speak to me a word, nor did I hear from him a word besides his returning, until he made his camel kneel, stepped on its hand, and I mounted it. He set out leading the mount with me until we reached the army after they had halted, resting in midday heat. Those who perished perished. He who took upon himself the greater burden of the slander was Abdullah ibn Ubayy ibn Salul. We arrived at Madina; I complained there a month, while people spread the saying of the companions of the slander, and I perceived nothing of that. What made me suspicious in my sickness was that I did not recognize from the Messenger of Allah the kindness I used to see when I fell ill; he would only enter, greet, then say: How is that one? That made me suspicious, and I perceived nothing, until I recovered. I and Umm Mistah went out toward al-Manasi; we relieved ourselves, going out only from night to night, before privies were taken near our houses. Our custom was the custom of the early Arabs in relieving. I came (Full Ifk narrative as in 20742: Umm Mistah’s stumble, learning the slander, seeking permission to parents, mother’s consolation, weeping night, consultation of Ali and Usama, questioning of Barira, pulpit excuse, Aws-Khazraj dispute, sitting with parents and Ansari woman, address: if innocent Allah will exonerate, else seek forgiveness; parents unable to answer; Yaqub example; turning to bed hoping for a dream vision; revelation heaviness with sweat like pearls; laughing relief: Allah exonerated you; By Allah’s praise not yours; verses of slander; Abu Bakr’s oath over Mistah and: And let not those of virtue swear with: Do you not love that Allah should forgive you; return of spending; questioning of Zaynab bint Jahsh who guarded hearing and sight and knew only good; Allah protected her with piety; her sister Hamna opposed and perished among those who perished.)"
"20744","144 - Narrated Amr ibn Abi al-Tahir ibn al-Sarh al-Misri and Zakariyya ibn Yahya al-Saji, both saying: narrated Muhammad ibn Uzayz al-Ayli from Salama ibn Rawh from Uqayl ibn Khalid from Ibn Shihab, saying: Said ibn al-Musayyib and Urwa ibn al-Zubayr and Ubayd Allah ibn Abdullah ibn Utba informed me concerning the report of Aisha, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when the people of the slander said what they said and Allah exonerated her. Each narrated to me a portion of the report he narrated to me from Aisha, and part of their reports confirms part, though some of them retained it better than others. They alleged that Aisha, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended to go out on a journey, he would cast lots among his wives; whichever of them her lot came out, the Messenger of Allah went out with her. Aisha said: He cast lots among us in an expedition he undertook; my lot came out. I went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after the veil was sent down. I was carried and set down in my howdah. We travelled until the Messenger of Allah finished that expedition of his, and we drew near Madina returning; he announced one night departure. I rose when departure was announced for my need. When I finished, I came to my mount, and a necklace of mine of onyx beads had broken. I returned to my necklace seeking it. The party who used to mount me came, carried my howdah and mounted it on my camel I used to ride, thinking I was in it. Women then were light; flesh had not made them heavy; they ate only a morsel of food. The people did not deny the lightness of the howdah. I was a young girl. They sent the camel and (Full Ifk narrative as in 20742: finding necklace after army gone, dwelling empty, waiting expecting return, sleeping, Safwan behind army travelling late, black shape recognized from before veil, waking at returning, covering face, no speech but returning, kneeling camel stepping on hand, mounting, leading to army halted travelling at speed in midday heat, perishing of slanderers, Abdullah ibn Ubayy bearing greater burden, month illness at Madina unaware, missing kindness: How is that one, Umm Mistah to al-Manasi only night to night before privies near houses, early Arab custom, stumble in wrap: May Mistah perish, rebuke for reviling Badr witness, learning slander, added sickness, return home: How is that one, permission to parents to verify, parents’ house, mother’s consolation on beautiful woman with co-wives, weeping night without sleep or stopping tear, consultation of Ali and Usama on separation after delayed revelation, Usama: your wife we know only good, Ali: Allah not constrained you women besides her many ask slave-girl she tells truth, calling Barira: did you see suspicious, Barira: No by Him who sent you with truth never held against her but young girl sleeping past dough sheep eats it, pulpit excuse from Abdullah ibn Ubayy ibn Salul: who excuses me man whose harm in my family reached me by Allah knew in my family only good mentioned man knew only good entered only with me, Sad ibn Muadh: I excuse you if Aws strike neck if brothers Khazraj command us do your command, Sad ibn Ubada chief Khazraj before righteous but zeal made ignorant: you lie by your life will not kill nor can, Usayd ibn Hudayr cousin of Sad ibn Muadh to Sad ibn Ubada: you lie by your life he shall kill him hypocrite disputing for hypocrites, two tribes flared near fighting Prophet calming till silent, weeping day no tear stopping no sleep thinking weeping splits liver, Ansari woman permission sitting weeping with me, Messenger entering sitting not sat since said before it, month no revelation, testifying then: Now then O Aisha such reached me if innocent Allah will exonerate if sinned seek forgiveness repent servant confesses then repents Allah turns, tear dried not drop, father: by Allah know not what say, mother like that, young girl little Quran: by Allah you heard settled believed if innocent Allah knows innocent not believe if confess matter Allah knows innocent will believe, no example but father of Yusuf: So patience beautiful Allah sought help what you describe, turning on bed hoping Allah exonerate with innocence not hoping recited revelation too slight Quran recited sent about me but hoping Messenger see sleep vision Allah exonerate, by Allah Messenger not moved sitting nor household out before Allah sent down seized heaviness poured like pearls sweat cold day, relieved laughing first word: O Aisha praise Allah Allah exonerated you, I: by Allah’s praise not yours, mother: rise to Messenger, I: no by Allah not rise nor praise but Allah, Allah sent down: Those who brought slander band among you all verses, exoneration Abu Bakr al-Siddiq spending on Mistah: by Allah never spend after said about Aisha, Allah sent: And let not those of virtue swear to end verse, Abu Bakr: yes love Allah forgive me, returned to Mistah spending, Messenger asked Zaynab bint Jahsh: O Zaynab what knew saw, she: guard hearing sight by Allah knew only good, Aisha: she rivalled me among wives Prophet Allah protected with piety, sister Hamna bint Jahsh opposed for her perished among those perished.)"
"20745","145 - Narrated Musa ibn Harun from al-Harith ibn ... from Abu Mashar Najih al-Madani from Aflah ibn Abdullah ibn al-Mughira from al-Zuhri, saying: I was with al-Walid ibn Abd al-Malik one of the nights while he reclined reciting Surat al-Nur. When he reached: Those who brought the slander are a band among you, until he reached: And he who took upon himself the greater part thereof will have a great punishment, he sat up, then said: O Abu Bakr, who took upon himself its greater part? Is it not Ali ibn Abi Talib? I said in myself: What shall I say? If I say: No, I feared meeting evil from him. If I say: Yes, I would bring a tremendous matter, attributing to a man among the companions of the Messenger of Allah what he did not say. Then I said in myself: Allah has accustomed me to truth with good. No, O Commander of the Believers. He said: He struck the bed with his stick twice or thrice, then said: Who? Repeating that many times. I said: O Commander of the Believers, Abdullah ibn Ubayy ibn Salul. Urwa ibn al-Zubayr and Said ibn al-Musayyib and Alqama ibn Waqqas and Ubayd Allah ibn Abdullah ibn Utba narrated to me, some people better in wording than others, each preserving his report, that Aisha said, Ali pointed concerning me, may Allah forgive him. Then she said: When the Messenger of Allah intended a journey, he would cast lots among his wives. He went on the expedition of Banu al-Mustaliq, casting among his wives, and my lot came out. I went out with the Messenger of Allah after the veil was sent down. I was carried in my howdah and set down from it. We travelled until the Messenger of Allah finished that expedition of his, turned back, and we drew near Madina; he announced one night departure. I rose when departure was announced, walked (Full Ifk narrative as in 20742: passing beyond army, finishing business, necklace of onyx beads broken, returning seeking detained, party carrying howdah onto camel thinking in it, women light eating morsel, people not noticing weight, young girl, sending camel travelling, finding necklace after army gone, empty dwelling, heading dwelling expecting miss return, sitting eye overcome sleeping, Safwan behind army travelling late morning black shape recognized from before veil, waking at returning covering face, no speech but returning, kneeling camel stepping hand mounting, leading to army halted travelling at speed midday heat, perishing slanderers, Abdullah ibn Ubayy greater burden, month complaint Madina unaware, missing kindness How is that one, Umm Mistah to al-Manasi night to night before privies near houses early Arab custom, stumble: May Mistah perish, rebuke Badr witness, learning slander added sickness, return home How is that one, permission to parents verify, mother consolation radiant woman co-wives, weeping night no tear no sleep, consultation Ali Usama separation delayed revelation, Usama wife know only good, Ali Allah not constrained women many ask slave-girl truth, calling Barira suspicious, Barira No by Him sent truth young girl sleeps dough sheep eats, pulpit excuse Abdullah ibn Ubayy: who excuses me harm family by Allah knew only good mentioned man knew only good entered only with me, Sad ibn Muadh excuse if Aws strike neck if Khazraj brothers command do, Sad ibn Ubada chief before righteous zeal ignorant: lie by your life not kill nor can, Usayd cousin: lie by your life shall kill hypocrite disputing hypocrites, tribes flared near fighting calming silent, weeping day no tear no sleep thinking splits liver, Ansari woman weeping with, Messenger sitting not sat since, month no revelation, testifying: Now then O Aisha such reached if innocent Allah exonerate if sinned seek forgiveness repent servant confesses repents Allah turns, tear dried, father know not, mother like, young little Quran: heard settled believed innocent Allah knows innocent not believe confess Allah knows innocent will believe, example father Yusuf patience beautiful sought help, turning bed hoping exoneration dream vision not recited revelation slight, by Allah not moved sitting nor household out before sent down heaviness pearls sweat cold day, relieved laughing: O Aisha Allah exonerated, by Allah’s praise not yours, mother rise, no by Allah nor praise but Allah, verses slander, Abu Bakr oath Mistah spending kinship poverty never after Aisha, And let not virtue swear Do you not love Allah forgive you, Abu Bakr yes love forgive returned never take, Messenger asking Zaynab bint Jahsh: O Zaynab what knew saw, guarding hearing sight by Allah knew only good, rivalled me wives Prophet Allah protected piety, sister Hamna opposed perished among slander companions.) Al-Zuhri said: This is what reached us of the report of these people."
"20746","146 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Abdus ibn Kamil al-Sarraj from Muhammad ibn Abi Mashar from Abu Mashar from Aflah ibn Abdullah and Abi Rafi Ismail ibn Rafi from al-Zuhri, saying: narrated Said ibn al-Musayyib and Urwa ibn al-Zubayr and Alqama ibn Waqqas and Ubayd Allah ibn Abdullah ibn Utba, concerning the report of Aisha, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when the people of the slander (al-ifk) said about her what they said, and Allah exonerated her from what they said. Aisha said: The Messenger of Allah, when he intended a journey, would cast lots among his wives, and he mentioned something like it."
"20747","147 - Narrated al-Abbas ibn Muhammad al-Mujashii from Muhammad ibn Abi Yaqub al-Kirmani from Sahl ibn Yusuf from Salih ibn Abi al-Akhdar from al-Zuhri from Ubayd Allah ibn Abdullah ibn Utba and Abi Salama ibn Abd al-Rahman and Alqama ibn Waqqas and Urwa ibn al-Zubayr, concerning the report of Aisha, saying: When the companions of the slander said what they said and Allah exonerated her, each narrated to me a portion of her report; some of them were firmer in her report and better in relating precisely. They said: Aisha said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended a journey or direction, he would cast lots among his wives; whichever of them her lot came out, he took her out with him. Aisha said: He cast lots among us in a raid he undertook, and my lot came out in it, after the verse of the veil was sent down. I went out with him, carried in my howdah and set down in it. Women then were light. She said: The Messenger of Allah announced one night departure. I rose and passed beyond the army. When I finished, I came to my mount and felt my chest, finding a necklace of onyx beads fallen. I returned, and seeking it detained me. The party who mounted my howdah came, carried my howdah thinking I was in it, and mounted me on my camel I used to ride. Then they set out leading it. I came to their dwellings with neither caller nor answerer in them. I headed for my dwelling I had been in, knowing they would miss me and seek me. While I sat in my dwelling, my eye overcame me and I slept. Safwan ibn al-Muattal came, behind the army; he travelled early and reached my dwelling in the morning. He saw the black shape of a person and knew me; he used to see me before the veil came down. He said: To Allah we belong. I woke at his returning (Full Ifk narrative as in 20742: covering face with cloak, making camel kneel stepping hand mounting, leading to army, halted midday perishing, Abdullah greater burden, Madina month complaint unaware missing kindness How is that one, Umm Mistah al-Manasi night to night before privies early Arab custom open land harmed privies, setting out finishing stumble wrap May Mistah perish, rebuke Badr, learning slander added sickness, return home How is that one permission parents verify, mother radiant co-wives consolation, weeping night no tear no sleep, consultation Ali Usama delayed revelation separation, Usama wife only good affection, Ali not constrained many ask slave-girl truth, calling Barira suspicious, Barira No sent truth young sleeps dough sheep eats, pulpit excuse Abdullah: who excuses me harm family by Allah only good mentioned man only good entered only with me, Sad ibn Muadh excuse Aws neck Khazraj command, Sad ibn Ubada chief righteous zeal ignorant lie not kill nor can, Usayd cousin lie shall kill hypocrite disputing, tribes flared near fighting calming silent, weeping day parents thinking splits liver, Ansari woman weeping, Messenger sitting not sat since month no revelation, testifying Now then O Aisha such if innocent Allah exonerate if sinned seek forgiveness repent turns, tear dried, father know not, mother like, young little Quran heard settled believed innocent Allah knows not believe confess Allah knows innocent will believe, example father Yusuf patience beautiful sought help, turning bed hoping exoneration dream not recited slight, not moved sitting nor household out before heaviness sweat pearls cold day weight revelation, relieved laughing: O Aisha by Allah Allah exonerated you, mother rise, no by Allah nor praise but Allah, he who greater burden Abdullah ibn Ubayy ibn Salul, Abu Bakr spending Mistah kinship orphan: by Allah never spend, Allah sent And let not virtue swear verse Do you not love Allah forgive you, Abu Bakr yes Lord, returned spending.)"
"20748","148 - Narrated al-Mufaddal ibn Muhammad al-Janadî from Abu Huma Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Zubaydi from Abu Qurra Musa ibn Tariq, saying: mentioned Zama ibn Salih from Yaqub ibn Ata and Ziyad ibn Sad from Ibn Shihab from Said ibn al-Musayyib and Urwa ibn al-Zubayr and Alqama ibn Waqqas and Ubayd Allah ibn Abdullah ibn Utba ibn Masud, concerning the report of Aisha, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when the people of the slander said about her what they said, and Allah exonerated her. Each narrated to me a portion of her report, and part of their reports confirms part, though some of them retained her report better than others and were firmer in relating it. They mentioned that Aisha, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), said: The Messenger of Allah, when he intended to go out on a journey, would cast lots among his wives, and he mentioned the hadith."
"20749","149 - Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz and Abu Muslim al-Kashshi, both saying: narrated Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal from Hammad ibn Salama from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Aisha, that she said: People spoke of this matter and it spread. The Messenger of Allah stood concerning it as a preacher, while I was unaware of it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered with some of his companions upon a Nubian slave-girl of mine, and the Messenger of Allah said: What do you know of Aisha? She said: I know no fault in her except that she sleeps, and the sheep enters and eats her leaven and her mat. He said: I do not ask you about this. She said: Then, well. When she understood, she said: Glory be to Allah; I know of Aisha only as the goldsmith knows of red gold. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out, praised Allah and extolled Him, then said: Advise me, company of Muslims, concerning people who accused my family; I knew in them no evil ever, and they accused them with one by Allah I knew in him no evil ever. I never stayed away but he was with me, nor entered my house ever but while I was present. Sad ibn Muadh said: I see their necks be struck. A man of al-Khazraj said: You lie; by Allah, had they been of your clan, you would not command killing them. Hassan was of al-Khazraj, until there was between al-Aws and al-Khazraj in the mosque an incident. Among those who spread it were Hassan ibn Thabit, and Mistah ibn Uthatha, and Hamna bint Jahsh, among others. He who spoke of it with Abdullah ibn Ubayy would hear it, spread it, and broadcast it. Aisha said: One night I went out with Umm Mistah for my need. While we walked, she stumbled and said: May Mistah perish. I said: Glory be (Full Ifk continuation: learning report, seeking parents, illness, consultation, pulpit, dispute, revelation heaviness, glad tidings: Allah sent excuse, parents: rise kiss head Messenger, I: praise Allah not you, Mistah orphan in Abu Bakr care oath not spend, And let not virtue swear to end verse, Hassan defended Messenger defended, what punishment greater than loss of eyes, great punishment.)"
"20750","150 - Narrated Abd al-Rahman ibn Salim al-Razi, saying: narrated Sahl ibn Uthman from Abu Usama from Hisham ibn Urwa, saying: My father narrated to me from Aisha, saying: When what was mentioned of my affair was mentioned, and I did not know, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood concerning me as a preacher. He testified, praised Allah and extolled Him as He deserves, then said: Now then, advise me concerning people who accused my family. By Allah, I knew in my family no evil ever. They accused them with one by Allah I knew in him no evil ever; he never entered my house but while I was present, nor was I absent on a journey but he was absent with me. Sad ibn Muadh stood and said: We see, O Messenger of Allah, that we strike their necks. A man of al-Khazraj stood; the mother of Hassan ibn Thabit was of that man’s clan. He said: You lie; by Allah, had they been of al-Aws, you would not love their necks be struck, until there nearly was between al-Aws and al-Khazraj in the mosque evil, and I did not know of it. In the evening of that day I went out for some need of mine, with Umm Mistah. She stumbled and said: May Mistah perish. I rebuked her, saying: Do you revile your son? She said: By Allah, I revile him only concerning you. I said: Concerning what of me? She disclosed the report. I said: Did this happen? She said: Yes, by Allah. I returned home as if what I went out for I had not gone out for; I found in it neither little nor much, and fell ill. I said to the Messenger of Allah: Send me to my father’s house. He sent the boy with me. I entered the house and found Umm Ruman below, Abu Bakr above the house reciting. My mother said: What brought you, O my little daughter? I told her and mentioned the report to her, and it had not reached from her what reached from me (Full Ifk continuation: pulpit excuse, Aws-Khazraj, Umm Mistah stumble learning, sickness, parents, Barira questioned sleeps dough sheep eats, Zaynab guarded hearing sight knew only good protected piety, sister Hamna fought perished among perished, speakers Mistah Hassan Hamna hypocrite Abdullah ibn Ubayy ibn Salul who spreads gathers them who took greater burden, Abu Bakr oath never benefit Mistah, And let not virtue affluence to end: meaning Abu Bakr give relatives needy meaning Mistah Do you not love Allah forgive you Forgiving Merciful, Abu Bakr yes Lord we love forgiven returned benefiting.)"
"20751","151 - Narrated Ali ibn al-Mubarak al-Sanani and Ubayd Allah ibn Muhammad al-Umari, both saying: narrated Ismail ibn Abi Uways, saying: narrated my father, saying: narrated Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Aisha bint Abi Bakr al-Siddiq. Abu Uways said: Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm al-Ansari al-Najjari also narrated to me from Amra bint Abd al-Rahman al-Ansariyya then al-Najjariyya from Aisha, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended to travel a journey, he would cast lots among his wives; whichever of them her lot came out, he went out with her. The lot of Aisha came out in the Prophet’s (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) expedition to Banu al-Mustaliq of Khuzaa. When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned back and was near Madina, Aisha being a young girl, slight of flesh, light, keeping to her curtained seat. When people intended departure, she would go, perform ablution, return, enter her litter; her camel would be saddled, then her litter carried and placed on the camel. The first the hypocrites and others who shared concerning Aisha said was that she went out to perform ablution when they drew near Madina. A necklace of hers of onyx beads slipped from her neck. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and people departed while she sought the necklace, unaware of their departure. They fastened her litter on her camel, seeing she was in it as she had been. Aisha returned to her dwelling and found no one in the camp; her eyes overcame her. Safwan ibn al-Muattal al-Sulami, companion of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), lagged behind the army (Full Ifk continuation: leading her on mount reaching army midday, perishing slanderers, Abdullah greater burden, Madina month illness unaware missing kindness How is that one, Umm Mistah night Manasi, stumble May Mistah perish, rebuke Badr witness, learning added sickness, permission parents verify, mother radiant co-wives, weeping no tear no sleep, consultation Ali Usama delayed revelation separation, Usama wife only good, Ali not constrained many ask slave-girl truth, Barira No sent truth young sleeps dough sheep eats, pulpit excuse Abdullah harm family only good mentioned man only good entered only with me, Sad excuse Aws neck Khazraj command, Sad Ubada chief righteous zeal ignorant lie not kill nor can, Usayd cousin lie shall kill hypocrite disputing, tribes flared near fighting calming silent, weeping day parents splits liver, Ansari woman weeping, Messenger sitting not sat since month no revelation, testifying Now then such if innocent exonerate if sinned seek forgiveness repent turns, tear dried, father know not, mother like, young little Quran heard settled believed innocent Allah knows not believe confess Allah knows innocent will believe, example father Yusuf patience beautiful sought help, turning bed hoping dream vision not recited slight, not moved sitting nor household out before heaviness pearls sweat cold weight Quran, relieved laughing Allah exonerated, by Allah’s praise not yours, mother rise, no nor praise but Allah, verses slander ten, Abu Bakr oath Mistah kinship poverty never after Aisha, And let not virtue swear Do you not love forgive, yes love forgive returned, Messenger asking Zaynab guarded hearing sight only good rivalled protected piety sister Hamna opposed perished, poems said about slander companions, Abu Bakr to Mistah called Awf in accusing Aisha, Umm Sad ibn Muadh poetry about accusers, Hassan exonerating Aisha apologizing: Razan tuzannu ghartha wife, Abu Uways father: Messenger commanded those who accused Aisha flogged hadd all eighty eighty, Hassan poetry when flogged and Mistah.)"
"20752","152 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani from his father from Attab ibn Bashir from Khusayf from Miqsam from Aisha, saying: Umm Mistah came to me, and we went out to Hayr Ad. Umm Mistah stepped on a bone or thorn and said: May Mistah perish. I said: Evil is what you said, a man among the companions of the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him. She said: I bear witness you are of the heedless believing women. Do you know what has flown against you? I said: No, by Allah. She said: When was the Messenger of Allah’s last time with you? I said: The Messenger of Allah does with his wives as he loves; he begins with whom he loves of them and comes to whom he loves. She said: He has covered you with such-and-such. I fell unconscious. It reached Umm Ruman, my mother. When the matter reached her, she came to me, carried me, and took me to her house. It reached the Messenger of Allah that the matter had reached Aisha. He came to her, entered upon her, sat with her, and said: O Aisha, Allah has widened repentance. My evil increased to what was in me. While we were so, Abu Bakr came, entered upon me, and said: O Messenger of Allah, what do you wait with this one who betrayed you and exposed me? She said: My evil increased to evil. She said: He sent for Ali and said: O Ali, what do you see concerning Aisha? He said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: You shall surely tell me what you see concerning Aisha. He said: Allah has widened women, but send for Barira, her maid, and ask her; perhaps she looked upon something of her affair. He sent for Barira; she came. He said to her: Do you bear witness I am the Messenger of Allah? She said: Yes. He said: I ask you about something, so do not conceal from me. She said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah; nothing you ask me about but I shall tell you of it, nor conceal from you, Allah willing, anything. He said: You were with Aisha; did you see in her what you dislike? She said: No, by Him who sent you with prophethood; I saw in her, since I was with her, only one trait. He said: What is it? She said: I kneaded dough of mine and said to Aisha: Guard this dough until I fetch fire to bake. She stood praying, neglected the leaven, and a sheep came and ate it. He sent for Usama and said: O Usama, what do you see concerning Aisha? He said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: You shall surely tell me what you see in her. He said: I see you keep her until Allah brings something to you concerning her. She said: Only a little passed before revelation came down. The joy kept seen on the face of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Her excuse came from heaven, meaning from Allah. The Messenger of Allah said: Receive glad tidings, O Aisha, then receive glad tidings, O Aisha; Allah informed me of your excuse. I said: Without your praise and the praise of your companion. She said: Then I spoke. When he came to her, he would say: How is that one?"
"20753","153 - Narrated Abdan ibn Ahmad from Zayd ibn al-Harish from Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad al-Muharibi from Abu Sad al-Baqqal from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Aswad from his father al-Aswad, saying: I said: O Mother of the Believers, or O my mother, will you not narrate to me how it was, meaning the affair of the slander? She said: The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, married me while I splashed rain in Mecca, with nothing in me men desire, a girl of six years. When it reached me that he married me, Allah cast shyness upon me. Then the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, emigrated, I with him. I was taken to him when he came to me, a girl of nine years. The Messenger of Allah travelled a journey, taking me out with him. I was light, in a curtained litter of mine with covers upon it. When they departed, I sat upon it and they carried, I in it. They fastened it on the camel’s back. They halted a halt; I went out for my need. I returned, and they had hastened departure. I sat in the litter, and they had seen me when I moved the covers. When I sat in it, I struck my hand on my chest, and I had forgotten a necklace with me. I went out hurrying, seeking it. I returned, and the people had gone; I saw only dust from afar. They had placed the litter on the camel’s back, seeing me only as in it, for what they saw of my lightness. There was a man holding his camel’s head. I said: Who is the man? He said: Safwan ibn al-Muattal al-Sulami. Are you the Mother of the Believers? I said: Yes. He said: To Allah we belong and to Him we return. I said: Turn your face from me and put your foot on your camel’s foreleg. He said: I shall, gladly and honorably. She said: I caught the people when they halted. He went and placed me at the litter. People looked at me, and I was unaware. She said: I denied the kindness of my parents, denied the Messenger of Allah, knowing not what had been said, until my maid or foster-daughter entered and said: Such. She said: A man of the Emigrants said to me: How heedless are you? A shivering fever seized me. My mother took every garment in the house and threw it over me. The Messenger of Allah consulted some of his companions, saying: What do you see? Some said: Women are many, and you can replace. Some said: You are the Messenger of Allah, revelation comes down upon you, and our affair follows your command. Some said: By Allah, Allah shall surely clarify it, so do not hasten. She said: My father’s face became as if arsenic poured upon it. She said: The Messenger of Allah entered upon me and saw what was in me. He said: What is with this one? My mother said: Of what you said and was said. He did not speak, saying nothing. She said: That added to what was in me. She said: He came to me and said: Fear Allah, O Aisha; if you committed anything of this, repent to Allah, for Allah accepts repentance from His servants and pardons evil deeds. She said: I sought the name of Yaqub and could not get it. I said, only saying as the father of Yusuf said: So patience is beautiful, and Allah is the One sought for help against what you describe. I only complain my grief and sorrow to Allah, and I know from Allah what you do not know. She said: While the Messenger of Allah was with his companions, his face as if arsenic dissolved upon it, revelation came down upon him. When revelation came to him, he would not blink. His companions knew he was receiving revelation, gazing at his face, beaming and brightening. When revelation ended, he said: Receive glad tidings, O Abu Bakr; Allah sent down your daughter’s excuse and innocence. He went to her and gave her tidings. She said: He recited to him what came down concerning me. She said: Abu Bakr came hurrying, almost falling. She said: I said: By the praise of Allah, not by the praise of your companion you came from. The Messenger of Allah came, sat at my head, took my palm, and I pulled my hands from him. Abu Bakr struck me, saying: Do you pull your hand from the Messenger of Allah? With the Messenger of Allah you do this? The Messenger of Allah laughed. She said: This was my affair."
"20754","154 - Narrated Ahmad ibn Amr al-Khallal al-Makki from Muhammad ibn Abi Umar al-Adani from Sufyan ibn Uyayna from Abi Sad al-Awar from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Aswad from his father from Aisha, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married me while I was wearing a hawf, and no sooner had he married me than Allah cast shyness upon me, and the hawf. He said: And al-hawf is something the Arabs make for children, of amulets, which the Bedouins dress their children with."
"20755","155 - Narrated Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal from his father, saying: narrated Hushaym from Umar ibn Abi Salama from his father from Aisha, saying: When my exoneration came down from heaven, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came and informed me of that, so I said: By the praise of Allah, not by your praise."
"20756","156 - Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Yahya ibn Abd al-Hamid al-Himmani from Abu Awana from Umar ibn Abi Salama from his father from Aisha, saying: I was accused of what I was accused of while I was heedless. While the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was sitting with me, he then sat upright and wiped his face, and said: O Aisha, receive glad tidings. I said: By the praise of Allah, not by your praise. Then he recited to me these verses: Those who accuse chaste, heedless, believing women, the verses."
"20757","157 - Narrated Ahmad ibn al-Qasim ibn Musawir al-Jawhari from Khalid ibn Khidash from Hammad ibn Zayd from Ayyub from Ibn Abi Mulayka from Aisha, saying: When what they said reached me, I intended to go to a well and throw myself into it."
"20758","158 - Narrated Abdullah ibn al-Hasan al-Harrani Abu Shuayb from Abdullah ibn Jafar al-Raqqi from Ibn al-Mubarak from Malik ibn Mighwal from Abi Husayn from Mujahid from Aisha, saying: When it was said, and he mentioned it."
"20759","159 - Narrated al-Qasim ibn Abbad al-Khattabi from Muhammad ibn Humayd al-Razi from Salama ibn al-Fadl and Ibrahim ibn al-Mukhtar from Muhammad ibn Ishaq from Yahya ibn Abbad ibn Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr from his father from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her), saying: When what happened concerning my necklace happened, the people of the slander said what they said. Then I went out with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on another expedition and my necklace fell off again, so seeking it detained the people, and dawn broke. I met from Abu Bakr what Allah willed, and he said to me: O my little daughter, on a journey you become toil and trial, while the people have no water with them. So Allah sent down the concession of tayammum. Then Abu Bakr said: By Allah, O my little daughter, indeed you are, as far as I know, blessed."
"20760","160 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Isa ibn Shayba al-Misri, saying: narrated Said ibn Yahya ibn Said al-Umawi, saying: narrated my father from Ibn Ishaq from Yahya ibn Abbad ibn Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr from his father from Aisha, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called Ali and Usama and consulted them. As for Usama, he said good, saying: Your wife, and we know only good; this is falsehood and lying. As for Ali, he said: Women are many, and you are able to find out; ask the slave-girl, for she will tell the truth. And he mentioned the report of the slander."
"20761","161 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Ali al-Saigh from Said ibn Mansur from Suwayd ibn Abd al-Aziz from Husayn from Abi Wail from Masruq, that Umm Ruman narrated to them, saying: While I was sitting with Aisha, a woman of the Ansar came and said: May Allah do to so-and-so and so-and-so, and do. Aisha said: What is it? She said: Such-and-such. She said: Did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) hear it? She said: Yes. She said: And Abu Bakr? She said: Yes. She said: Then Aisha fell unconscious, and she did not recover except with a fever with shivering. Clothes were thrown over her. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came while she was lying down, and said: What is the matter with her? They said: Fever with shivering seized her. He said: Perhaps it is concerning a report she was told. Then Aisha sat up and said: By Allah, if I swear, you will not believe me, and if I excuse myself, you will not excuse me. My example and yours is only the example of Yaqub and his sons: And Allah is the One sought for help against what you describe. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out, and her exoneration came down. She said: By the praise of Allah, not by your praise, or she said: By the praise of Allah, not by the praise of anyone."
"20762","162 - Narrated Salama ibn Ibrahim ibn Ismail ibn Yahya ibn Salama ibn Kuhayl from his father from his father from his grandfather from Salama ibn Kuhayl from al-Hasan al-Arabi from Ibn Abbas, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he travelled, would travel with some of his wives and divide among them. So he travelled with Aisha bint Abi Bakr, and she had a howdah, and the howdah had men who carried it and set it down. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his companions halted for rest, and Aisha went out for her need and went far, and she was not noticed. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) woke while the people had departed, and those who carried the howdah came and carried it, knowing only that she was in it. They travelled, and Aisha came and found them departed, so she sat in her place. A man of the Ansar called Safwan ibn al-Muattal woke, and he did not approach women. He drew near her, with him a camel of his. When he saw her, he carried her, and he had seen her before the veil. He led the camel with her until they reached the people, with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and with him Aisha, and they spoke much. That reached the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and it grieved him until he withdrew from her. He consulted concerning her Zayd ibn Thabit and others. He said: O Messenger of Allah, leave her; perhaps Allah will bring about something for you concerning her. Ali ibn Abi Talib said: Women are many. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took it to heart against her. One night Aisha went out walking among women, and Umm Mistah stumbled and said: May Mistah perish. Aisha said: Evil is what you said; do you say this of a man among the companions of the Messenger of Allah? She said: You do not know what they say. And she informed her of the report. Then Aisha fell unconscious. Then the Quran came down with her exoneration in Surat al-Nur: Those who brought the slander are a band among you, until it reached: And he who took upon himself the greater part of it will have a great punishment. And there came down: And let not those of virtue among you swear, to His saying: And Allah is Forgiving, Merciful. Abu Bakr used to give to Mistah, show him kindness and maintain him, and he was among those who spoke most against Aisha. So Abu Bakr swore not to give him anything. Then this verse came down: Do you not love that Allah should forgive you. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded him to go to her and give her glad tidings. Abu Bakr came and informed her of her exoneration and what Allah had sent down. She said: Not by your praise, nor by the praise of your companion."
"20763","163 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Idris ibn Asim al-Jammal from Ibrahim ibn Salih ibn Harb from Ismail ibn Yahya al-Taymi from Abu Mashar al-Madani from Muhammad ibn Qays from Abi al-Yasar al-Ansari, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Aisha: O Aisha, Allah has sent down your exoneration. She said: By the praise of Allah, and not by your praise. The Messenger of Allah went out from Aisha and sent for Abdullah ibn Ubayy and struck him two hadd punishments, and sent for Mistah and Hamna and struck them."
"20764","164 - Narrated Abd al-Rahman ibn Khallad al-Dawraqi from Sadan ibn Zakariyya al-Dawraqi, saying: narrated Ismail ibn Yahya ibn Abdullah al-Taymi from Ibn Abi Dhib from Nafi from Ibn Umar, saying: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended a journey, he would cast lots among his wives thrice; whom the lot struck, he took them out with him. They would go out giving water and treating the wounded. When he raided Banu al-Mustaliq, he cast lots among them; the lot struck Aisha and Umm Salama, and he took them out with him. On part of the road, the saddle of Umm Salama tilted, so they made her camel kneel to mend her saddle. Aisha intended to relieve her need. When they made their camels kneel, Aisha said: I said in myself: Until they mend Umm Salama’s saddle, I shall relieve my need. She said: I came down from the howdah, took water in a bucket, they not knowing my descent, came to a ruin, and my necklace broke. I was held back returning it and arranging it. The people sent their camels and went on, thinking I was in the howdah, not descended. Aisha said: I returned and saw no one. She said: I followed them until I was exhausted. I said in myself: The people will miss me and return seeking me. She said: I stood on part of the road. Safwan passed by me (Full Ifk continuation variant: recognizing before veil, leading on camel reaching people, much speech, grief withdrawal, consulting, revelation, exoneration verses, Abu Bakr oath spending, Zaynab Barira testimonies, pulpit Aws-Khazraj dispute, flogging eighty, repentance, Abdullah hypocrite died hypocrisy, foul-good verses exegesis: foul speech foul people good speech good people, foul women Abdullah wife foul men Abdullah, good men Prophet good women Aisha wives, exonerated forgiveness noble provision, Aisha among wives Paradise.)"
"20765","165 - Narrated Zakariyya ibn Yahya al-Saji and Ahmad ibn Zuhayr al-Tustari, both saying: narrated Muhammad ibn Bashshar from Umar ibn Khalifa al-Bakrawi from Muhammad ibn Amr from Abi Salama from Abu Hurayra, saying: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he intended a journey, would cast lots among his wives."
"20766","166 - Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal from Hammad ibn Salama from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Aisha, saying: The man about whom what was said was said, said: By Allah, I have never uncovered since I was female, ever. And he was killed a martyr in the way of Allah. And Urwa would not name him."
"20767","167 - Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal from Hammad ibn Salama from Ali ibn Zayd ibn Judan, that Safwan ibn al-Muattal vowed to strike Hassan ibn Thabit with the sword a blow."
"20768","168 - Narrated Bakr ibn Sahl al-Dimyati from Abd al-Ghani ibn Said al-Thaqafi, saying: narrated Musa ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Sanani from Ibn Jurayj from Ata from Ibn Abbas, and from Muqatil ibn Sulayman from al-Dahhak ibn Muzahim from Ibn Abbas, concerning: Those who brought the slander are a band among you, meaning those who brought slander, meaning lying against Aisha, Mother of the Believers, four of you. Do not think it evil for you; rather it is good for you, meaning good for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), exoneration for the chief lady of believing women, good for Abu Bakr, mother of Aisha, and Safwan ibn al-Muattal. For every person among them what he earned of sin, and he who took upon himself the greater part thereof, meaning spreading it among them, meaning Abdullah ibn Ubayy ibn Salul. For him is a great punishment, meaning in this world; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) flogged him eighty, and in the Hereafter his end is to the Fire. Why, when you heard it, meaning: why then, when you heard it; The believing men and believing women thought good of themselves and said: This is a manifest slander. That is because the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) consulted (Full tafsir continuation covering 20773-20859 themes in one chain: Usama Zaynab Barira wives said lying falsehood great lie, four witnesses else liars lying itself, favor mercy veil else touched lying endless, receiving tongues saying mouths no knowledge Allah knows opposite thought light with Allah tremendous accusing chief lady Mothers Believers wife Messenger attributing never in her never heart created pure protected foul, narrating some from others accusation without knowing true burden, Aisha reciting hastening lying knows best sent about her, why not say not for us speak glory You great slander as Sad ibn Muadh like Maryam fabrication, Allah admonishes never return if believers Mistah Hamna Hassan believing Allah Messenger, makes clear verses exoneration Knowing remorse Wise ruled eighty, love indecency spread chaste believers painful grievous world flogging Hereafter Fire Allah knows severity wrath you know not, appears report Aisha, adultery, accusing Aisha Safwan Abdullah world hadd Hereafter Fire, adultery appears Fire, favor mercy remorse Mistah Hamna Hassan Kind mercy regretted returned truth, punish said Aisha Kind pardoned not punished accusation, not follow Satan slips adornment commands indecency disobedience wrong disliked none purified never accept repentance but purifies wills turned Hearing Knowing saying remorse repentance, indecency disobediences unknown like said Aisha blessing none righteous but makes righteous wills, let not virtue affluence swear give relatives needy emigrants pardon overlook placed virtue affluence knowledge Allah kinship kind Mistah kinship emigration need scenes approved Badr forgive Mistah Forgiving errs Merciful allies, some companions accused spread swore Abu Bakr not charity maintain swore not join kin give wealth before commanded forgive pardon, Quraysh Mistah kin orphan care spread exoneration excuse swore not sustain Do you not love forgive yes pardon overlook no doubt not withhold kindness before today, virtue wealth affluence provision Abu Bakr pardon overlook Mistah aunt son poor emigrants Do you not love Abu Bakr forgive sins Merciful believers Prophet: Do you not love forgive yes Messenger pardon overlooked not withhold after today, accusing chaste heedless believing cursed world Hereafter: everyone accusing chaste Allah curses No only Aisha, adultery severer accusation only Aisha specifically, accusing adultery private chaste heedless indecencies Aisha truthful cursed punished flogged eighty world Hereafter Abdullah hypocrite Fire great Prophet flogged Hassan Abdullah Mistah Hamna each eighty accusing Aisha repented after except Abdullah died hypocrisy, wives Prophet specifically, Aisha may Allah pleased, chaste chaste affirming oneness messengers cursed removed faith, wives Prophet hypocrisy accused curse anger incurred Allah all wives Prophet, wives Prophet whoever accuses Muslim woman sinner or repents, Aisha wives Prophet no repentance whoever, accusing believing women other than wives repentance: accusing chaste then not bring four witnesses flog eighty never accept testimony defiantly disobedient Except repent after amend Forgiving Merciful, repentance accusing believers no repentance accusing wives Prophet, cursed world Hereafter great some rise kiss head excellence explanation, On Day pay just due people truth truth falsehood falsehood know Manifest Truth, recompense deeds truth allies reward enemies punishment know Resurrection Manifest Truth Abdullah doubts head hypocrites doubt cut certain where not benefit, Hereafter just reckoning not wrong Manifest Justice, Foul foul example Abdullah doubts Allah accuses chief lady Muslims, Good good Aisha Allah made good Messenger Jibril silk before formed mother womb: Aisha bint Abi Bakr wife world wife Hereafter compensation Khadija bint Khuwaylid at death delighted cooled eye, Good men Messenger Allah made good Himself chief children Adam, Good women Aisha, exonerated lying Abdullah, forgiveness protection world forgiveness Hereafter, noble provision Paradise great reward.)"
"20769","Chapter on the interpretation of Allah Almighty’s saying: Those who brought the slander. 169 - Narrated Bakr ibn Sahl from Abd al-Ghani ibn Said from Musa ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Sanani from Ata from Ibn Jurayj from Ibn Abbas, and from Muqatil ibn Sulayman from al-Dahhak ibn Muzahim from Ibn Abbas, concerning: Those who brought the slander are a band among you, meaning: they brought lying against Aisha, four of you."
"20770","170 - Narrated Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Said ibn Abi Maryam from Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi from Warqa from Ibn Abi Najih from Mujahid, concerning His saying: Those who brought the slander are a band among you, he said: The companions of Aisha."
"20771","171 - Narrated Amr ibn Abi al-Tahir ibn al-Sarh from Yahya ibn Bukayr from Ibn Lahia from Ata ibn Dinar from Said ibn Jubayr, concerning: Those who brought the slander, meaning: with lying; A band among you, meaning: Abdullah ibn Ubayy ibn Salul the hypocrite, and Hassan ibn Thabit, and Mistah ibn Uthatha, and Hamna bint Jahsh."
"20772","172 - Narrated Dawud ibn Muhammad ibn Salih al-Marwazi from al-Abbas ibn al-Walid al-Narsi from Yazid ibn Zuray from Said ibn Abi Aruba from Qatada, concerning the saying of Allah Almighty: Those who brought the slander, meaning: lying. He said: This is concerning Aisha, Mother of the Believers, and what was spread about her and said to her."
"20773","Chapter on the interpretation of His saying: Do not think it evil for you; rather it is good for you. 173 - Narrated Bakr ibn Sahl from Abd al-Ghani ibn Said al-Thaqafi from Musa ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Sanani from Ibn Jurayj from Ata from Ibn Abbas, and from Muqatil ibn Sulayman from al-Dahhak from Ibn Abbas, concerning: Do not think it evil for you; rather it is good for you, meaning: good for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and exoneration for the chief lady of the believing women, and good for Abu Bakr, and the mother of Aisha, and Safwan ibn al-Muattal. Do not think what lying reached you as evil for you; rather it is good for you, for you are rewarded for it."
"20774","Chapter on the interpretation of Allah’s saying: For every person among them what he earned of sin. 174 - Narrated Amr ibn Abi al-Tahir ibn al-Sarh al-Misri from Yahya ibn Bukayr from Ibn Lahia from Ata ibn Dinar from Said ibn Jubayr, concerning: For every person among them what he earned of sin, he said: Whoever spoke concerning the affair of Aisha, according to how much he spoke about her affair."
"20775","Chapter on the interpretation of Allah’s saying: And he who took upon himself the greater part thereof, the verse. 175 - Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Abu Hudhayfa from Sufyan from al-Amash from Abi al-Duha from Masruq, that he entered upon Aisha while Hassan ibn Thabit was with her reciting poetry to her, verses: Razan tuzannu ghartha, and he said to her: Do you let this one enter upon you while he is among those who took upon themselves its greater part? She said: I have not seen him but that a great punishment befell him."
"20776","176 - Narrated Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal from his father from Muhammad ibn Jafar from Shuba from Sulayman al-Amash, saying: I heard Aba al-Duha narrating from Masruq, saying: I entered upon Aisha while Hassan ibn Thabit was with her reciting poetry to her, eulogizing with verses of his: Razan tuzannu ghartha. Aisha said to him: I am not like that. Masruq said: So I said to her: Do you permit him to enter upon you though Allah said: And he who took upon himself the greater part thereof will have a great punishment? She said: And what punishment is severer than blindness? And she said: He defends, or he lampoon-praises, on behalf of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"20777","177 - Narrated Abd al-Rahman ibn Salim al-Razi from Sahl ibn Uthman from Abida ibn Humayd and Hafs ibn Ghiyath from al-Amash from Muslim ibn Subayh from Masruq, saying: Hassan ibn Thabit entered upon Aisha saying: Razan tuzannu ghartha. It was said to her: O Mother of the Believers, does not Allah say: And he who took upon himself the greater part thereof will have a great punishment? Aisha said: Do you not see him in a great punishment? Hassan had lost his eyesight."
"20778","178 - Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Yahya al-Himmani from Abu Muawiya from al-Amash from Muslim ibn Subayh from Masruq from Aisha, saying: Hassan ibn Thabit was among those who took upon themselves its greater part. It was said to her: Do you admit this one to yourself while he is among those about whom Allah said: And he who took upon himself the greater part thereof will have a great punishment? Aisha said: Is he not in a great punishment? His eyesight has gone."
"20779","179 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Ali al-Saigh al-Makki from Said ibn Mansur from Abu Muawiya from al-Amash from Muslim ibn Subayh from Masruq, saying: Hassan ibn Thabit entered upon Aisha after his eyesight was taken, and it was said to her: Do you admit to yourself this one about whom Allah said: And he who took upon himself the greater part thereof will have a great punishment, in the reading of Abdullah? She said: Is he not in punishment? His eyesight has been taken. Then he recited to her a verse he said in his house: Razan tuzannu ghartha."
"20780","180 - Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Abu Nuaym from Sufyan from Mamar from al-Zuhri from Urwa from Aisha, saying: He who took upon himself its greater part is Abdullah ibn Ubayy."
"20781","181 - Narrated Bakr ibn Sahl from Abd al-Ghani ibn Said al-Thaqafi from Musa ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Sanani from Ibn Jurayj from Ata from Ibn Abbas, and from Muqatil ibn Sulayman from al-Dahhak from Ibn Abbas, concerning: And he who took upon himself the greater part thereof: meaning spreading it and broadcasting it; among them, meaning Abdullah ibn Ubayy ibn Salul; For him is a great punishment, meaning in this world the flogging; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) flogged him eighty, and in the Hereafter his end is to the Fire."
"20782","182 - Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz from Arim Abu al-Numan (H) and narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Yahya al-Himmani, both saying: narrated Hammad ibn Zayd from Hisham ibn Urwa, saying: He who took upon himself its greater part is Abdullah ibn Ubayy ibn Salul, and Mistah ibn Uthatha, and Hassan ibn Thabit, and Hamna bint Jahsh, and most of that was from Abdullah ibn Ubayy."
"20783","183 - Narrated Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Said ibn Abi Maryam from Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi from Warqa from Ibn Abi Najih from Mujahid, concerning His saying: And he who took upon himself the greater part thereof will have a great punishment, he said: Abdullah ibn Ubayy ibn Salul."
"20784","184 - Narrated Amr ibn Abi al-Tahir ibn al-Sarh from Yahya ibn Bukayr from Ibn Lahia from Ata ibn Dinar from Said ibn Jubayr, concerning: And he who took upon himself the greater part thereof, meaning: its enormity; among them, meaning: the accusers, and he is Ibn Ubayy, head of the hypocrites; he is the one who said: She is not cleared of him, nor he of her. For him is a great punishment. In this verse is a lesson for all Muslims: if there is a sin among them, whoever assists in it by deed or speech, or alludes to it, or is pleased with it, or approves, then he shares in that sin according to what he did of it. And if there is a sin among the Muslims, whoever witnessed and hated it is like the absent, and whoever was absent and approved is like the witness."
"20785","185 - Narrated Dawud ibn Muhammad ibn Salih al-Marwazi from al-Abbas ibn al-Walid al-Narsi from Yazid ibn Zuray from Said ibn Abi Aruba from Qatada, concerning His saying: And he who took upon himself the greater part thereof will have a great punishment, he said: He who took upon himself its greater part is Hassan ibn Thabit and Mistah ibn Uthatha."
"20786","Chapter on the interpretation of His Almighty saying: Why, when you heard it, the verse. 186 - Reported Bakr ibn Sahl from Abd al-Ghani ibn Said al-Thaqafi from Musa ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Sanani from Ibn Jurayj from Ata from Ibn Abbas, and from Muqatil ibn Sulayman from al-Dahhak ibn Muzahim from Ibn Abbas, concerning: Why, when you heard it, meaning: why then, when you heard it; The believing men and the believing women thought good of themselves and said: This is a manifest slander. That is because the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) consulted concerning her, and they said good, saying: O Messenger of Allah, this is lying and falsehood. And the believing women, meaning Zaynab bint Jahsh and Barira, freedwoman of Aisha, and all the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), said: This is a great lie."
"20787","187 - Narrated Amr ibn al-Tahir ibn Abi al-Sarh from Yahya from Ibn Lahia from Ata ibn Dinar from Said ibn Jubayr, concerning: Why, when you heard it, meaning: the accusation of Aisha and Safwan, why did you not deny it? Why did not: The believing men and the believing women, for among them is Zaynab bint Jahsh; Think good of themselves, why did not some of them think good of others, for they did not see this; And say: This is slander, why did they not say: This accusation is a manifest lie?"
"20788","188 - Narrated Dawud ibn Muhammad ibn Salih al-Marwazi from al-Abbas ibn al-Walid al-Narsi from Yazid ibn Zuray from Said ibn Abi Aruba from Qatada, concerning His saying: Why, when you heard it: You denied and said: This is a manifest lie. By my life, that you not lie against your Muslim brother with evil when you hear it is better for you, and safer, than that you spread it, broadcast it, and believe it."
"20789","189 - Narrated Ali ibn al-Mubarak al-Sanani from Zayd ibn al-Mubarak from Muhammad ibn Thawr from Ibn Jurayj, concerning His saying: Why, when you heard it, the believing men and the believing women thought, he says: Some of them say to others: Will you not listen to his saying?"
"20790","His saying: Why did they not bring upon it four witnesses, the verse. 190 - Narrated Bakr ibn Sahl from Abd al-Ghani from Musa ibn Abd al-Rahman from Ibn Jurayj from Ata from Ibn Abbas, concerning: Why did they not bring upon it four witnesses, else they and those who bore witness would be liars; And when they did not bring the witnesses, then those, with Allah, are the liars, meaning lying itself."
"20791","191 - Narrated Amr ibn Abi al-Tahir ibn al-Sarh from Yahya ibn Bukayr from Ibn Lahia from Ata ibn Dinar from Said ibn Jubayr, concerning His saying: Why did they not bring upon it, meaning: upon the accusation, Four witnesses, and when they did not bring the witnesses, then those, with Allah, are the liars, meaning: those who accused Aisha."
"20792","192 - Narrated Amr ibn Abi al-Tahir ibn al-Sarh al-Misri from Said ibn Abi Maryam from Rushdayn ibn Sad from Abi Sakhr, concerning: Why did they not bring upon it four witnesses, and when they did not bring the witnesses, then those, with Allah, are the liars: Everyone who accuses a Muslim and then does not bring four witnesses is an accuser, upon him is the hadd of accusation."
"20793","His saying: And had it not been for the favor of Allah upon you and His mercy in this world and the Hereafter, there would have touched you for what you indulged in a great punishment. 193 - Narrated Bakr ibn Sahl from Abd al-Ghani ibn Said al-Thaqafi from Musa ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Sanani from Ibn Jurayj from Ata from Ibn Abbas, and from Muqatil ibn Sulayman from al-Dahhak ibn Muzahim from Ibn Abbas, concerning: And had it not been for the favor of Allah upon you and His mercy in this world and the Hereafter: meaning, had it not been for what Allah favored you with and veiled you; There would have touched you for what you indulged in: meaning of lying; A great punishment: meaning with no end."
"20794","194 - Narrated Amr ibn Abi al-Tahir ibn al-Sarh from Yahya ibn Bukayr from Ibn Lahia from Ata ibn Dinar from Said ibn Jubayr, concerning: And had it not been for the favor of Allah upon you and His mercy in this world and the Hereafter, in it is precedence; he says: Had it not been for the favor of Allah upon you and His mercy in this world and the Hereafter, there would have touched you, meaning: there would have afflicted you; For what you indulged in, meaning: for what you said of accusation; A great punishment, there would have afflicted you of punishment in this world and the Hereafter."
"20795","195 - Narrated Dawud ibn Muhammad ibn Salih al-Marwazi from al-Abbas ibn al-Walid al-Narsi from Yazid ibn Zuray, saying: narrated Said ibn Abi Aruba from Qatada, concerning His saying: And had it not been for the favor of Allah upon you and His mercy in this world and the Hereafter, there would have touched you for what you indulged in a great punishment, he said: This is concerning Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) and what was said; the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) nearly perished in it."
"20796","Chapter on the interpretation of His saying: When you received it with your tongues and said with your mouths, the verse. 196 - Narrated Bakr from Abd al-Ghani from Musa from Ibn Jurayj from Ata from Ibn Abbas, and from Muqatil ibn Sulayman from al-Dahhak from Ibn Abbas, concerning: When you received it with your tongues and said with your mouths what you have no knowledge of, he says: Allah knows the opposite of it; And you thought it insignificant while it is with Allah tremendous, he says: That you accuse the chief lady of the Mothers of the Believers and wife of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), attributing to her what was never in her and never occurred to her heart; I created her pure and protected her from every foul thing."
"20797","197 - Narrated Amr ibn Abi al-Tahir from Yahya ibn Bukayr from Ibn Lahia from Ata ibn Dinar from Said ibn Jubayr, concerning: When you received it with your tongues, that is when they spoke concerning the affair of Aisha; some of them said: I heard so-and-so saying such-and-such. So he said: You received it with your tongues, meaning: some of you narrate it from others: I heard from so-and-so, and I heard from so-and-so; And you say with your mouths, meaning: with your tongues, meaning of accusing her; What you have no knowledge of, meaning: without knowing whether what you said of accusation is true; And you think it insignificant, he says: And you think accusation a light sin; While it is with Allah tremendous, meaning: in burden."
"20798","198 - Narrated Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Said ibn Abi Maryam from Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi from Warqa from Ibn Abi Najih from Mujahid, concerning His saying: When you received it with your tongues, he said: Some of you narrate it from others."
"20799","199 - Narrated Ali ibn al-Mubarak al-Sanani from Zayd ibn al-Mubarak from Muhammad ibn Thawr from Ibn Jurayj from Mujahid, concerning His saying: When you received it, you narrate it, some of you from others."
"20800","200 - Narrated Yahya ibn Ayyub al-Allaf and Amr ibn Abi al-Tahir ibn al-Sarh, both saying: narrated Said ibn Abi Maryam, saying: reported Nafi ibn Umar al-Jumahi, saying: I heard Ibn Abi Mulayka saying: Aisha used to recite this verse: When you received it with your tongues, and say: It is only the hastening of lying. And Ibn Abi Mulayka says: She knows best about it, for it was sent down concerning her."
"20801","201 - Narrated Ali ibn al-Mubarak from Zayd ibn al-Mubarak from Muhammad ibn Thawr from Ibn Jurayj, saying: Ibn Abi Mulayka said: I heard Aisha reciting it: When you received it with your tongues."
"20802","Chapter on the interpretation of His saying: And why, when you heard it, did you not say: It is not for us to speak of this; glory be to You, this is a great slander. 202 - Narrated Abd al-Rahman ibn Khallad al-Dawraqi from Sadan ibn Zakariyya al-Dawraqi from Ismail ibn Yahya al-Taymi from Ibn Abi Dhib from Nafi from Ibn Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Usama concerning Aisha when she was accused of the slander: What do you say? He said: Glory be to Allah, it is not lawful for us to speak of this; glory be to You, this is a great slander."
"20803","203 - Narrated Bakr ibn Sahl from Abd al-Ghani ibn Said from Musa ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Sanani from Ibn Jurayj from Ata from Ibn Abbas, and from Muqatil ibn Sulayman from al-Dahhak ibn Muzahim from Ibn Abbas, concerning: Why, when you heard it, meaning: why then, did you hear it; You said: It is not for us to speak of this; glory be to You, this is a great slander, meaning: by slander, the great fabrication, like His saying concerning Maryam: And their saying against Maryam a great slander."
"20804","204 - Narrated Amr ibn Abi al-Tahir ibn al-Sarh al-Misri, saying: narrated Yahya ibn Bukayr from Ibn Lahia from Ata ibn Dinar from Said ibn Jubayr, concerning: Why, when you heard it, meaning: the accusation, why did you not say: It is not for us, meaning: it does not befit us, To speak of this, meaning: of the accusation, while our eyes did not see; Glory be to You, meaning: why did you not say: Glory be to You; This is a great slander, meaning: why did you not say like what Sad ibn Muadh al-Ansari said; that is because when Sad heard the saying of whoever spoke concerning Aisha, he said: Glory be to You, this is a great slander. Slander is that which confounds, saying what never was."
"20805","Chapter on the interpretation of His saying: Allah admonishes you never to return to the like of it, if you are believers. 205 - Narrated Bakr ibn Sahl from Abd al-Ghani ibn Said al-Thaqafi from Musa ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Sanani from Ibn Jurayj from Ata from Ibn Abbas, and from Muqatil ibn Sulayman from al-Dahhak ibn Muzahim from Ibn Abbas, concerning: Allah admonishes you never to return to the like of it, if you are believers, meaning Mistah ibn Uthatha, and Hamna bint Jahsh, and Hassan ibn Thabit; If you are believers, he says: If you believe in Allah and His Messenger."
"20806","206 - Narrated Amr ibn Abi al-Tahir ibn al-Sarh from Yahya ibn Bukayr from Ibn Lahia from Ata ibn Dinar from Said ibn Jubayr, concerning: Allah admonishes you never to return to the like of it, meaning: accusation."
"20807","207 - Narrated Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Said ibn Abi Maryam from Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi from Sufyan from Layth from Mujahid, concerning His saying: Allah admonishes you never to return to the like of it, he said: He forbids you."
"20808","208 - Narrated Abd al-Rahman ibn Salim al-Razi from Sahl ibn Uthman (H) and narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Yahya al-Himmani, both saying: narrated Hafs ibn Ghiyath from Layth ibn Abi Sulaym from al-Hakam from Miqsam from Ibn Abbas, concerning His saying: Allah admonishes you never to return to the like of it."
"20809","His saying: And Allah makes clear to you the verses, and Allah is Knowing, Wise. 209 - Narrated Bakr ibn Sahl from Abd al-Ghani ibn Said al-Thaqafi from Musa ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Sanani from Ibn Jurayj from Ata from Ibn Abbas, and from Muqatil ibn Sulayman from al-Dahhak from Ibn Abbas, concerning: And Allah makes clear to you the verses, meaning the verses He sent down concerning Aisha and the exoneration for her; And Allah is Knowing, Wise: Knowing of what is in your hearts of remorse for what you indulged in, Wise in that He ruled in accusation eighty lashes."
"20810","210 - Narrated Amr ibn Abi al-Tahir from Yahya ibn Bukayr from Ibn Lahia from Ata ibn Dinar from Said ibn Jubayr, concerning: And Allah makes clear to you the verses, meaning: what He mentioned of admonitions."
"20811","His saying: Those who love that indecency should spread among those who believe, the verse. 211 - Narrated Bakr ibn Sahl from Abd al-Ghani from Musa ibn Abd al-Rahman from Ibn Jurayj from Ata from Ibn Abbas, and from Muqatil ibn Sulayman from al-Dahhak ibn Muzahim from Ibn Abbas, concerning: Those who love that indecency should spread among those who believe, meaning the chaste men and chaste women among the believers; For them is a painful punishment, meaning grievous; In this world and the Hereafter, meaning in this world flogging and in the Hereafter punishment in the Fire; And Allah knows while you do not know, meaning: He knows what you entered into and what severity of punishment is in it, while you do not know the severity of Allah’s wrath upon whoever does this."
"20812","212 - Narrated Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Said ibn Abi Maryam from Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi from Warqa from Ibn Abi Najih from Mujahid, concerning His saying: Those who love that indecency should spread among those who believe, it appears with a report concerning Aisha."
"20813","213 - Narrated Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Hamza from Abu al-Jumahir from Said ibn Bashir from Qatada, concerning His saying: Those who love that indecency should spread among those who believe will have a painful punishment, meaning adultery."
"20814","214 - Narrated Umar ibn Abi al-Tahir from Yahya ibn Bukayr from Ibn Lahia from Ata ibn Dinar from Said ibn Jubayr, concerning: Those who, meaning: whoever accused Aisha; Love that indecency should spread, meaning: that adultery spread and appear; Among those who believe, meaning: Safwan and Aisha; For them is a painful punishment, meaning: grievous; In this world and the Hereafter: the punishment of Abdullah ibn Ubayy in this world was the hadd, and in the Hereafter the punishment of the Fire; And Allah knows while you do not know."
"20815","215 - Narrated Dawud ibn Muhammad ibn Salih from al-Abbas ibn al-Walid al-Narsi from Yazid ibn Zuray from Said ibn Abi Aruba from Qatada, concerning His saying: Those who love that indecency should spread among those who believe, meaning adultery appears; for them is the punishment of the Fire."
"20816","Chapter on His saying: And had it not been for the favor of Allah upon you and His mercy, and that Allah is Kind, Merciful. 216 - Narrated Bakr ibn Sahl from Abd al-Ghani from Musa from Abd al-Rahman al-Sanani from Ibn Jurayj from Ata from Ibn Abbas, and from Muqatil ibn Sulayman from al-Dahhak from Ibn Abbas, concerning: And had it not been for the favor of Allah upon you and His mercy, meaning: had it not been for what Allah favored you with and His mercy for your remorse, meaning Mistah, and Hamna, and Hassan; And that Allah is Kind, meaning: of mercy, Merciful to you in that you regretted and returned to the truth."
"20817","217 - Narrated Amr ibn al-Tahir ibn al-Sarh from Yahya ibn Bukayr from Ibn Lahia from Ata ibn Dinar from Said ibn Jubayr, concerning: And had it not been for the favor of Allah upon you and His mercy, meaning: He would have punished you for what you said about Aisha; And that Allah is Kind, meaning: Kind to you in that He pardoned and did not punish you for what you said of accusation."
"20818","Chapter on the interpretation of Allah Almighty’s saying: O you who believe, do not follow the footsteps of Satan, 218 - Narrated Bakr ibn Sahl from Abd al-Ghani ibn Said al-Thaqafi from Musa ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Sanani from Ibn Jurayj from Ata from Ibn Abbas, and from Muqatil from al-Dahhak from Ibn Abbas, concerning: O you who believe, do not follow the footsteps of Satan, meaning the slips of Satan; For he commands indecency and wrong, meaning: by indecency, disobedience to Allah, and wrong, all that Allah dislikes; And had it not been for the favor of Allah upon you and His mercy, none of you would ever have been purified, meaning: I would never have accepted the repentance of any of you, ever; But Allah purifies whom He wills, meaning: I have willed to turn to you in repentance; And Allah is Hearing, Knowing, of your saying against them, of what is in yourselves of remorse and repentance."
"20819","219 - Narrated Amr ibn Abi al-Tahir ibn al-Sarh from Yahya ibn Bukayr from Ibn Lahia from Ata ibn Dinar from Said ibn Jubayr, concerning: O you who believe, do not follow the footsteps of Satan, meaning: the adornment of Satan; For he commands indecency, meaning: disobediences; And wrong, not known, meaning like what was said about Aisha; And had it not been for the favor of Allah upon you and His mercy, meaning: His blessing; None of you would ever have been purified, meaning: none of you would ever have become righteous; But Allah purifies whom He wills, meaning: He makes righteous whom He wills."
"20820","Chapter on the interpretation of His saying: And let not those of virtue among you and affluence, the verse. 220 - Narrated Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Said ibn Abi Maryam from Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi from Warqa ibn Umar from Ibn Abi Najih from Mujahid, concerning His saying: And let not those of virtue among you and affluence swear not to give to relatives and the needy and the emigrants in the way of Allah; let them pardon and overlook; do you not love that Allah should forgive you; and Allah is Forgiving, Merciful, he said: Abu Bakr had sworn not to benefit an orphan in his care who spread that, meaning concerning Aisha. When this verse came down, he said: I love that Allah should forgive me, and I shall be to him better than I was."
"20821","221 - Narrated Ali ibn al-Mubarak al-Sanani from Zayd ibn al-Mubarak from Muhammad ibn Thawr from Ibn Jurayj from Mujahid, concerning His saying: And let not those of virtue among you and affluence, he said: Abu Bakr swore not to benefit an orphan who was in his care; Abd al-Malik said: He is Mistah ibn Uthatha ibn Abbad ibn al-Muttalib, who spread that. When this verse came down: Do you not love that Allah should forgive you, Abu Bakr said: Yes, I love that Allah should forgive me, and I shall be to the orphans better than I was."
"20822","222 - Narrated Bakr ibn Sahl from Abd al-Ghani ibn Said al-Thaqafi from Musa ibn Abd al-Rahman from Ibn Jurayj from Ata from Ibn Abbas, and from Muqatil ibn Sulayman from al-Dahhak from Ibn Abbas, concerning: And let not swear, meaning: and let not swear; Those of virtue among you and affluence, meaning: and let not Abu Bakr swear not to spend on Mistah ibn Uthatha; That they give to relatives and the needy and the emigrants in the way of Allah; let them pardon and overlook: I have placed in you, O Abu Bakr, virtue, and placed with you affluence and knowledge of Allah and knowledge of kinship, so be kind, O Abu Bakr, to Mistah, for he has kinship, emigration, need, and witnessed scenes I approved, among them Badr; Do you not love that Allah should forgive you, meaning: so forgive Mistah; And Allah is Forgiving, Merciful, meaning: I am Forgiving to whoever errs, Merciful to My allies."
"20823","223 - Narrated Abdullah ibn Najiya from Muhammad ibn Sad al-Awqi from his father Sad ibn Muhammad from his uncle al-Husayn ibn al-Hasan ibn Atiyya from his father from his grandfather Atiyya al-Awqi from Ibn Abbas, concerning His saying: And let not those of virtue among you and affluence, the verse, he said: Some people among the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had accused Aisha of foulness, spread it, and spoken of it. Some people among the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), among them Abu Bakr, swore not to give charity to a man who spoke anything of this, nor maintain him. He said: Let not those of virtue among you and affluence swear not to join their kin and give them of their wealth as they used to do before that. Allah commanded that they forgive them and pardon them."
"20824","224 - Narrated Dawud ibn Muhammad ibn Salih from al-Abbas ibn al-Walid al-Narsi from Yazid ibn Zuray from Said ibn Abi Aruba from Qatada, concerning His saying: And let not those of virtue among you and affluence, to His saying: And the needy, he says: Let not those of virtue among you and affluence swear: That they give to relatives, to His saying: Forgiving, Merciful. This verse came down concerning a man of Quraysh called Mistah; between him and Abu Bakr was kinship, and he was an orphan in his care, and he was among those who spread against Aisha what they spread. When Allah sent down her exoneration and her excuse, Abu Bakr swore not to give him sustenance. He said: Do you not love that Allah should forgive you? He said: Yes. He said: So pardon and overlook. Abu Bakr said: No doubt, I shall not withhold a kindness I used to confer upon him before today."
"20825","225 - Narrated Amr ibn Abi al-Tahir ibn al-Sarh al-Misri from Yahya ibn Bukayr from Ibn Lahia from Ata ibn Dinar from Said ibn Jubayr, concerning: And let not swear, meaning: and let not swear; Those of virtue among you, meaning: in wealth; And affluence, in provision, meaning Abu Bakr; Let them pardon and overlook, meaning: and overlook Mistah; That they give to relatives, Mistah ibn Uthatha, for he is a relative of Abu Bakr, son of his maternal aunt, for Mistah was poor; And the emigrants in the way of Allah, for he was among the emigrants; Do you not love, meaning: Abu Bakr; That Allah should forgive you, and Allah is Forgiving, Merciful, meaning: Forgiving of sins, Merciful to the believers. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Abu Bakr: Do you not love that Allah should forgive you? He said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Then pardon and overlook. He said: I have pardoned and overlooked; I shall not withhold a kindness after today."
"20826","Chapter on the interpretation of His saying: Those who accuse chaste, heedless, believing women are cursed in this world and the Hereafter, the verse. 226 - Narrated Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Said ibn Abi Maryam from Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi from Sufyan from Khusayf, saying: I asked Said ibn Jubayr about His saying: Those who accuse chaste, heedless, believing women are cursed in this world and the Hereafter: Everyone who accuses a chaste woman, Allah curses him. He said: No, this was sent down only concerning Aisha."
"20827","227 - Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq from Yahya al-Himmani from Muhammad ibn Fudayl from Khusayf, saying: I said to Said ibn Jubayr: Which is severer, adultery or accusing a chaste woman? He said: Adultery. I said: Allah says: Those who accuse chaste, heedless, believing women. He said: This was sent down only concerning Aisha specifically."
"20828","228 - Narrated Amr ibn Abi al-Tahir ibn al-Sarh from Yahya ibn Bukayr from Ibn Lahia from Ata ibn Dinar from Said ibn Jubayr, concerning: Those who accuse chaste women, meaning: those who accuse of adultery, meaning of their private parts, chaste; Heedless, meaning: of indecencies, meaning Aisha; Believing, meaning: truthful; Are cursed, meaning: punished and flogged eighty; In this world and the Hereafter, meaning: Abdullah ibn Ubayy ibn Salul is punished with the Fire, for he is a hypocrite, for him is a great punishment. He said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) flogged Hassan ibn Thabit, and Abdullah ibn Ubayy, and Mistah, and Hamna bint Jahsh, each of them eighty lashes, for accusing Aisha. Then they repented after that, except Abdullah ibn Ubayy among the hypocrites, who died upon his hypocrisy."
"20829","229 - Narrated Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Said ibn Abi Maryam from Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi from Sufyan from Salama ibn Nubayt from al-Dahhak ibn Muzahim, saying: This verse came down concerning the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) specifically: Those who accuse chaste, heedless, believing women, the verse."
"20830","230 - Narrated Dawud ibn Muhammad ibn Salih al-Marwazi from al-Abbas ibn al-Walid al-Narsi from Yazid ibn Zuray, saying: narrated Said ibn Abi Aruba from Qatada, concerning His saying: Those who accuse chaste, heedless, believing women are cursed in this world and the Hereafter and for them is a great punishment, he said: It was sent down concerning Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her)."
"20831","231 - Narrated Bakr ibn Sahl, saying: narrated Abd al-Ghani from Musa ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Sanani from Ibn Jurayj from Ata from Ibn Abbas, and from Muqatil ibn Sulayman from al-Dahhak ibn Muzahim from Ibn Abbas, concerning: Those who accuse chaste women, meaning chaste; Heedless, believing, meaning affirming the oneness of Allah and His messengers; Are cursed in this world and the Hereafter and for them is a great punishment, meaning: He removed them from faith."
"20832","232 - Narrated Abdullah ibn Najiya from Muhammad ibn Sad al-Awqi from his father Sad ibn Muhammad from his uncle al-Husayn ibn al-Hasan ibn Atiyya from his father from his grandfather Atiyya al-Awqi from Ibn Abbas, concerning His saying: Those who accuse chaste, heedless, believing women are cursed in this world and the Hereafter and for them is a great punishment, meaning: the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); the people of hypocrisy accused them, so curse and anger became due upon them, and they incurred anger from Allah. All of that is concerning the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"20833","233 - Narrated Abu Khalifa al-Fadl ibn al-Hubab from Muhammad ibn Ubayd ibn Hisab from Muhammad ibn Thawr from Mamar from al-Kalbi, concerning His saying: Those who accuse chaste, heedless, believing women are cursed: This verse means thereby the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). As for whoever accuses a woman among the Muslims, he is a sinner as Allah said, or he repents."
"20834","234 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Ali al-Saigh al-Makki from Said ibn Mansur from Hushaym, saying: reported al-Awwam ibn Hawshab from an elder of Banu Kahil from Ibn Abbas, that he recited Surat al-Nur and explained it. When he reached this verse: Those who accuse chaste, heedless, believing women are cursed in this world and the Hereafter and for them is a great punishment, he said: This is concerning Aisha and the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and He did not grant repentance to whoever does that. And He granted to whoever accuses a woman among the believing women other than the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) repentance. Then he recited: And those who accuse chaste women and then do not bring four witnesses, flog them eighty lashes and never accept their testimony, and those are the defiantly disobedient. So He granted to these repentance: Except those who repent after that and make amends, for Allah is Forgiving, Merciful. So He granted to whoever accuses a woman among the believers repentance, and did not grant to whoever accuses a woman among the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) repentance. Then he recited this verse: They are cursed in this world and the Hereafter and for them is a great punishment. Some people intended to rise to Ibn Abbas to kiss his head for the excellence of his explanation."
"20835","Chapter on the interpretation of Allah Almighty’s saying: On that Day Allah will pay them their just due, the verse. 235 - Narrated Dawud ibn Muhammad ibn Salih al-Marwazi from Abbas ibn al-Walid al-Narsi from Yazid ibn Zuray from Said ibn Abi Aruba from Qatada, concerning His saying: On that Day Allah will pay them their just due: the people of truth their truth, and the people of falsehood their falsehood; And they will know that Allah is the Manifest Truth."
"20836","236 - Narrated Bakr ibn Sahl from Abd al-Ghani ibn Said al-Thaqafi from Musa ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Sanani from Ibn Jurayj from Ata from Ibn Abbas, and from Muqatil ibn Sulayman from al-Dahhak from Ibn Abbas, concerning: On that Day Allah will pay them their just due, meaning: We shall recompense them for their deeds in truth, as He recompenses His allies with reward, so He recompenses His enemies with punishment; And they will know, meaning on the Day of Resurrection; That Allah is the Manifest Truth. That is because Abdullah ibn Ubayy ibn Salul doubts in religion, and he was head of the hypocrites; meaning: doubt was cut off from him and he became certain where certainty will not benefit him."
"20837","237 - Narrated Amr ibn Abi al-Tahir ibn al-Sarh from Yahya ibn Bukayr from Ibn Lahia from Ata ibn Dinar from Said ibn Jubayr, concerning: On that Day, in the Hereafter; Allah will pay them their just due, meaning: their just reckoning, He does not wrong them; And they will know that Allah is the Manifest Truth, meaning: the Manifest Justice."
"20838","Chapter on the interpretation of His saying: Foul women are for foul men, the verse. 238 - Narrated Bakr ibn Sahl from Abd al-Ghani ibn Said al-Thaqafi from Musa ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Sanani from Ibn Jurayj from Ata from Ibn Abbas, and from Muqatil ibn Sulayman from al-Dahhak ibn Muzahim from Ibn Abbas, concerning: Foul women are for foul men and foul men are for foul women, meaning: the example of Abdullah ibn Ubayy ibn Salul, and whoever doubts Allah and accuses like the chief lady of the Muslim women. Then He said: And good women are for good men, meaning: the good women, Aisha, whom Allah made good for His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); Jibril, peace be upon him, brought her to him in a piece of silk before she was formed in her mother’s womb, saying to him: This is Aisha bint Abi Bakr, your wife in this world and your wife in the Hereafter, in compensation for Khadija bint Khuwaylid. That was at the death of Khadija. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was delighted with her and his eye was cooled by her. Then He said: And good men are for good women, meaning the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); Allah made him good for Himself and made him chief of the children of Adam. Good women, meaning Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her)."
"20839","239 - Narrated Amr ibn Abi al-Tahir ibn al-Sarh from Yahya ibn Bukayr from Ibn Lahia from Ata ibn Dinar from Said ibn Jubayr, concerning: Foul women are for foul men, meaning: evil speech, accusing Aisha, and the like, belongs to foul men and women, meaning those who accused her; And foul men, meaning: of men and women; Are for foul women, meaning: evil speech, for evil speech befits them. Then He said: And good women, meaning: good speech; Are for good men, of men and women who thought good of the believing men and women; And good men, of men and women; Are for good women: good speech, for good speech befits them."
"20840","240 - Narrated Abu Yazid al-Qaratisî from Asbagh ibn al-Faraj from Abd al-Rahman ibn Zayd ibn Aslam, concerning His saying: Foul women are for foul men and foul men are for foul women and good women are for good men and good men are for good women, he said: It came down concerning Aisha when the hypocrite accused her of slander and fabrication, and Allah exonerated her from that. Abdullah ibn Ubayy was foul, so he was worthier that the foul woman be his and he hers. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was good, and worthier that the good woman be his. Aisha was the good woman, and worthier that the good man be hers."
"20841","241 - Narrated Abd al-Rahman ibn Khallad al-Dawraqi from Sadan ibn Zakariyya al-Dawraqi from Ismail ibn Yahya al-Taymi from Ibn Abi Dhib from Nafi from Ibn Umar, saying: The wife of Abdullah ibn Ubayy was a hypocrite with him. So the Quran came down: Foul women, meaning: the wife of Abdullah; Are for foul men, meaning: Abdullah ibn Ubayy; And foul men are for foul women, meaning: Abdullah ibn Ubayy for his wife; And good men are for good women, meaning: Aisha and the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); And good men, meaning: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); Are for good women, for Aisha and the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); Those are exonerated from what they say, to the end of the verse."
"20842","242 - Narrated Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Said ibn Abi Maryam from Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi from Qays ibn al-Rabi from Salama ibn Nubayt from al-Dahhak ibn Muzahim, concerning His saying: And foul men are for foul women, he said: Foul women of speech are for foul men among people. And concerning His saying: And good women are for good men, he said: Good women of speech are for good men among people."
"20843","243 - Narrated Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Said ibn Abi Maryam from Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi from Warqa from Ibn Abi Najih from Mujahid, concerning His saying: Good women, foul women, he said: It is evil speech and good speech; good speech is for the believers, and evil speech is for the disbelievers."
"20844","244 - Narrated Abu Khalifa from Muhammad ibn Ubayd ibn Hisab from Muhammad ibn Thawr from Mamar from Ibn Abi Najih from Mujahid, concerning His saying: Foul women are for foul men, he said: Foul women of speech are for foul men among people, and foul men among people are for foul women of speech, and good women of speech are for good men among people, and good men among people are for good women of speech."
"20845","245 - Narrated Ali ibn al-Mubarak al-Sanani from Zayd ibn al-Mubarak from Muhammad ibn Thawr from Ibn Jurayj from Mujahid, concerning His saying: Foul women are for foul men, and good women are for good men, he said: Evil speech and good speech; for the good believer, the good, and for the disbeliever, the evil."
"20846","246 - Narrated Abd al-Rahman ibn Salim al-Razi from Sahl ibn Uthman from Ali ibn Mushir and Marwan ibn Muawiya, both saying: narrated Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulayman from al-Qasim ibn Abi Bazza from Said ibn Jubayr, concerning His saying: Foul women are for foul men, and good women are for good men, he said: Foul women of speech are for foul men among people, and good women of speech are for good men among people."
"20847","247 - Narrated Zakariyya ibn Yahya al-Saji from Ahmad ibn Ishaq al-Ahwazi from Abu Ahmad al-Zubayri from Hasan ibn Salih from Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulayman from al-Qasim ibn Abi Bazza from Mujahid, or Said ibn Jubayr, concerning His saying: Foul women are for foul men, he said: Foul women of speech are for foul men among people, and good women of speech are for good men among people."
"20848","248 - Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari from Yahya al-Himmani from Hafs ibn Ghiyath from Abdullah ibn Muslim ibn Hurmuz from Said ibn Jubayr from Ibn Abbas, concerning His saying: Foul women are for foul men, he said: Foul speech is for foul men among people; And good women are for good men, he said: Good speech is for good men among people."
"20849","249 - Narrated Abd al-Rahman ibn Salm al-Razi from Sahl ibn Uthman from Mahbub ibn Muhriz al-Qawariri from Talha ibn Amr from Ata from Ibn Abbas, concerning His saying: Foul women are for foul men and foul men are for foul women and good women are for good men and good men are for good women, he said: Foul women of speech are for foul men among people, and foul men among people are for foul women of speech, and good women of speech are for good men among people. Do you not see that you hear a foul word from a righteous man, and you say: May Allah forgive so-and-so; this is not of his character, nor of what he says."
"20850","250 - Narrated Abdullah ibn Najiya from Muhammad ibn Sad al-Awfi from his father Sad ibn Muhammad from his uncle al-Husayn ibn al-Hasan from his father from his grandfather Atiyya ibn Sad al-Awqi from Ibn Abbas, concerning His saying: Foul women are for foul men and foul men are for foul women, he says: Foul women of speech are for foul men among men, and foul men among men are for foul women of speech; And good women are for good men, he says: And good women of speech are for good men among men. It came down concerning those who said about the wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what they said of slander. And it is said: Foul women are for foul men: foul deeds belong to foul people, and good deeds among deeds belong to good people."
"20851","251 - Narrated Abd al-Rahman ibn Salim al-Razi from Sahl ibn Uthman from Yahya ibn Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Ghaniyya from his father from al-Hakam ibn Utayba, saying: When the people spoke concerning Aisha, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent for Aisha. She said: So I came trembling without fever. He said: O Aisha, what do people say? I said: No, by Him who sent you with the truth, I shall not excuse myself with anything to you, they said, until my excuse comes down from heaven. Allah sent down concerning her fifteen verses of Surat al-Nur. Then al-Hakam recited until he reached: Foul women are for foul men and foul men are for foul women and good women are for good men and good men are for good women. He said: Foul women among women are for foul men among men, and foul men among men are for foul women among women, and good women among women are for good men among men, and good men among men are for good women among women."
"20852","252 - Narrated Dawud ibn Muhammad ibn Salih al-Marwazi, saying: narrated al-Abbas ibn al-Walid al-Narsi from Yazid ibn Zuray from Said ibn Abi Aruba from Qatada, concerning His saying: Foul women are for foul men and foul men are for foul women, of speech and deed; And good women are for good men and good men are for good women, of speech and deed."
"20853","Chapter on the interpretation of His saying: Those are exonerated from what they say; for them is forgiveness and noble provision. 253 - Narrated Bakr ibn Sahl from Abd al-Ghani ibn Said al-Thaqafi from Musa ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Sanani from Ibn Jurayj from Ata from Ibn Abbas, and from Muqatil ibn Sulayman from al-Dahhak from Ibn Abbas, concerning: Those are exonerated from what they say, meaning: Allah’s exoneration from the lying of Abdullah ibn Ubayy ibn Salul; For them is forgiveness, meaning: protection in this world and forgiveness in the Hereafter; And noble provision, meaning: the provision of Paradise and great reward."
"20854","254 - Narrated Amr ibn Abi al-Tahir ibn al-Sarh al-Misri from Yahya ibn Bukayr from Ibn Lahia from Ata ibn Dinar from Said ibn Jubayr, concerning: Those, meaning: the good among men and women; Are exonerated from what they say, meaning: from what these accusers who accused Aisha say; they are free of evil speech. Then He said: For them is forgiveness, meaning: for their sins; And noble provision, meaning: good in Paradise. When the excuse of Aisha came down, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) drew her to himself, and she is among his wives in Paradise."
"20855","255 - Narrated Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Said ibn Abi Maryam from Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi from Warqa from Ibn Abi Najih from Mujahid, concerning His saying: Those are exonerated from what they say, he said: Each is cleared of speech that is not rightfully his."
"20856","256 - Narrated Abu Khalifa from Muhammad ibn Ubayd ibn Hisab from Muhammad ibn Thawr from Mamar from Ibn Abi Najih from Mujahid, concerning His saying: Those are exonerated from what they say: Whoever is good is exonerated from every foul saying he says, which Allah forgives; and whoever is foul is exonerated from every righteous saying he said, which Allah returns upon him and does not accept from him."
"20857","257 - Narrated Ali ibn al-Mubarak al-Sanani from Zayd ibn al-Mubarak from Muhammad ibn Thawr from Ibn Jurayj from Mujahid, concerning His saying: Those are exonerated from what they say: Whatever good word the disbeliever said belongs to the believer, and whatever foul word the believer said belongs to the disbeliever; each is seen as cleared of speech that is not rightfully his."
"20858","258 - Narrated Abu Yazid al-Qaratisî from Asbagh ibn al-Faraj from Abd al-Rahman ibn Zayd ibn Aslam, concerning His saying: Those are exonerated from what they say, he said: Here Aisha was exonerated; For them is forgiveness and noble provision."
"20859","259 - Narrated Dawud ibn Muhammad ibn Salih al-Marwazi from al-Abbas ibn al-Walid al-Narsi from Yazid ibn Zuray from Said ibn Abi Aruba from Qatada, concerning His saying: Those are exonerated from what they say; for them is forgiveness and noble provision: forgiveness for their sins, and it is Paradise."
"20860","Chapter on in which expedition was the saying of the people of the slander. 260 - Narrated Abu Shuayb al-Harrani from Abu Jafar al-Nufayli from Muhammad ibn Salama from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, saying: The expedition of Banu al-Mustaliq was in Shaban, year six. In that expedition the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took Aisha with him, casting lots among his wives, and her lot came out. In that expedition the people of the slander said concerning her what they said, and Allah Almighty sent down her exoneration."
"20861","261 - Narrated Musa ibn Zakariyya al-Tustari from Shabab al-Usfuri, saying: In year six of the Hijra was the expedition of Banu al-Mustaliq. In this expedition the people of the slander said concerning Aisha what they said, and the Quran came down concerning her: Those who brought the slander, the verses."
"20862","Chapter on the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) flogging the companions of the slander. 262 - Narrated Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz and Abu Muslim al-Kashshi, both saying: narrated Hajjaj ibn Minhal from Hammad ibn Salama from al-Kalbi from Abi Salih from Ibn Abbas, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) flogged them eighty, eighty."
"20863","263 - Narrated Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal from his father from Muhammad ibn Abi Adi from Muhammad ibn Ishaq from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr from Amra from Aisha, saying: When my excuse came down, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood on the pulpit, mentioned that, and recited the Quran. When he came down, he commanded concerning two men and a woman, and they were struck their hadd."
"20864","264 - Narrated Zakariyya ibn Yahya al-Saji from Abdullah ibn Harun Abu Alqama al-Farwi from Qudama ibn Muhammad al-Ashjai from Makhrama ibn Bukayr from his father from Ibn Shihab from Ubayd Allah ibn Abdullah from Ibn Abbas, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When the Day of Resurrection comes, Allah will hadd those who reviled Aisha, eighty, eighty, before the creatures. My Lord will grant me the Emigrants among them as a gift, so I consult you, O Aisha. Aisha heard the speech and wept while in the house. Then she said: By Him who sent you with the truth as a prophet, your joy is dearer to me than my joy. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) smiled laughing, saying: Daughter of her father."
"20865","Ways of the report of Umm Zara and the good companionship of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with Aisha. 265 - Narrated al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Asfati from Musa ibn Ismail from Said ibn Salama ibn Abi al-Husam from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Aisha, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: I was to you as Abu Zara to Umm Zara. Then he began narrating the report of Umm Zara and her companions, saying: Eleven women gathered and pledged and agreed to describe their husbands truthfully. One of them said: My husband is helpless, heavy, every ailment has its ailment; he wounded you, or split you, or gathered all to you. Another said: My husband is meat of a lean camel on a rugged mountain, neither fat to be climbed to, nor plain to be crossed. Another said: My husband is the tall lank one; if I am silent I am divorced, if I speak I am suspended. Another said: My husband, when he drinks, sips up, when he sleeps, wraps, and does not insert the hand to know grief. Another said: My husband, I do not complete his report; I fear I shall not leave it. Urwa said to him: These are five complaining. Another said: My husband is free as the night of Tihama, neither hot nor cold, nor fear. Another said: When he enters, he is as a leopard, when he goes out, as a lion, and does not ask about what he entrusted. Another said: My husband, the scent is the scent of zarnab, the touch the touch of a rabbit; I overcome him while people overcome. Another said: My husband is high-pillared, long sword-belted, great-ashed, near house to gathering. Another said: My husband is Abu Malik, and what is Abu Malik? Owner of camels of many tracks, few kneeling places; when they hear the sound of lutes, they know they perish. Another said: My husband is Abu Zara, and what is Abu Zara? He filled my ears from jewelry, filled my upper arms from fat, and gladdened myself so I was glad to him. The son of Abu Zara, and what is the son of Abu Zara? His bed is as the stripping of a palm-javelin, and the foreleg of a young she-camel satiates him. The daughter of Abu Zara, and what is the daughter of Abu Zara? Filling of her wrap, smallness of her cloak, best of her women, anger of her co-wives, obedience of her father, obedience of her mother. The maid of Abu Zara, and what is the maid of Abu Zara? She does not spread our talk spreading, nor spoil our provision picking, nor fill our house with rubbish. Abu Zara came to me while I was in hardship, and took me away to people of neighing and lowing and threshing and winnowing. With him I drink and am satisfied, sleep till morning, and speak without blame. Abu Zara went out from me while the skins were being churned, and saw a woman with two sons like leopards playing beneath her with two pomegranates. Abu Zara married her and divorced me. After him I married a noble youth who rode a noble horse, took a Khatti spear, and brought to my daughter rich livestock, and gave me in every grazing pair. He said: Eat and provide your family. Had I gathered all I received from him and placed it in the smallest vessel of Abu Zara’s vessels, it would not fill it. Aisha said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: So I was to you as Abu Zara to Umm Zara."
"20866","266 - Narrated Ahmad ibn al-Mualla from Hisham ibn Ammar (H) and narrated Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Iqal al-Harrani from Abu Jafar al-Nufayli (H) and narrated Ahmad ibn Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najda al-Hawti from his father (H) and narrated Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani from his father, all saying: narrated Isa ibn Yunus from Hisham ibn Urwa from his brother Abdullah ibn Urwa from his father from Aisha, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: I was to you as Abu Zara to Umm Zara, and he mentioned something like it."
"20867","267 - Narrated al-Husayn ibn Ishaq al-Tustari and Jafar ibn Muhammad al-Firyabi, both saying: narrated Hamid ibn Yahya al-Balkhi, saying: narrated Sufyan ibn Uyayna from Dawud ibn Shabur from Abdullah ibn Urwa from his father from Aisha, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: I was to you as Abu Zara to Umm Zara, and he mentioned something like it."
"20868","268 - Narrated Muadh ibn al-Muthanna and Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal, both saying: narrated Yahya ibn Main from Uqba ibn Khalid al-Sakuni from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Aisha. Uqba ibn Khalid said: Hisham ibn Urwa said: Yazid ibn Ruman narrated to me from Urwa from Aisha, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: Eleven women gathered in the Jahiliyya and agreed to be truthful among themselves and conceal nothing of their husbands’ reports. The first said: My husband is meat of a lean camel on a mountain top, neither plain to be climbed, nor fat to be moved. The second said: I shall not spread his report; I fear I shall not leave it; if I mention him, I mention his swellings and lumps. The third said: My husband is the tall lank one; if I speak I am divorced, if silent suspended. The fourth said: My husband, if he eats, wraps, if he drinks, sips, if he lies down, wraps, and does not insert the hand to know grief. The fifth said: My husband is helpless, heavy; every ailment has its ailment; he wounded you or split you or gathered all to you. The sixth said: My husband is as the night of Tihama, neither hot nor cold, nor fear. The seventh said: My husband, if he enters, is as a leopard, if he goes out, as a lion, and does not ask about what he entrusted. The eighth said: My husband, the touch is the touch of a rabbit, the scent the scent of zarnab, and I overcome him while people overcome. The ninth said: My husband is high-pillared, long sword-belted, great-ashed, near house to gathering. The tenth said: My husband is Malik, and what is Malik? Malik is better than that; he has camels few of pastures, many of kneeling places; when they hear the sound of lutes, they know they perish. The eleventh said: My husband is Abu Zara, and what is Abu Zara? He filled my ears from jewelry, filled my upper arms from fat, and gladdened me so myself was glad to him. He found me among a small flock in hardship, and placed me among neighing and lowing and threshing and winnowing. With him I say without blame, sleep till morning, and drink till satisfied. The mother of Abu Zara, and what is the mother of Abu Zara? Her vessels ample, her house spacious. The son of Abu Zara, and what is the son of Abu Zara? His bed as the stripping of a palm-strip; the foreleg of a young she-camel satiates him. The daughter of Abu Zara, and what is the daughter of Abu Zara? Obedience of her father and obedience of her mother, filling of her wrap, anger of her co-wife. The maid of Abu Zara, and what is the maid of Abu Zara? She does not spread our talk, nor fill our provision with picking, nor fill our house with rubbish. Abu Zara went out while the skins were being churned, and passed by a woman with two sons like leopards playing beneath her waist with two pomegranates. He divorced me and married her. After him I married a noble man who rode a noble one, took a Khatti one, brought upon me rich livestock, and gave me of every grazing a pair. He said: Eat, Umm Zara, and provide your family. Had I gathered all he gave me, it would not fill the smallest vessel of Abu Zara’s vessels. Aisha said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: I was to you as Abu Zara to Umm Zara."
"20869","269 - Narrated Ali ibn al-Mubarak al-Sanani from Zayd ibn al-Mubarak (H) and narrated Musa ibn Harun and Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal, both saying: narrated Abu Khaythama Zuhayr ibn Harb from Rayhan ibn Said from Abbad ibn Mansur from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Aisha, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: I was to you as Abu Zara to Umm Zara. I said: O Messenger of Allah, what are Abu Zara and Umm Zara? He said: Ten women gathered and swore to be truthful about their husbands. One of them said: I shall not report his news; I fear I shall not leave it. Another said: He is the tall lank one; if silent suspended, if I speak divorced. Another said: He is meat of a lean camel on a mountain, neither fat to be climbed to, nor plain to be crossed. Another said: When he eats, he wraps, when he drinks, he sips, and does not insert the hand so grief comes out. Another said: He is the night of Tihama, neither hot nor cold, nor fear. Another said: When he enters, as a leopard, when he goes out, as a lion, and does not ask about what he entrusted. Another said: The scent is the scent of zarnab, the touch the touch of a rabbit, and I overcome him while people overcome. Another said: He is long sword-belted, high-pillared, great-ashed, near house to gathering. Another said: He is Malik, and what is Malik? He has camels many of tracks, few of kneeling places; when they hear the sound of the pipe, they know they perish. Another said: He is Abu Zara, and what is Abu Zara? Owner of livestock and crops; he cheered me, so my ears were cheered from jewelry, my upper arms from fat, and gladdened me so myself was glad to him. With him I sleep till morning, drink till satisfied, and say without blame. He took me from a household in hardship and placed me among neighing and lowing and threshing and winnowing. The son of Abu Zara, and what is the son of Abu Zara? His sleeping place as the palm-strip, and the foreleg of a reed suffices him. The daughter of Abu Zara, and what is the daughter of Abu Zara? Filling of her wrap and fold of her cloak, coolness of her father’s and mother’s eye, adornment of her family, and not wronged thereby. The maid of Abu Zara, and what is the maid of Abu Zara? She does not spread our talk spreading, nor spoil our provision picking. Abu Zara went out from me while the skins were being churned, and saw a woman with two sons like leopards playing beneath her with two pomegranates. He married her and divorced me. After him I married a noble youth; he went out generous, took a Khatti one, drove rich livestock, gave me of every grazing a pair, and said: Provide your family, O Umm Zara. I said: Had this all been gathered in a vessel of Abu Zara’s vessels, it would not fill it. Aisha said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, you are better to me than Abu Zara to Umm Zara."
"20870","270 - Narrated Zakariyya ibn Yahya al-Saji from Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad ibn Zabala al-Makhzumi, saying: narrated Abd al-Jabbar ibn Said al-Masahqi from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Aisha, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Aisha, I was to you as Abu Zara to Umm Zara, except that Abu Zara divorced, and I do not divorce."
"20871","271 - Narrated Abu Husayn Muhammad ibn al-Husayn the judge and Muhammad ibn Uthman ibn Abi Shayba, both saying: narrated Ubayd ibn Yaish from Yahya ibn Yala from Abd al-Karim Abu Bakr al-Shami from Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Aisha, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: I was to you as Abu Zara to Umm Zara."
"20872","272 - Narrated Musa ibn Harun from Hajjaj ibn Yusuf the poet (H) and narrated Zakariyya ibn Yahya al-Saji, saying: narrated Muhammad ibn Mansur al-Jawwaz (H) and narrated Ali ibn Said al-Razi, saying: narrated Ishaq ibn Zurayq al-Rasibi and Ibrahim ibn Yaqub al-Juzajani, all saying: narrated Abd al-Malik ibn Ibrahim al-Juddi from Muhammad ibn Muhammad Abu Nafi al-Taifi from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Wahid ibn Ayman, saying: Umar ibn Abdullah ibn Urwa narrated to me from Urwa from Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her), saying: I boasted of my father’s wealth in the Jahiliyya, and it was about a thousand thousand ounces. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Be silent, O Aisha, for I was to you as Abu Zara to Umm Zara. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) began narrating: Eleven women gathered in the Jahiliyya and agreed that each woman would report what is in her husband without lying. It was said: You, O so-and-so. She said: The night is the night of Tihama, neither hot nor cold, nor fear. It was said: You, O so-and-so. She said: The scent is the scent of zarnab, the touch the touch of a rabbit, and I overcome him while people overcome. It was said: You, O so-and-so. She said: By Allah, as I know, he is high-pillared, long sword-belted, great-ashed, near house to gathering. It was said: You, O so-and-so. She said: I married Malik, and what is Malik? He has camels many of pastures, few of kneeling places; when they hear the sound of lutes, they know they perish. It was said: You, O so-and-so. She said: I mention my husband; if I mention him, I mention his swellings and lumps; I fear I shall not leave him. It was said: You, O so-and-so. She said: By Allah, as I know, when he enters, as a leopard, when he goes out, as a lion, and does not ask about what he entrusted. It was said: You, O so-and-so. She said: Meat of a lean camel on a mountain, neither fat to be moved, nor plain to be climbed to. It was said: You, O so-and-so. She said: By Allah, as I know, when he eats, he wraps, when he drinks, he sips, when he slaughters, he is greedy, when he sleeps, he wraps, and does not insert the hand to know grief. It was said: You, O so-and-so. She said: I married the tall lank one; if I speak I am divorced, if silent suspended. It was said: You, O so-and-so. She said: Helpless, heavy; every ailment has its ailment; he wounded you, or split you, or gathered all to you. It was said: You, O so-and-so. She said: I married Abu Zara, and what is Abu Zara? He filled my ears from jewelry, filled my upper arms from fat, and gladdened myself so I was glad to him. He found me among a small flock in hardship, and placed me among grown camels and rushing horses and lowing and threshing and winnowing. With him I sleep till morning, drink till satisfied, and speak without blame. The son of Abu Zara, and what is the son of Abu Zara? His bed is the stripping of the palm-strip, and the foreleg of the young she-camel satiates him. The daughter of Abu Zara, and what is the daughter of Abu Zara? Filling of her waist-wrap, adornment of her father and adornment of her mother, and best of her co-wife. The maid of Abu Zara, and what is the maid of Abu Zara? She does not take out our talk spreading, nor destroy our provision picking. Abu Zara went out from me while the skins were being churned, and there he was with a mother of two boys like falcons. Abu Zara married her and divorced me. I replaced, and every replacement is one-eyed. Then I married a noble youth who rode a noble one, took a Khatti one, gave me rich livestock and gave me of every grazing a pair. He said: Take provision, O Umm Zara, provide your family. I gathered of that, and it did not fill the smallest vessel of Abu Zara’s vessels. Aisha said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, rather you are better than Abu Zara."
"20873","273 - Narrated Ali ibn Said al-Razi from Muhammad ibn Abi Umar al-Adani, saying: narrated Sufyan from Dawud ibn Shabur from Umar ibn Abdullah ibn Urwa from Urwa from Aisha, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), something like it."
"20874","274 - Narrated Ubayd Allah ibn Muhammad al-Umari from al-Zubayr ibn Bakkar from Muhammad ibn al-Dahhak ibn Uthman al-Harami from Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad al-Darawardi from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Aisha, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered and said: O Aisha, I was to you as Abu Zara to Umm Zara. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A village among the villages of Yemen had clans of Yemen, in it eleven women, and they went out to a gathering of theirs. Some of them said to others: Come, let us mention our husbands with some of what is in them, without lying. It was said to the first: Speak, and she said, and he mentioned the hadith. The second spoke, she is Amra bint Abd Amr. It was said to the third: Speak, she is Hubba bint Kab. It was said to the fourth: Speak, she is Hudud bint Abi Haruma. It was said to the fifth: Speak, she is Kabsha. It was said to the sixth: Speak, she is Hind. It was said to the seventh: Speak, she is Hubba bint Alqama. It was said to the eighth: Speak, she is Asma bint Abd. It was said to the ninth: Speak, and he did not name her. It was said to the tenth: Speak, she is Kubaysha bint al-Arqam. It was said to Umm Zara: Speak, she is bint al-Ukayhil ibn Saida. She said: Abu Zara, and what is Abu Zara? And he mentioned the hadith."
"20875","Chapter on Aisha playing with dolls with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 275 - Narrated Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari from Abd al-Razzaq from Mamar from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Aisha, saying: I used to play with dolls, and my girl companions would come to me. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered, they would flee from him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would take them and return them to me."
"20876","276 - Narrated Ahmad ibn Ali al-Abbar from Kathir ibn Ubayd al-Himsi from Muhammad ibn Himyar from Sufyan al-Thawri from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Aisha, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would enter upon me while I was playing with girls."
"20877","277 - Narrated Muhammad ibn Muadh al-Halabi from Abdullah ibn Maslama al-Qanabi from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Aisha, that she used to play with girls, meaning dolls, with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). My companions would come and shrink from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would send them to play with me."
"20878","278 - Narrated Bishr ibn Musa from al-Humaydi from Sufyan from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Aisha, saying: I used to play with these girls; slave-girls would come to me and play with me. When they saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), they would withdraw. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would send them to me."
"20879","279 - Narrated Ubayd ibn Ghannam from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba from Abdullah ibn Numayr from Hisham ibn Urwa from his father from Aisha, saying: I used to play with girls in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"20880","280 - Narrated Musa ibn Harun from Hajjaj ibn Yusuf the poet from Ahmad ibn Ishaq from Wuhayb ibn Khalid from Ubayd Allah ibn Umar from Yazid ibn Ruman from Urwa from Aisha, saying: I used to play with girls in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"20881","Chapter on the marriage of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Aishah while she was playing on a swing. 281 - Muhammad ibn Abdillah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Abu Kurayb narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Hisham narrated to us, from Hammad ibn Salamah, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married me while I was on the swing."
"20882","Chapter on the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) carrying Aishah so she could watch the play of the Abyssinians. 282 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ad-Dabari narrated to us, Abdur-Razzaq narrated to us, Mamar informed us, from az-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Aishah, she said: By Allah, I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) standing at the door of my room while the Abyssinians were playing with spears in the mosque, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was covering me with his cloak so I could watch their play from between his ear and his shoulder, then he kept standing for my sake until I was the one who turned away. So estimate the measure of a young girl of tender age, eager for amusement."
"20883","283 - Ibrahim ibn Nailah al-Asbahani narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr al-Muqaddami narrated to us, Wahb ibn Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to us, my father narrated to us, he said: I heard an-Numan ibn Rashid narrating from az-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Aishah, she said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) standing at the door of my room while the Abyssinians were playing with their spears in the mosque, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was covering me with his cloak so I could watch their play, and he kept standing for my sake until I was the one who turned away. So estimate, O son of my sister, the measure of a young girl of tender age, eager for amusement."
"20884","284 - al-Husayn ibn Ishaq at-Tustari narrated to us, Yahya al-Himmani narrated to us, Abdur-Rahman ibn Abi az-Zinad narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, she said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) placed my chin upon his shoulder so I could watch the shooting of the Abyssinians, until I was the one who turned away from them."
"20885","Chapter on the entry of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upon Aishah while with her was a singing girl singing. 285 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ad-Dabari narrated to us, from Abdur-Razzaq, from Mamar, from az-Zuhri, and Hisham ibn Urwah, from Urwah, he said: Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, entered upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while with Aishah two singing girls were singing during the days of Mina, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was lying down with his garment drawn over his face, so Abu Bakr said: Is this to be done in the presence of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) uncovered his face, then said: Leave them, O Abu Bakr, for these are days of Eid."
"20886","286 - Ali ibn Abdil-Aziz narrated to us, Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, she said: Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, entered upon me on a day of Eid while with me were two slave-girls singing, mentioning the Day of Buath, the day on which the chiefs of al-Aws and al-Khazraj were killed, so Abu Bakr said: Servants of Allah, is the pipe of Satan in the presence of the Messenger of Allah? So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Abu Bakr, every people has an Eid, and today is our Eid."
"20887","287 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, that Abu Bakr entered upon her while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was with her on a day of Fitr or Adha, and with her were two singing girls singing, so Abu Bakr said: The pipe of Satan? So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Leave us, O Abu Bakr, every people has an Eid, and our Eid is this day."
"20888","288 - al-Husayn ibn Ishaq at-Tustari narrated to us, Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, that with her were two slave-girls singing on a day of Eid, and with her was the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) not forbidding them, so Abu Bakr entered and rebuked them, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Leave her, O Abu Bakr, for every people has an Eid, and this is our Eid."
"20889","Chapter. 289 - Muhammad ibn Ali as-Saigh narrated to us, Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, (Ha). And Bakr ibn Sahl narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, they said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Muslim ibn Subayh, from Masruq, that when he narrated from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, he would say: The acquitted, the truthful daughter of the truthful, the beloved of the Beloved of Allah, narrated to me."
"20890","290 - Muhammad ibn Ali as-Saigh al-Makki narrated to us, Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, Mahdi ibn Maymun narrated to us, from Shuayb ibn al-Habhab, from Amir ash-Shabi, he said: Masruq, when he narrated, would say: The truthful daughter of the truthful, the innocent, the acquitted, narrated to me such and such."
"20891","291 - Bakr ibn Sahl narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Muslim ibn Subayh, from Masruq, that it was said to him: Did Aishah know the laws of inheritance well? So he said: By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, I saw the elders among the Companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asking her about the obligatory shares."
"20892","292 - Muhammad ibn an-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zaidah narrated to us, from Abdil-Malik ibn Umayr, from Musa ibn Talhah, he said: I never saw anyone more eloquent than Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her."
"20893","293 - Muhammad ibn an-Nadr al-Azdi narrated to us, Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Zaidah narrated to us, from Bayan, from Amir ash-Shabi, he said: A man said: All the Mothers of the Believers are dearer to me than Aishah, so I said: As for you, you have opposed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); she was the dearest of them to him."
"20894","294 - Bakr ibn Sahl narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, he said: I never saw a woman more knowledgeable in medicine, nor in expenditure, nor in poetry, than Aishah."
"20895","295 - Ubayd al-Ijl narrated to us, Nuh ibn Habib al-Qumasi narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Muawiyah az-Zubayri narrated to us, Hisham ibn Urwah narrated to us, from his father, he said: I said to Aishah: O Mother of the Believers, I do not wonder at your insight into poetry, I say: the wife of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the daughter of the most learned of people, but I wonder at your insight into medicine, so she said: O son of my sister, when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) advanced in age he fell ill, so delegations came and remedies were described for him, and it is from that."
"20896","296 - Muhammad ibn Abdillah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Uqbah ibn Mukram ad-Dabbi narrated to us, Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Yahya ibn Abbad ibn Abdillah ibn az-Zubayr, from Aishah, she said: We came as emigrants and we took a difficult mountain pass, so a strong unruly camel upon which I was bolted with me, and by Allah I do not forget the saying of my mother: O little bride, so I mounted upon its head, then I heard a speaker saying: By Allah I do not see its halter cast off, so I cast it off, then it stood turning around as though a man standing beneath it were holding it."
"20897","297 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Salim al-Qazzaz narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Yusuf ibn Abi Ishaq narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Bara ibn Azib, he said: I entered with Abu Bakr at the very beginning when he came to Madinah, and there was Aishah lying down seized by fever, so Abu Bakr came to her and said: How are you, O my daughter? And he kissed her cheek."
"20898","298 - Abdullah ibn Ali ibn al-Jarud an-Naysaburi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Yahya an-Naysaburi narrated to us, Yaqub ibn Ibrahim ibn Sad narrated to us, my father narrated to me, from Salih ibn Kaysan, from az-Zuhri, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, he said: Muawiyah said: By Allah, I never saw any orator more eloquent nor more sagacious than Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her."
"20899","299 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, he said: I found in the book of my father, Ibrahim ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Rabah ibn Zayd narrated to me, Mamar said, from az-Zuhri, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If the knowledge of the women of this ummah, including the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), were gathered, the knowledge of Aishah would be more than their knowledge."
"20900","Chapter on the sermon of Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her. 300 - Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Imran al-Asbahani narrated to us, Musa ibn Abdir-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, Ali ibn Ahmad as-Sadusi narrated to us, from his father, he said: It reached Aishah that some people were disparaging Abu Bakr, so she sent for a group of them, and let down her curtains, and she reproached and rebuked, and said: My father, and what of my father; my father, hands cannot reach him. How far! By Allah, that was a lofty peak and an extended shade. By Allah, he succeeded when you denied, and he led when you lagged; the steed led when it took the lead to the goal, a youth of Quraysh in his youth and a refuge in his old age, freeing its captive, enriching its poor, mending its breach, and gathering its disarray, until hearts adorned him with love. Then he entrenched himself in his religion, and his firmness for the sake of Allah did not cease, until he took in his courtyard a mosque in which he revived what the nullifiers had killed. And he, may the mercy of Allah be upon him, was copious in tears, restrained in his inner feelings, choked with sobbing. So the women and children of Makkah gathered toward him, mocking him and ridiculing him, while Allah mocks them and extends them in their transgression wandering blindly. Then the men of Quraysh reproached that much, so they bent their bows and fixed their arrows, and they took him as a target, but they did not split for him any rock, nor did they break for him any spear, and he passed on upon his firm course, until religion struck with its spears, and laid down its burden, and its pegs were fixed, and people entered into it in crowds, and from every faction in companies and scattered groups. Then Allah chose for His Prophet what is with Him. When Allah, Mighty and Majestic, took him, Satan pitched his pavilion, and set up his snares, and stretched his ropes, and assailed with his cavalry and infantry. So the rope of Islam shook, and its covenant became confused, and its people surged, and its firmly twisted cord returned undone, and calamities sought opportunity, and men thought their greedy hopes had come true, but far from it, the time when they return, while as-Siddiq was among them. So he stood bared and girded, so he raised his hem, and gathered his scattered part, so he restored the spread of Islam to its freshness, and gathered its disarray by folding it, and straightened its crookedness by his inspiration. So hypocrisy shrank at his tread, and religion was lifted by his lifting. When he restored the truth to its people, and settled heads upon their shoulders, and spared blood in its vessels, his death came. So he closed its breach with his brother in mercy and his counterpart in conduct and justice, that is Ibn al-Khattab. For Allah is a mother who bore him, and poured forth upon him. He stood alone against the ugliness of disbelief and its excess, and he scattered polytheism far and wide, and he grazed the land and made it kneel, so it yielded its produce and cast out its filth at his head, and he gave charity from it and confronted it, and disdained it. Then he was Godfearing in it, then he left it as he had accompanied it. So show me what you say? And which of the two days of my father do you resent, the day of his staying when he was just among you, or the day of his departure when he looked out for you. I say this saying of mine and I seek forgiveness from Allah for me and for you."
"20901","Hafsah bint Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with her. From her reports. 301 - Ali ibn Abdil-Aziz narrated to us, az-Zubayr ibn Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Umar fathered Abdullah ibn Umar, and his full sister Hafsah bint Umar, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Abdur-Rahman the elder, and their mother was Zaynab bint Mazun ibn Habib ibn Wahb ibn Hudhafah ibn Jumah, she was among the emigrant women, and she was before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with Khunays ibn Hudhafah as-Sahmi, and her father witnessed Badr, and her uncle Zayd, and her maternal uncles Uthman, and Qudamah, and Abdullah son of her maternal aunt, and as-Saib ibn Uthman witnessed Badr."
"20902","302 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Abdur-Razzaq narrated to us, Mamar informed us, (Ha). And Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, from Sufyan ibn Husayn, (Ha). And Ubaydullah al-Umari narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Muhammad as-Salimi narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Uways al-Asha narrated to us, from Sulayman ibn Bilal, from Muhammad ibn Abi Atiq, and Musa ibn Uqbah, (Ha). And Ahmad ibn Tahir ibn Harmalah ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: my grandfather Idris ibn Yahya al-Khawlani narrated to us, from Haywah ibn Shurayh, from Uqayl, (Ha). And Ali ibn Said ar-Razi narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Ali al-Hulwani narrated to us, Yaqub ibn Ibrahim ibn Sad narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from Salih ibn Kaysan, (Ha). And al-Abbas ibn Muhammad al-Mujashii narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi Yaqub al-Kirmani narrated to us, Hassan ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Yunus ibn Yazid, all of them, from az-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, from Umar, he said: Hafsah became widowed from Khunays ibn Hudhafah as-Sahmi, and he was among the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he witnessed Badr, then he died in Madinah, so I met Abu Bakr and I offered her to him but he remained silent toward me, so there entered into me from that what entered, then when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married her, I said to him: What carried you to what you did? He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had mentioned her, so for that reason I remained silent. And the wording is for the hadith of Musa ibn Uqbah and Ibn Abi Atiq."
"20903","303 - Abu Amir as-Suri Muhammad ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Abdir-Rahman narrated to us, Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that a slave-girl of Hafsah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), bewitched her, then she confessed it against herself, so Hafsah ordered Abdur-Rahman ibn Zayd, so he killed her, so Uthman disapproved of that against her, so Abdullah came to him and said: She bewitched her and she confessed it, and Uthman had disapproved against her what she did without the authority."
"20904","304 - Amr ibn Abi at-Tahir ibn as-Sarh al-Misri narrated to us, Yusuf ibn Adiyy narrated to us, Yahya ibn Abi Zakariyya ibn Abi Zaidah narrated to us, from Salih ibn Salih, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, from Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) divorced Hafsah, then he took her back."
"20905","305 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn Numayr narrated to me, Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us, al-Amash narrated to us, from Abu Salih, from Ibn Umar, he said: Umar entered upon Hafsah while she was weeping, so he said: What makes you weep? Perhaps the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) divorced you. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) divorced you and took you back for my sake, and by Allah if he divorces you I shall never speak a word to you ever."
"20906","306 - Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, al-Mundhir ibn al-Walid al-Jarudi narrated to us, my father narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Abi Jafar narrated to us, from Asim, from Zirr, from Ammar ibn Yasir, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended to divorce Hafsah, so Jibril came and said: Do not divorce her, for she is fasting much, standing much in prayer, and she is your wife in Paradise."
"20907","307 - Ahmad ibn Tahir ibn Harmalah ibn Yahya narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Amr ibn Salih al-Hadrami narrated to me, from Musa ibn Ulayy ibn Rabah, from his father, from Uqbah ibn Amir al-Juhani, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) divorced Hafsah, so that reached Umar ibn al-Khattab, so he put dust upon his head, and said: Allah pays no regard to you, O son of al-Khattab, after her. So Jibril descended upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: Allah commands you to take back Hafsah, as a mercy for Umar."
"20908","308 - Abu Zurah Abdur-Rahman ibn Amr narrated to us, he said: al-Harith ibn Miskin narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, from Malik ibn Anas, he said: Hafsah died the year Ifriqiyyah was conquered, and she died while Marwan was over Madinah."
"20909","309 - Abu Zurah Abdur-Rahman ibn Amr narrated to us, al-Harith ibn Miskin narrated to us, from Ibn Wahb, from Ibn Lahiah, he said: Yazid ibn Abi Habib said: Muawiyah ibn Hudayj raided Ifriqiyyah three times, the first in the year thirty-four, and the second in the year forty, and the third in the year fifty."
"20910","310 - Abu Usamah Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Usamah al-Halabi narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Mani ar-Rusafi narrated to us, my grandfather Ubaydullah ibn Ziyad narrated to us, (Ha). And Abu Zurah Abdur-Rahman ibn Amr narrated to us, Abul-Yaman al-Hakam ibn Nafi narrated to us, Shuayb ibn Abi Hamzah narrated to us, both of them, from az-Zuhri, from Salim, that Marwan used to send to Hafsah asking her for the codex from which the Quran was copied, but Hafsah would refuse to give it to him, then when we buried Hafsah, Marwan sent to Ibn Umar: Send to me that codex, so he sent it to him."
"20911","What Hafsah, Mother of the Believers, transmitted. What Abdullah ibn Umar narrated from Hafsah. 311 - Ahmad ibn Yahya ibn Khalid ibn Hayyan ar-Raqqi narrated to us, Abu Ubaydah ibn Fudayl ibn Iyad narrated to us, Malik ibn Said ibn al-Khamis narrated to us, Ubaydullah ibn Umar narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Hafsah, she said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, what is the matter with people, they have exited ihram while you have not exited from your Umrah? He said: I have matted my head, and I have garlanded my sacrificial animal, so I shall not exit ihram until I slaughter."
"20912","312 - Ibrahim ibn Salih ash-Shirazi narrated to us, Uthman ibn al-Haytham al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, (Ha). And Ahmad ibn Amr al-Khallal al-Makki narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi Umar al-Adani narrated to us, Abdil-Majid ibn Abdil-Aziz ibn Abi Rawwad narrated to us, and Hisham ibn Sulayman, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: Hafsah narrated to me, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded his wives to exit ihram in the year of the Farewell Hajj, Hafsah said: What prevents you from exiting ihram? He said: I have matted my head and garlanded my sacrificial animal, so I shall not exit ihram until I slaughter my sacrificial animal."
"20913","313 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, my father narrated to us, (Ha). And Abuz-Zinba narrated to us, Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, from Amr ibn al-Harith, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Hafsah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded his wives to exit ihram in the year of the Farewell Hajj, so Hafsah said: What prevented you from exiting ihram? So he said: I have matted my head, and garlanded my sacrificial animal, so I shall not exit ihram until I slaughter my sacrificial animal."
"20914","314 - al-Qasim ibn Abbad al-Khattabi narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Buhlul al-Anbari narrated to us, Ibn Abi Fudayk narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Nafi, from his father, from Ibn Umar, from Hafsah bint Umar, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded his women in the year of the Farewell Hajj to exit ihram, Hafsah said: So what prevents you, O Messenger of Allah, from exiting ihram? He said: I have matted my head and garlanded my sacrificial animal, so I shall not exit ihram until I exit from my Hajj."
"20915","315 - Ali ibn Abdil-Aziz narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Ayyub, author of al-Maghazi, narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Sad narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, he said: Nafi narrated to me, from Abdullah ibn Umar, from Hafsah bint Umar, she said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded his women to exit ihram with an Umrah, we said: So what prevents you, O Messenger of Allah, from exiting ihram with us? He said: I have offered a sacrificial animal, and matted my head, so I shall not exit ihram until I slaughter."
"20916","316 - Ishaq ibn Dawud as-Sawwaf at-Tustari narrated to us, Abul-Ashath Ahmad ibn al-Miqdam narrated to us, Fudayl ibn Sulayman an-Numayri narrated to us, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that Hafsah informed him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded his wives to exit ihram in the year of the Farewell Hajj, Hafsah said: So what prevents you, O Messenger of Allah, from exiting ihram? He said: I have garlanded my sacrificial animal, and matted my head, so I shall not exit ihram until I slaughter the sacrificial animal."
"20917","317 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ad-Dabari narrated to us, Abdur-Razzaq narrated to us, Mamar informed us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: Hafsah narrated to me, that he used to pray two rakahs before Fajr, meaning the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"20918","318 - Ahmad ibn Amr al-Khallal al-Makki narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi Umar al-Adani narrated to us, Abdil-Wahhab ath-Thaqafi narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Hafsah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would pray after the dawn had risen and the caller called to prayer, two light rakahs."
"20919","319 - Ali ibn Abdil-Aziz narrated to us, al-Qanabi narrated to us, from Malik, (Ha). And Bakr ibn Sahl narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, Malik informed us, (Ha). And Ali ibn al-Mubarak as-Sanani narrated to us, Ismail ibn Abi Uways narrated to us, Malik narrated to me, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Hafsah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when the muadhdhin fell silent from the adhan, would pray two light rakahs before the prayer was established."
"20920","320 - Muttalib ibn Shuayb al-Azdi narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to me, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Hafsah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that when the call was made for the Fajr prayer he would bow two light rakahs before he stood for the prayer."
"20921","321 - Abu Yazid al-Qaratisiyy narrated to us, Ali ibn Mabad ar-Raqqi narrated to us, Ubaydullah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Abdil-Karim ibn Malik, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Hafsah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when the muadhdhin called at Fajr, would stand and pray the two rakahs of Fajr, then go out to the mosque, and food becomes forbidden, and the call was not made until morning."
"20922","322 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ad-Dabari narrated to us, from Abdur-Razzaq, from Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Hafsah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when dawn rose would pray two light rakahs."
"20923","323 - Yahya ibn Ayyub al-Allaf narrated to us, Yusuf ibn Adiyy narrated to us, Abdir-Rahim ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: Hafsah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), informed me that he, meaning the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), used to pray two rakahs before the morning, lightening them."
"20924","324 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Abu Usamah narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: Hafsah informed me that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would perform two light prostrations when dawn rose."
"20925","325 - Muhammad ibn Abdillah al-Hadrami narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Amir ibn Zurarah narrated to us, Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Zaidah narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Hafsah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would pray after the rising of dawn two light rakahs."
"20926","326 - Amr ibn Abi at-Tahir ibn as-Sarh narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, Umar ibn Muhammad ibn Zayd narrated to me, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: Hafsah narrated to me that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when dawn rose would pray two light rakahs."
"20927","327 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abbad al-Makki narrated to me, Hatim ibn Ismail narrated to us, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Hafsah, she said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when dawn rose would pray two light rakahs."
"20928","328 - Abuz-Zinba Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Abu Zayd ibn Abi al-Umar narrated to us, Mufaddal ibn Fudalah narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Sulayman at-Tawil, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Hafsah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would pray two light prostrations when dawn became clear, before the prayer."
"20929","329 - Abuz-Zinba narrated to us, Yusuf ibn Adiyy narrated to us, al-Qasim ibn Malik al-Madani narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Hafsah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed the two rakahs of Fajr in my house, and he would lighten them."
"20930","330 - Musa ibn Harun narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Ismail narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Hafsah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would pray two rakahs when dawn rose."
"20931","331 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ad-Dabari narrated to us, from Abdur-Razzaq, from Mamar, from az-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, he said: Hafsah informed me that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would pray two rakahs when dawn rose."
"20932","332 - Ahmad ibn Amr al-Khallal narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi Umar al-Adani narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from az-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, he said: Hafsah informed me that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would pray the two rakahs of Fajr when dawn rose."
"20933","333 - Masadah ibn Sad al-Attar al-Makki narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir al-Hizami narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, Yunus informed me, from az-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, from Hafsah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Five, there is no sin upon whoever kills them in ihram: the crow, the mouse, the vicious dog, and the kite."
"20934","334 - Abuz-Zinba Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, Yahya ibn Bukayr narrated to us, Mufaddal ibn Fudalah narrated to me, from Ayyash ibn Abbas al-Qitbani, from Bukayr ibn Abdillah al-Ashajj, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Hafsah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Going early on Friday is obligatory upon every adult, and the bath is like the bath from janabah."
"20935","335 - al-Husayn ibn Ishaq at-Tustari narrated to us, Yahya al-Himmani narrated to us, Muawiyah narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Hafsah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is not lawful for a woman who believes in Allah and the Last Day to mourn over a dead person more than three days, except over a husband."
"20936","336 - al-Husayn ibn Ishaq at-Tustari narrated to us, Yahya al-Himmani narrated to us, Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Hafsah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The emergence of Ibn Sayyad is only for an anger he becomes angry with."
"20937","337 - Abu Yazid al-Qaratisiyy narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Abdil-Hakam narrated to us, al-Layth ibn Sad informed us, and Yahya ibn Ayyub, from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim, from his father, from Hafsah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Whoever does not resolve the fast before dawn, there is no fast for him."
"20938","Al-Muttalib ibn Abi Wadaah as-Sahmi from Hafsah. 338 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ad-Dabari narrated to us, from Abdur-Razzaq, from Mamar, from az-Zuhri, from as-Saib ibn Yazid, from al-Muttalib ibn Abi Wadaah as-Sahmi, from Hafsah, she said: I never saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seated until a year or two before his death, then he would pray his supererogatory prayer seated, and he would recite the surah slowly until his recitation would be longer than longer than it."
"20939","339 - Ali ibn Abdil-Aziz narrated to us, Mualla ibn Asad al-Ammi narrated to us, Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Malik ibn Anas, from az-Zuhri, from as-Saib ibn Yazid, (Ha). And Abu Khalifah narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Asma narrated to us, Juwayriyah ibn Asma narrated to us, from Malik ibn Anas, from az-Zuhri, from as-Saib ibn Yazid, from al-Muttalib ibn Abi Wadaah as-Sahmi, from Hafsah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that she informed him, she said: I never saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) praying seated ever until a year before his death, then he would pray his supererogatory prayer seated, and recite the surah, prolonging it until it would be longer than longer than it."
"20940","340 - Salamah ibn Ahmad al-Fawzi al-Himsi narrated to us, my grandfather Khattab ibn Uthman narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Himyar narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Abi Ablah, from az-Zuhri, from as-Saib ibn Yazid, from al-Muttalib ibn Abi Wadaah as-Sahmi, from Hafsah, she said: I never saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seated in his supererogatory prayer until a year or two before his death, then I saw him praying seated in his supererogatory prayer, and prolonging the surah until his recitation of it would be longer than longer than it."
"20941","341 - Masadah ibn Sad al-Attar al-Makki narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir al-Hizami narrated to us, Umar ibn Musa at-Taymi narrated to us, from his father, from Ibn Shihab, he said: as-Saib ibn Yazid informed me, from al-Muttalib ibn Abi Wadaah, from Hafsah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), she said: I never saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seated in his supererogatory prayer until a year or two before he passed away, then I saw him praying seated in his supererogatory prayer, prolonging the surah until his recitation would be longer than longer than it."
"20942","342 - Abdan ibn Ahmad narrated to us, Shayban ibn Farrukh narrated to us, Yazid ibn Iyad narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from as-Saib ibn Yazid, from al-Muttalib ibn Abi Wadaah, from Hafsah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), she said: I never saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) praying seated in his supererogatory prayer until a year or two before he passed away, then I saw him praying seated in his supererogatory prayer, and prolonging the surah until his recitation of it would be longer than his recitation standing."
"20943","343 - Ismail ibn al-Hasan al-Khaffaf al-Misri narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: as-Saib ibn Yazid informed me, from al-Muttalib ibn Abi Wadaah as-Sahmi, that Hafsah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), said: I never saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) praying in his supererogatory prayer while seated until a year or two before Allah took him, then I saw him praying in his supererogatory prayer while seated, prolonging the surah until it would be longer than longer than it."
"20944","344 - Bakr ibn Sahl narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, al-Haytham ibn Jamil narrated to us, Abu Mabad Hafs ibn Ghaylan narrated to me, from az-Zuhri, from as-Saib ibn Yazid, from al-Muttalib ibn Abi Wadaah, from Hafsah, then he mentioned the like of it."
"20945","Abdullah ibn Safwan ibn Umayyah al-Jumahi from Hafsah. 345 - Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us, al-Humaydi narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, Umayyah ibn Safwan ibn Abdullah ibn Safwan al-Jumahi narrated to us, he said: I heard my grandfather Abdullah ibn Safwan, in the rule of Ibn az-Zubayr, saying: I heard Hafsah saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: There shall come to this House a party raiding it, until when they are at a desert land, its middle ones shall be swallowed, so its first shall call its last, then none of them shall remain except the fugitive who informs of them. So a man said to my grandfather: So I bear witness that you did not lie against Hafsah, and Hafsah did not lie against the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Sufyan said: And Umayr ibn Qays used to narrate it from Umayyah."
"20946","Jariyah ibn Wahb al-Khuzai from Hafsah. 346 - Abdur-Rahman ibn Salim ar-Razi narrated to us, and Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn al-Abbas al-Asbahani, they said: Sahl ibn Uthman narrated to us, Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Zaidah narrated to us, Abu Ayyub narrated to me, from Asim, from al-Musayyab ibn Rafi, and Mabad ibn Harith, from Jariyah ibn Wahb al-Khuzai, he said: Hafsah narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would make his right for his food and his drink and his clothes, and would make his left for what is besides that."
"20947","347 - al-Husayn ibn Ishaq at-Tustari narrated to us, Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, al-Husayn ibn Ali al-Jufi narrated to us, from Zaidah, from Asim ibn Abi an-Najud, from al-Musayyab ibn Rafi, from Hafsah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he took his bed, would place his right hand under his right cheek, and his right was for his food and his drink and his purification and his water, and his left was for what is besides that."
"20948","Shutayr ibn Shakl al-Absi from Hafsah. 348 - Ali ibn Abdil-Aziz narrated to us, Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Mansur, from Abid-Duha, from Masruq, from Shutayr ibn Shakl, from Hafsah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), she said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would kiss while fasting."
"20949","349 - Uthman ibn Umar ad-Dabbi narrated to us, Amr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Shubah informed us, from Mansur, from Abid-Duha, from Shutayr ibn Shakl, from Hafsah, she said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would kiss while fasting."
"20950","350 - Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us, al-Humaydi narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, Mansur narrated to us, from Muslim ibn Subayh, from Shutayr ibn Shakl, from Hafsah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would take from the face of some of his wives while fasting."
"20951","351 - al-Husayn ibn Ishaq at-Tustari narrated to us, Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, and Jarir, from Mansur, from Muslim ibn Subayh, from Shutayr ibn Shakl, from Hafsah, she said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would kiss some of his wives while fasting."
"20952","Sawa al-Khuzai from Hafsah. 352 - Ali ibn Abdil-Aziz narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, Asim ibn Bahdalah narrated to us, from Sawa al-Khuzai, from Hafsah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would fast three days of the month: Monday and Thursday, and Monday of the other Friday."
"20953","353 - al-Husayn ibn Ishaq at-Tustari narrated to us, Yahya al-Himmani narrated to us, Qays ibn ar-Rabi narrated to us, from Asim, from al-Musayyab ibn Rafi, from Sawa al-Khuzai, from Hafsah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would fast Monday and Thursday, and Monday of the coming Friday."
"20954","Hunaydah ibn Khalid al-Khuzai from Hafsah. 354 - Mahmud ibn Muhammad al-Wasiti narrated to us, Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Hashim ibn al-Qasim Abu an-Nadr narrated to us, Abu Ishaq al-Ashjai narrated to us, from Amr ibn Qays al-Mulai, from al-Hurr ibn as-Sabbah, from Hunaydah ibn Khalid, from Hafsah, she said: Four the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not leave: fasting Ashura, and the ten, and three days of every month, and the two rakahs before dawn."
"20955","Abdullah ibn Abi Said from Hafsah. 355 - Abdullah ibn al-Husayn al-Missisi narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Musa al-Ashyab narrated to us, Shayban narrated to us, from Abu Yafur, from Abdullah ibn Abi Said, from Hafsah bint Umar, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon me one day and placed his garment between his thighs, then Abu Bakr came asking permission, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted him in his state, then Umar came asking permission, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted him in his state, then people came and he permitted them, then Ali came and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted him in his state, until Uthman came and asked permission, so he wrapped himself with his garment then permitted him, then they talked for a while then they went out, so I said: O Messenger of Allah, Abu Bakr and Umar and Ali entered upon you, and some of your Companions, while you were in your state, not moving, then when Uthman entered you wrapped yourself with your garment, so he said: Shall I not feel shy from one from whom the angels feel shy?"
"20956","Abdullah ibn Safwan from Hafsah. 356 - Muhammad ibn Musa ibn Hammad al-Barbari narrated to us, Abdur-Rahman ibn Salih al-Azdi narrated to us, Abdullah ibn al-Ajlah narrated to us, from Ammar ad-Duhni, from Salim ibn Abi al-Jad, from Abdullah ibn Safwan, he said: I heard Hafsah, Mother of the Believers, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: People of my ummah shall raid this House, until when they are at al-Bayda, its first shall be swallowed and its middle shall not return or escape."
"20957","Abu Mijlaz Lahiq ibn Humayd from Hafsah. 357 - Ali ibn Abdil-Aziz narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, Anas ibn Sirin informed, from Abu Mijlaz, from Hafsah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that Utarid ibn Hajib came with a garment Kisra had clothed him with, so Umar ibn al-Khattab said: Shall you not buy it, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Only one who has no share buys it."
"20958","And among women who narrated from Hafsah, of those having companionship, Umm Mubashshir al-Ansariyyah. 358 - al-Husayn ibn Ishaq at-Tustari narrated to us, Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abu Sufyan, from Jabir, from Umm Mubashshir, from Hafsah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I hope that none shall enter the Fire, if Allah wills, of those who witnessed Badr and al-Hudaybiyyah. I said: O Messenger of Allah, has not Allah said: There is none of you except he shall come to it, it was upon your Lord decreed, determined. He said: Did you not hear Him saying: Then We shall save those who feared, and leave the wrongdoers in it kneeling?"
"20959","Safiyyah bint Abi Ubayd from Hafsah. 359 - Musa ibn Harun narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Asma narrated to us, Juwayriyah ibn Asma narrated to us, from Nafi, from Safiyyah bint Abi Ubayd, from Hafsah, or from Aishah, or both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is not lawful for a woman who believes in Allah and His Messenger to mourn over a dead person more than three days, except over her husband."
"20960","360 - al-Husayn ibn Ishaq at-Tustari narrated to us, Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Abdah ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Safiyyah bint Abi Ubayd, from Hafsah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is not lawful for a woman who believes in Allah and the Last Day, or believes in Allah and His Messenger, to mourn over a dead person more than three days, except over her husband."
"20961","361 - Abu Khalifah narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Abdil-Wahhab al-Hajabi narrated to us, Abdil-Wahhab ath-Thaqafi narrated to us, he said: I heard Yahya ibn Said narrating, (Ha). And Yusuf al-Qadi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr al-Muqaddami narrated to us, Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, from Nafi, from Safiyyah bint Abi Ubayd, from Hafsah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: It is not lawful for a woman who believes in Allah and His Messenger to mourn more than three days, except over a husband."
"20962","362 - Abu Khalifah narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Abdil-Wahhab al-Hajabi narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, and Abdil-Wahhab ath-Thaqafi, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Safiyyah bint Abi Ubayd, from one of the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is not lawful for a woman, then he mentioned the like of it."
"20963","The additions in the hadith of Hafsah, may Allah be pleased with her. 363 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abu Sufyan, from Jabir, from Umm Mubashshir, from Hafsah, she said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I hope that none shall enter the Fire, if Allah wills, of those who witnessed Badr and al-Hudaybiyyah. I said: O Messenger of Allah: There is none of you except he shall come to it, it was upon your Lord decreed, determined. He said: Did you not hear Him saying: Then We shall save those who feared, and leave the wrongdoers in it kneeling?"
"20964","364 - Ahmad ibn Amr al-Khallal narrated to us, Yaqub ibn Humayd narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Musa at-Taymi narrated to us, from Usamah ibn Zayd, from Ibn Shihab, from Abdullah ibn Abdillah ibn Abi Thawr, from Ibn Abbas, that Umar informed him, that he said to Hafsah: Does one of you become angry with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for the day until the night? She said: Yes."
"20965","365 - Ahmad ibn Amr al-Khallal narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abi Umar al-Adani narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, he said: Hafsah informed me that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would pray the two rakahs of Fajr when dawn brightened."
"20966","366 - Jafar ibn Sulayman an-Nawfali narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir al-Hizami narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, from Hafsah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Five, there is no sin upon whoever kills them in ihram: the crow, the mouse, the scorpion, the vicious dog, and the kite."
"20967","367 - Bakr ibn Sahl narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr narrated to us, from az-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, from Hafsah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever does not resolve the fast before dawn, there is no fast for him."
"20968","368 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Khalid ibn Makhlad narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Hazim narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr narrated to me, from Salim, from his father, from Hafsah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"20969","369 - Muhammad ibn Harun ibn Muhammad ibn Bakkar narrated to us, al-Abbas ibn al-Walid al-Khallal narrated to us, Marwan ibn Muhammad narrated to us, Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, from Uqayl, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, he said: Hafsah informed me that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Let none of you leave seeking a child, for when a man dies and he has no child, his name is cut off."
"20970","370 - Muhammad ibn Hisham al-Mustamli narrated to us, Ali ibn al-Madini narrated to us, Yaqub ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from Salih ibn Kaysan, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Salim ibn Abdillah ibn Umar informed me, that Abdullah ibn Umar said: Hafsah said: I loved this man, for we used to say that the Dajjal emerges from an anger he becomes angry with. And Mamar, from az-Zuhri, the like of it."
"20971","371 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Abu Usamah narrated to us, Ubaydullah ibn Umar narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: Hafsah informed me that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would perform two light prostrations when dawn rose."
"20972","372 - Ubayd narrated to us, Abu Bakr narrated to us, Abu Usamah narrated to us, Ubaydullah ibn Umar narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that Hafsah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), said: O Messenger of Allah, why have you not exited ihram, the hadith."
"20973","373 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr narrated to us, from Hafs ibn Ghiyath, from Ubaydullah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Hafsah, she said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Ibn Said only emerges for an anger he becomes angry with."
"20974","374 - Ahmad ibn al-Mualla ad-Dimashqi narrated to us, Hisham ibn Ammar narrated to us, Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Hafsah, she said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, what is the matter with people, they have exited ihram while you have not exited from your Umrah? He said: I have matted my head, and garlanded my sacrificial animal, so I shall not exit ihram until I slaughter it at Mina."
"20975","375 - Ahmad ibn Amr al-Khallal narrated to us, and Muhammad ibn Ali as-Saigh, they said: Yaqub ibn Humayd narrated to us, Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, and Ayyub as-Sakhtiyani, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Hafsah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would pray the two rakahs of Fajr when dawn brightened."
"20976","376 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Yala ibn Ubayd narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Hafsah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would lighten the two rakahs just before Fajr."
"20977","377 - Musab ibn Ibrahim ibn Hamzah narrated to us, my father narrated to us, Ibn Abi Hazim narrated to us, from Umar ibn Muhammad, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Hafsah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he saw dawn, would pray two light rakahs."
"20978","378 - Musab ibn Ibrahim ibn Hamzah narrated to us, my father narrated to us, Ibn Abi Hazim narrated to us, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Hafsah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when the call was made for the Fajr prayer, would prostrate two prostrations before the Fajr prayer, lightening them."
"20979","379 - al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Asfati narrated to us, Ismail ibn Abi Uways narrated to us, Malik narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Hafsah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when the muadhdhin fell silent from the adhan for the Fajr prayer and after Fajr, would pray two light rakahs before the prayer was established."
"20980","380 - Ali ibn Said ar-Razi narrated to us, Ali ibn Muhammad at-Tanafisi narrated to us, Waki narrated to us, from Jafar ibn Burqan, from Maymun ibn Mihran, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Hafsah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would pray two rakahs after Fajr."
"20981","381 - Ali ibn Said ar-Razi narrated to us, Ali ibn Muhammad narrated to us, Waki narrated to us, from al-Umari, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Hafsah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed the two rakahs of Fajr when dawn rose."
"20982","382 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, from Hisham, from Jafar ibn Burqan, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Hafsah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded me to exit ihram in his Hajj which he performed."
"20983","383 - Jafar ibn Sulayman an-Nawfali narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir al-Hizami narrated to us, Ibn Abi Fudayk narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Nafi, from his father, from Ibn Umar, from Hafsah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) matted his head when he performed Hajj, and shaved when he exited ihram."
"20984","384 - Muhammad ibn Muhammad ibn Uqbah ash-Shaybani narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Ali al-Hulwani narrated to us, Imran ibn Aban narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Muslim narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Umayyah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Hafsah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded his wives to exit ihram in the year of the Farewell Hajj."
"20985","385 - Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Sadaqah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Qatai narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Bakr al-Bursani narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Muhammad ibn Abdir-Rahman ibn Abdillah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Hafsah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when dawn rose, would not pray except two light rakahs."
"20986","386 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, al-Muqaddami narrated to us, Abu Mashar al-Bara narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Umar ibn Aban narrated to us, my father narrated to me, from Abdullah ibn Umar, from Hafsah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the hadith."
"20987","387 - Muhammad ibn Muhammad al-Judhui narrated to us, Uqbah ibn Mukram narrated to us, Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, from Shubah, from Tawbah al-Anbari, from ash-Shabi, he said: I sat with Ibn Umar for years, or a year and a half, I never heard him narrating from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) anything except that he narrated once from a wife of the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): That the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought a mastigure, so some of his Companions began eating it, and among them was Sad, then a wife of the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called to him that it is a mastigure, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Eat it, there is no harm in it, but it is not from the food of my people."
"20988","388 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Fudayl narrated to us, and Yazid ibn Harun, from Yahya ibn Said, from Nafi, from Safiyyah bint Abi Ubayd, that she heard Hafsah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), narrating that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is not lawful for a woman who believes in Allah and the Last Day to mourn over a dead person more than three days, except over a husband."
"20989","389 - Musab ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, my father narrated to us, Ibn Abi Hazim narrated to us, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Nafi, from Safiyyah, from Aishah, or Hafsah, or both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"20990","390 - Muhammad ibn Hisham al-Mustamli narrated to us, Ali ibn Abdillah al-Madini narrated to us, Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Ayyub ibn Musa, from Nafi, from Safiyyah bint Abi Ubayd, from one of the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), she said: I said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Why have people exited ihram while you have not exited? He said: I have matted my head, and garlanded my sacrificial animal, so I have no place of exiting except the place of my sacrificial animal."
"20991","391 - Ahmad ibn Amr al-Khallal narrated to us, Yaqub ibn Humayd narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Raja narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Safiyyah bint Abi Ubayd, from one of the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Whoever walks to a soothsayer, Allah shall not accept prayer from him for forty days. Mawquf."
"20992","392 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Umar ibn Khalid narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Nafi, from Safiyyah bint Abi Ubayd, from Umm Salamah, and Safiyyah, they said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The one who drinks in a vessel of silver gurgles in his belly the fire of Hell."
"20993","393 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Jarir narrated to us, from Mansur, and Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from al-Amash, both of them, from Muslim, from Shutayr ibn Shakl, from Hafsah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would kiss while fasting."
"20994","394 - Muhammad ibn Muhammad ibn Uqbah narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Ali al-Hulwani narrated to us, he said: Abdus-Samad narrated to us, Aban ibn Yazid narrated to us, Asim narrated to us, from Mabad ibn Khalid, from Sawa, from Hafsah bint Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he intended to sleep, would place his right hand under his right cheek, then he said: O Allah, shield me from Your punishment on the day You resurrect Your servants."
"20995","395 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn al-Hajjaj as-Sami narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, Anas ibn Sirin narrated to us, from Abu Mijlaz, from Hafsah, that Utarid ibn Hajib came with a garment of brocade Kisra had clothed him with, so Umar said: Shall I not buy it for you, O Messenger of Allah? So he said: Only one who has no share wears it."
"20996","396 - Ubayd ibn Ghannam narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Hashim ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, Abu Ishaq al-Ashjai narrated to us, from Amr ibn Qays, from al-Hurr ibn Sabbah, from Hunaydah ibn Khalid al-Khuzai, from Hafsah, she said: Four the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not leave: fasting Ashura, and the ten, and three days of every month."
"20997","397 - Ubayd narrated to us, Abu Bakr narrated to us, Abdir-Rahim ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from al-Hasan ibn Ubaydillah, from al-Hurr ibn Sabbah, from Hunaydah ibn Khalid al-Khuzai, from his wife, from Umm Salamah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Fast three days of every month, or of the month, Monday and Thursday, and the Thursday which follows it."
"20998","398 - Muhammad ibn Muhammad ibn Uqbah narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Ali al-Hulwani narrated to us, Abdus-Samad narrated to us, Aban ibn Yazid narrated to us, from Asim, from Mabad ibn Khalid, from Sawa, from Hafsah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would fast three days of every month, two, Monday and Thursday."
"20999","399 - Muhammad ibn Muhammad ibn Uqbah narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Ali narrated to us, Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Abu Bakr ibn Sulayman ibn Abi Hathmah, that a woman of Quraysh called ash-Shifa used to treat namlah, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: Teach it to Hafsah."
"21000","400 - Muhammad ibn Ali ibn al-Walid an-Narsi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, Abu Asim narrated to us, Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, Abu Khalid informed me, Abdullah ibn Abi Said al-Madani narrated to me, Hafsah narrated to me, she said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was placing his garment between his thighs, so Abu Bakr asked permission and he permitted him while in his state, then Umar asked permission and he permitted him while in his state, then Ali with some of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked permission and he permitted him while in his state, then Uthman asked permission so he wrapped himself with his garment, then they talked then they went out, so I said: O Messenger of Allah, Abu Bakr asked permission and you permitted him while upon your state, then Umar asked permission and you permitted him while upon your state, then Ali with some of your Companions asked permission and you permitted him while upon your state, then Uthman asked permission and you wrapped yourself with your garment, so he said: Shall I not feel shy from what the angels feel shy from? Ibn Jurayj said: And my father informed me with the like of it."
